"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["school_mathematics_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["school_mathematics_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["school_mathematics_en"][2]={"school_mathematics_en:M2-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M2-L1 – Families of functions in calculus<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M2 – Upper secondary education / Abitur<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Upper secondary education / Abitur<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> No specific prerequisites\nstated.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Classification:<\/strong> Upper secondary education at a\nGymnasium, calculus / foundations for the basic and advanced courses\n(Grundkurs and Leistungskurs) · Format: systematic overview with\nguidance on reading notation<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is this about?<\/h2>\n<p>In calculus we study not just individual numbers, but entire\n<strong>relationships between quantities<\/strong>. A function takes an\nadmissible input and assigns exactly one value to it. Different types of\nfunctions produce different patterns of change: straight-line, curved,\nexponentially growing or periodically oscillating patterns. If we\nrecognise the family of functions, we often already know which questions\nmake sense — for example, about growth, limits, gradients, extreme\nvalues or long-term behaviour. The next lessons take us from recognising\nthese shapes to <strong>limits and derivatives<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Basic idea and intuition<\/h2>\n<p>Imagine a computer program that supplies measurements as a function\nof time. A constant processing rate leads to a <strong>straight\nline<\/strong>; accelerating change may be approximated by a\n<strong>parabola<\/strong>; a fixed percentage increase per step leads to\n<strong>exponential growth<\/strong>; a regularly repeating signal may be\nrepresented by a <strong>sine function<\/strong>. Mathematically, the\nbasic idea is the same: each admissible input has exactly one output.\nThe different families of functions differ in <em>how<\/em> that value is\ncalculated.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>New symbols before the first formula<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Symbol<\/th>\n<th>Read aloud<\/th>\n<th>Meaning here<\/th>\n<th>Other possible uses<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>g<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘eff’, ‘gee’<\/td>\n<td>Names of functions<\/td>\n<td>May name other quantities<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘eff of x’<\/td>\n<td>Output of function f for input x<\/td>\n<td>Brackets may also express grouping<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘ex’<\/td>\n<td>Independent input quantity<\/td>\n<td>Also a coordinate or an unknown value<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>b<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>c<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘ay’, ‘bee’, ‘see’<\/td>\n<td>Fixed model parameters<\/td>\n<td>Their roles are not universally fixed<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘x squared’, ‘x cubed’<\/td>\n<td>Second and third powers respectively<\/td>\n<td>Power rules from <a href=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L7<\/a><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>2<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2^x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nor <code>2^x<\/code><\/td>\n<td>‘two to the power of x’<\/td>\n<td>Variable appears in the exponent<\/td>\n<td>In many programming languages <code>^<\/code> is <em>not<\/em> an\nexponentiation operator<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘sine of x’<\/td>\n<td>Periodic sine value<\/td>\n<td>From <a href=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L16&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L16<\/a> and <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L20&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L20<\/a>; angles in calculus are\nusually in radians<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ln(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘natural logarithm of x’<\/td>\n<td>Logarithm to base e<\/td>\n<td><code>log<\/code> is not defined identically in every subject or\nprogram<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>e<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘Euler’s number’<\/td>\n<td>Number approximately 2.71828<\/td>\n<td>The letter e may be a variable in another context<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>≠<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x\\ne2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘x is not equal to two’<\/td>\n<td>Excluded input value<\/td>\n<td>General inequality symbol<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℝ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘set of real numbers’<\/td>\n<td>Possible real-valued inputs<\/td>\n<td>The concept of a set is formalised later in university mathematics\n<a\nhref=\"?course=university_mathematics&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L1<\/a><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>→<\/code><\/td>\n<td>‘maps to’, ‘tends to’<\/td>\n<td>Different meanings for a function mapping and, later, a limit<\/td>\n<td>Always read the meaning from the context<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>A formula in this lesson is also shown in <strong>simple plain-text\nnotation that can be read directly offline<\/strong>. This keeps the\nexpressions readable even if a local Markdown viewer does not support\nLaTeX.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Polynomials: sums of powers<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>polynomial<\/strong> is a finite sum of multiples of\nnon-negative integer powers of a variable. Straight lines and parabolas\nboth belong to this family.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=a\\cdot x^2+b\\cdot x+c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘Eff of x equals a times x squared plus\nb times x plus c.’ The parameters\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>b<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>c<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare fixed numbers;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the input;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmeans\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntimes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWhen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>≠<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a\\ne0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe obtain a <strong>quadratic polynomial<\/strong> — the familiar\nparabola from M1-L13. If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>≠<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b\\ne0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nit is a polynomial of degree one. If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe constant function\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nremains (degree zero for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mo>≠<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c\\ne0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthe zero polynomial is treated separately).<\/p>\n<p>Polynomials can have several extreme points or roots. Among other\nthings, they are suitable for locally approximating smooth\ncharacteristic curves in engineering. Whether an approximation still\nmakes sense outside the range of the data must be checked\nseparately.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Rational functions: one polynomial divided by another<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>rational<\/strong> expression has a polynomial in the\nnumerator and a polynomial in the denominator. The most important\nadditional question is: <strong>When is the denominator zero?<\/strong>\nThese inputs are excluded.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">only for <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>≠<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(x)=\\frac{x+1}{x-2}\\qquad\\text{only for }x\\neq2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘Gee of x equals x plus one, divided by\nx minus two; x must not be two.’\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>g<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nnames the function;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(x+1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the numerator,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(x-2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe denominator, and <code>/<\/code> means divided by. At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe denominator would be zero — the function is\n<strong>undefined<\/strong> there. As\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\napproaches two, the magnitude of the function value can become very\nlarge; we return to this behaviour in M2-L2.<\/p>\n<p>Such quotients arise, for example, in ratios such as cost per unit,\nspeed calculated from distance and time, or rational model functions. A\nzero denominator is a mathematical problem; it must not simply be\nreplaced with an arbitrarily invented number.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exponential functions: repeated growth by the same\nfactor<\/h2>\n<p>In an exponential function the <strong>variable is in the\nexponent<\/strong>, while the base is fixed.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>2<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=2^x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘Eff of x equals two to the power of x.’\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>2<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the fixed base;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndetermines the exponent. For example, the value at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis one, at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nit is two, and at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nit is eight. An exponential function with a base greater than one\ndescribes growth, provided that\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis considered in equal steps.<\/p>\n<p>More generally, a non-constant real exponential function\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>a<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=a^x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis defined for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>≠<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a\\ne1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand takes <strong>only positive function values<\/strong>. A base between\nzero and one gives exponential decay as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nincreases. The base\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwould give only the constant function\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe frequently used <strong>Euler’s number e<\/strong> is a special base;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e^x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas properties that are particularly convenient in differential\ncalculus.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Logarithmic functions: finding the exponent<\/h2>\n<p>The logarithmic function reverses an exponential function. It answers\nthe question: <strong>What exponent do I need to obtain a particular\nnumber?<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">because <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><msup><mn>2<\/mn><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\log_2(8)=3\\qquad\\text{because }2^3=8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘The logarithm of eight to base two\nequals three, because two cubed equals eight.’ <code>₂<\/code> denotes\nthe base two, not multiplication by two.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\log_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the name of the corresponding logarithmic function.<\/p>\n<p>The <strong>natural logarithm<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ln(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nuses the base\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>e<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nEuler’s number. As a real function,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ln(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis defined only for positive\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nLogarithms arise, among other things, in the study of growth processes,\norders of magnitude and measures of information.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Trigonometric functions: repeating patterns<\/h2>\n<p>The <strong>sine<\/strong> and <strong>cosine functions<\/strong>\nassign coordinates on the unit circle to an angle (<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L20&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L20<\/a>). As functions of a real\nnumber, they can also model repeated oscillations.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=\\sin(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘Eff of x equals sine of x.’ Here\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngenerally denotes an angle in <strong>radians<\/strong>, not\nautomatically in degrees. At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe function value is zero; at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mi>π<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=\\frac{\\pi}{2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nit is one; after a full period of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\pi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe pattern repeats.<\/p>\n<p>Such periodic functions are central to <strong>audio engineering,\nwaves, sensors, rotation and signal processing<\/strong>. A real signal,\nhowever, generally does not consist of exactly one sine wave, but may\ncombine several components and disturbances.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Comparison of the most important families of functions<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Family of functions<\/th>\n<th>Typical notation<\/th>\n<th>Characteristics<\/th>\n<th>Typical restriction<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Linear / polynomial of degree one<\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=mx+b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>≠<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m\\ne0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>Equal change per unit of x<\/td>\n<td>When\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nonly a constant function; limit extrapolation<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Quadratic / polynomial of degree two<\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=ax^2+bx+c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>≠<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a\\ne0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>Parabola, curvature, possible maximum/minimum<\/td>\n<td>When\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nnot quadratic; consider model domain<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rational<\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mfrac><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=\\frac{p(x)}{q(x)}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>Quotient of polynomials<\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≠<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q(x)\\ne0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Exponential<\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>a<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=a^x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>Same multiplication factor per step of x<\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand for a non-constant model\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>≠<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a\\ne1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Logarithmic<\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=\\ln(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>Inverse of an exponential function<\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Trigonometric<\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=\\sin(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>Recurring, periodic pattern<\/td>\n<td>Consider angular units and actual signal shape<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The names\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin the row for rational functions refer to <strong>polynomials<\/strong>,\nnot probabilities;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>m<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>b<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare the parameters of a straight line familiar from M1-L8. These\nexamples do not specify every possible form in each family.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Fully explained mini-example: linear or exponential?<\/h2>\n<p>A service initially processes <strong>100 tasks<\/strong>. Two simple\nmodels are possible: it increases its performance in each time interval\nby <strong>20 additional tasks<\/strong> or by <strong>20 per cent of the\ncurrent amount<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Linear:<\/mtext><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>L<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mi>t<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Exponential:<\/mtext><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>G<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1.2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>t<\/mi><\/msup><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{Linear:}\\quad L(t)&amp;=100+20t\\\\\n\\text{Exponential:}\\quad G(t)&amp;=100\\cdot\\left(1.2\\right)^t\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘Ell of t equals one hundred plus twenty\ntimes t.’ And: ‘Gee of t equals one hundred times one point two to the\npower of t.’\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the number of equal-length time steps\n(<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>…<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=0,1,2,3,\\ldots<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin the discrete model considered here);\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>L<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>G<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare model names;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>1.2<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the growth factor for an increase of 20 per cent per step.\nIntermediate values for non-integer\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwould be an additional modelling assumption.<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Time step\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/th>\n<th>Linear\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L(t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/th>\n<th>Exponential\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G(t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>0<\/td>\n<td>100<\/td>\n<td>100<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1<\/td>\n<td>120<\/td>\n<td>120<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2<\/td>\n<td>140<\/td>\n<td>144<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5<\/td>\n<td>200<\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>248.832<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">248.832<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The two models initially give similar values but later develop\ndifferently. The mathematically more appropriate model follows\n<strong>not from the prettier graph<\/strong>, but from the real\nmechanism: are a fixed 20 tasks added per step, or does the number grow\nproportionally to the amount already reached? Actual server capacity\ncannot grow exponentially without limit either; the assumption can apply\nonly within a certain range.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S11\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What does calculus study about functions?<\/h2>\n<p>With the families of functions we already know, we can ask: Which\ninputs are allowed? What is the function value for a particular input?\nWhere does the graph cross the axes? What pattern of change can we\nrecognise?<\/p>\n<p><strong>Calculus<\/strong> adds further questions: <strong>What value\ndoes a function approach?<\/strong> How quickly does it change <strong>at\none particular instant<\/strong>? Where does it reach a maximum or\nminimum value? And how much has <strong>accumulated<\/strong> over a\nperiod of time? This gives rise to the concepts of <em>limit<\/em>,\n<em>derivative<\/em> and <em>integral<\/em> — introduced in detail only in\nthe following lessons.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S12\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is it useful for?<\/h2>\n<p>In <strong>software engineering<\/strong>, choosing a family of\nfunctions is part of the modelling problem: runtimes, capacities and\ncosts can follow linear, curved or exponential patterns. In\n<strong>AI<\/strong>, function shapes are used in activation functions,\nprobability models and optimisation objectives. For <strong>measurements\nand signals<\/strong>, periodic models make it possible to distinguish\nregular processes from irregular deviations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S13\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>Assigning a function to a family is initially a <strong>mathematical\nclassification<\/strong>, not empirical proof that a real system works\nthat way. Domains must always be considered: division by zero is not\nallowed, real logarithms require positive arguments, and trigonometric\nformulas require an unambiguous angular unit. With discrete data, a\ncontinuous curve may also be merely a modelling assumption.<\/p>\n<p>The Abitur classification follows the general topic of calculus.\n<strong>Which individual methods are compulsory at basic or\nadvanced-course level in a particular Berlin examination year is checked\nseparately against the current official requirements.<\/strong><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S14\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Link to the next unit<\/h2>\n<p>In <strong><a href=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L2<\/a><\/strong> we\nstudy limits, the behaviour of functions near critical points and the\nidea of continuity. We also learn how to read the symbol\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">lim<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lim<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\naloud precisely.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S15\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<p>No individual sources are listed separately for this lesson.<\/p>\n<\/article>","school_mathematics_en:M2-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M2-L2 – Limits, limit notation and continuity<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M2 – Upper secondary education / Abitur<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Upper secondary education / Abitur<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> No specific prerequisites\nstated.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Classification:<\/strong> Upper secondary education at a\nGymnasium · Calculus<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is this about?<\/h2>\n<p>In calculus we often want to know how a function value behaves\n<strong>as we get increasingly close to a particular input<\/strong>. We\ndo not necessarily have to be able to substitute that input. A limit\ndescribes the value that the outputs approach — not necessarily a value\nthat the function actually takes at the point in question. Continuity\nanswers a different, related question: does the actual function value\nagree with what we observe immediately beside it?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Basic idea and intuition<\/h2>\n<p>Imagine zooming further and further into a graph around a certain\npoint. If the function values from the left and right approach the same\nheight, the function has a <strong>finite two-sided limit<\/strong>\nthere. The curve may nevertheless have a gap at exactly that point. A\nfunction is continuous there if the function value exists and agrees\nwith the value being approached. The <strong>two-sided<\/strong> limits\nstudied here require that we can approach the target from both\ndirections using admissible inputs; at endpoints we consider the\naccessible side.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-mathematics-en/media/img/S_MAT_021_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Straight line y equals x plus 1 with an open circle at (1,2); the function has a gap there and the limit is 2.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: The graph of y = x + 1 has a hole at (1, 2), yet the\nlimit there is still 2.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>A limit can exist even though the function is undefined at that\npoint.<\/p>\n<p>An approach also works for large inputs: we can ask whether a\nquantity approaches a fixed bound in the long term, grows without bound\nor oscillates. The symbol for ‘infinity’ does <strong>not denote an\nordinary real number<\/strong> that we could substitute like three or\nfive.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>New symbols before the first formula<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Symbol<\/th>\n<th>Read aloud<\/th>\n<th>Meaning here<\/th>\n<th>Other possible uses<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">lim<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lim<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘limit’<\/td>\n<td>Limit of a function<\/td>\n<td>Also used with sequences and series<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x\\to a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘x tends to a’<\/td>\n<td>Inputs approach a without necessarily reaching it<\/td>\n<td>Arrow also denotes a mapping in <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L18&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L18<\/a><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><msup><mi>a<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x\\to a^-<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘x tends to a from the left’<\/td>\n<td>Approach through smaller input values<\/td>\n<td>Superscript minus is <em>not<\/em> a negative sign for a here<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><msup><mi>a<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x\\to a^+<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘x tends to a from the right’<\/td>\n<td>Approach through larger input values<\/td>\n<td>Plus is a direction marker here<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>∞<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\infty<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>+<\/mi><mi>∞<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+\\infty<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>∞<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-\\infty<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘infinity’, ‘plus infinity’, ‘minus infinity’<\/td>\n<td>Unbounded increase or decrease, or increasingly large inputs<\/td>\n<td>Not ordinary real numbers<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘eff of x’<\/td>\n<td>Function value for input x<\/td>\n<td>Familiar from <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L4<\/a>/M2-L1<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n/\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>L<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘ay’ / ‘ell’<\/td>\n<td>a: target of inputs; L: possible value being approached<\/td>\n<td>Letters are not fixed mathematical constants<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>≠<\/code><\/td>\n<td>‘is not equal to’<\/td>\n<td>Values are unequal<\/td>\n<td>General comparison<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>≈<\/code><\/td>\n<td>‘is approximately equal to’<\/td>\n<td>Numerical approximation<\/td>\n<td>Do not read as an exact equality<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Reading limits in everyday language<\/h2>\n<p>A classic example is a simple straight line:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">lim<\/mi><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><\/munder><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>7<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\nf(x)&amp;=2x+1\\\\\n\\lim_{x\\to3}f(x)&amp;=7\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud, first line:<\/strong> ‘Eff of x equals two times x\nplus one.’\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the function name,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe input;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmeans two times x;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>+<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis a fixed addition.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud, second line:<\/strong> ‘The limit of eff of x as x\ntends to three equals seven.’\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">lim<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lim<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nasks for the value being approached;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x\\to3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndescribes the input approaching three;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the observed function value;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>7<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">7<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the limit. Here direct substitution is allowed because the straight\nline is continuous at three: twice three plus one equals seven.<\/p>\n<p>For more complicated functions, the limit question cannot always be\nanswered by substitution. That is exactly what makes the concept\nuseful.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A limit despite a gap: fully explained example<\/h2>\n<p>Consider a function that is <strong>undefined<\/strong> at x equals\none:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">(only for <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>≠<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">)<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\nf(x)&amp;=\\frac{x^2-1}{x-1}\\quad\\text{(only for }x\\ne1\\text{)}\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘Eff of x equals x squared minus one,\ndivided by x minus one. X must not be one.’ The horizontal line is the\nfraction bar,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmeans x times x, and <code>≠<\/code> means not equal to. At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nboth numerator and denominator would be zero; <strong>the original\ndivision is undefined there<\/strong>. The notation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mfrac><mn>0<\/mn><mn>0<\/mn><\/mfrac><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac00<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis an <em>indeterminate form<\/em> in the calculation of limits and does\nnot itself give a numerical value.<\/p>\n<p>The numerator can be factorised using the <strong>difference of two\nsquares<\/strong> familiar from M1-L13. For all admissible inputs, the\nexpression simplifies to:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>≠<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{x^2-1}{x-1}=\\frac{(x-1)(x+1)}{x-1}=x+1\\qquad(x\\neq1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘X squared minus one divided by x minus\none equals x plus one, provided that x is not one.’ Cancellation\nexplicitly requires a <strong>non-zero<\/strong> factor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWe have <strong>not<\/strong> defined the function at the forbidden\npoint; we have merely rewritten it for all other inputs.<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Input\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/th>\n<th>Output\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0.9<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.9<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>1.9<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.9<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0.99<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.99<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>1.99<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.99<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>1.01<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.01<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>2.01<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2.01<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>1.1<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>2.1<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2.1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The values approach two from both sides. In limit notation:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">lim<\/mi><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/munder><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lim_{x\\to1}\\frac{x^2-1}{x-1}=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘The limit of x squared minus one\ndivided by x minus one as x tends to one equals two.’ Two is the value\n<strong>that the function values approach<\/strong>; it is not\nautomatically the function value\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn this example\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndoes not exist. If we explicitly add\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(1)=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe original gap is filled. If instead we set\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(1)=5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<strong>the limit would remain 2<\/strong>, but the new function would\nnot be continuous at one. The limit does not depend on the individual\nfunction value at the target point.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From the left and from the right: one-sided limits<\/h2>\n<p>A function can behave differently from either side. This can be seen\nat a jump. Consider the following piecewise-defined model:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">for <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">for <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=\\begin{cases}0&amp;\\text{for }x&lt;0\\\\1&amp;\\text{for }x\\geq0\\end{cases}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘Eff of x is zero for negative inputs\nand one for inputs greater than or equal to zero.’ The less-than sign\n<code>&lt;<\/code> and <code>≥<\/code> (greater than or equal to) restrict\nthe respective input cases.<\/p>\n<p>Approaching <strong>from the left<\/strong> gives the limit zero, and\napproaching <strong>from the right<\/strong> gives the limit one:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">lim<\/mi><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><msup><mn>0<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><\/munder><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">lim<\/mi><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><msup><mn>0<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><\/munder><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x\\to0^-}f(x)&amp;=0\\\\\n\\lim_{x\\to0^+}f(x)&amp;=1\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘The limit of eff of x as x tends to\nzero from the left is zero; from the right it is one.’ Since the two\nvalues being approached <strong>do not agree<\/strong>, <strong>no finite\ntwo-sided limit<\/strong> exists at this point. This is a <strong>jump\ndiscontinuity<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What does continuity mean?<\/h2>\n<p>A function is continuous at a point if a function value exists there,\nif the graph approaches this same value from the left and right, and if\nthe limit and function value agree.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">lim<\/mi><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><\/mrow><\/munder><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lim_{x\\to a}f(x)=f(a)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘The limit of eff of x as x tends to a\nequals eff of a.’ On the left is the value being approached; on the\nright is the <strong>actual<\/strong> function value at input\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis equation is the short form of the continuity condition, provided\nthat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(a)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the finite two-sided limit are defined. At an <strong>interior\npoint<\/strong> of the domain, both sides of approach are relevant. At\nthe <strong>endpoint<\/strong> of an interval, continuity is checked\nrelative to the domain, that is, from the accessible side. Example:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mi>x<\/mi><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=\\sqrt{x}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\non\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x\\ge0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis right-continuous at zero, even though approaching from the left\nwithin its real domain is impossible. At isolated points, the usual\nlimit definition is not applicable in the same way as at points of an\ninterval.<\/p>\n<p>Intuitively, we distinguish a <strong>removable\ndiscontinuity<\/strong> (as in the cancelled rational-function example),\na <strong>jump<\/strong> (two different one-sided limits) and\n<strong>unbounded behaviour<\/strong> near a point (for instance,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><\/mfrac><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac1x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nnear zero: from the right the expression tends to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>+<\/mi><mi>∞<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+\\infty<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nfrom the left to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>∞<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-\\infty<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\ntherefore no common finite limit exists). A function that continues\nwithout a jump may nevertheless have a <strong>corner<\/strong>;\ncontinuity alone does not guarantee differentiability.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What happens for large inputs?<\/h2>\n<p>Consider\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><\/mfrac><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(x)=\\frac1x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor positive inputs only. The larger\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbecomes, the smaller and more nearly zero the positive value of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbecomes.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><\/mfrac><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">lim<\/mi><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mi>∞<\/mi><\/mrow><\/munder><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\ng(x)&amp;=\\frac1x\\qquad(x&gt;0)\\\\\n\\lim_{x\\to\\infty}g(x)&amp;=0\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘Gee of x is one divided by x, for\npositive inputs. The limit of gee of x as x tends to infinity is zero.’\nThe horizontal limiting value zero is not attained by the function\nvalues for finite positive\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mi>∞<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x\\to\\infty<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndescribes inputs growing without bound and is <strong>not substitution\nof x by a number called infinity<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is it useful for?<\/h2>\n<p>Limits prepare us for the concept of an <strong>instantaneous rate of\nchange<\/strong>: in <a href=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L3<\/a> we\nconsider increasingly small time intervals to determine a local\ngradient. In <strong>software development<\/strong>, limits are used in\nalgorithms, iterative approximation methods and stability analyses.\nContinuity helps us understand sensor and control signals, although\nactual measurements are discrete and noisy. In AI optimisation we also\ndistinguish between well-behaved smooth objectives and models with jumps\nor undefined regions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions, limitations and common mistakes<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A limit is <strong>not the same<\/strong> as substituting the target\nnumber. The function value may be missing there.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The form\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mfrac><mn>0<\/mn><mn>0<\/mn><\/mfrac><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac00<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis <strong>not a result<\/strong>. It initially shows only that direct\nsubstitution is insufficient for finding the limit; the function being\nstudied must be defined in a punctured neighbourhood of the target\npoint.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Different values from the right and left give no common finite\nlimit. Strong oscillation can also prevent a limit from existing, even\nif the function values remain bounded.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">‘Tends to infinity’ is not the same as ‘has a particularly large\nfinite value’.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Limits at a point are local statements. They do not automatically\nconfirm that a real model works outside its range of validity.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S11\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Link to the next unit<\/h2>\n<p>In <strong><a href=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L3<\/a><\/strong> we\nimagine shrinking an input interval further and further. The limit of an\naverage change then leads to the <strong>derivative<\/strong>, the\ninstantaneous rate of change and gradient of the tangent.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S12\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<p>No individual sources are listed separately for this lesson.<\/p>\n<\/article>","school_mathematics_en:M2-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M2-L3 – The derivative as an instantaneous rate of change<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M2 – Upper secondary education / Abitur<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Upper secondary education / Abitur<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> No specific prerequisites\nstated.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Classification:<\/strong> Upper secondary education at a\nGymnasium · Calculus<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is this about?<\/h2>\n<p>An average change can be calculated from two measurements. It is\noften more interesting to know <strong>how quickly a value changes at a\nsingle instant<\/strong> — for example, the speed right now rather than\nduring the last hour. Calculus solves this problem by imagining smaller\nand smaller gaps between the inputs being considered. The limit of the\naverage rates of change is called the <strong>derivative<\/strong>.\nGeometrically, the derivative describes the gradient of the tangent to\nthe graph.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Basic idea and intuition<\/h2>\n<p>On a straight line the gradient is the same everywhere; on a curved\nline it changes from point to point. If we join two nearby points on a\ncurve with a straight line, we obtain a <strong>secant<\/strong>. As the\nsecond point approaches the first, the gradient of the secant approaches\n— where the limit exists — the <strong>gradient of the tangent<\/strong>.\nThe derivative therefore gives a local answer to the question ‘How much\ndoes the output change for a tiny change in the input?’<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-mathematics-en/media/img/S_MAT_022_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Parabola f(x) = x² with P(1,1) and Q(2,4). The dashed orange secant has gradient 3; the green tangent at P has gradient 2.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=x^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe secant from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1,1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(2,4)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas slope 3, whereas the tangent at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas slope 2.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=x^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\nthe secant through\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1,1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(2,4)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas gradient 3;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f&#39;(1)=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the gradient of the tangent.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>New symbols before the first formula<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Symbol<\/th>\n<th>Read aloud<\/th>\n<th>Meaning here<\/th>\n<th>Other possible uses<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘delta’<\/td>\n<td>Finite change, the difference between two values<\/td>\n<td>Already used for differences in <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L8&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L8<\/a>; later also an operator\nname<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘delta x’, ‘delta y’<\/td>\n<td>Change in input or output over an interval<\/td>\n<td>Not identical to a differential<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>h<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘aitch’<\/td>\n<td>Small input gap from the point in question<\/td>\n<td>For example, height in <a href=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L5<\/a>,\nbut not a height here<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘x nought’, ‘x subscript zero’<\/td>\n<td>Fixed input where the gradient is sought<\/td>\n<td>Subscript zero may denote an initial value<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">lim<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lim_{h\\to0}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘limit as h tends to zero’<\/td>\n<td>Limit for an increasingly small gap<\/td>\n<td>Limit notation from <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L2<\/a><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f&#39;(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f&#39;(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘eff prime of x’<\/td>\n<td>First derivative of function f at x<\/td>\n<td>In geometry a prime may mark another figure<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘dee y by dee x’ or ‘differential y over differential x’<\/td>\n<td>Leibniz notation for the derivative of y with respect to x<\/td>\n<td>In integrals\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{d}x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas a different role; precise definition later<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s(t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘ess of t’<\/td>\n<td>Here: distance travelled as a function of time t<\/td>\n<td>Elsewhere s may denote another quantity<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Average rate of change: the difference quotient<\/h2>\n<p>Consider two inputs\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the corresponding outputs\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x_1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x_2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe average change per unit of input is:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>≠<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta x}=\\frac{f(x_2)-f(x_1)}{x_2-x_1}\\qquad (x_2\\ne x_1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘Delta y divided by delta x equals the\nfunction value at x two minus the function value at x one, divided by x\ntwo minus x one.’\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the total difference in output;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the difference in input; <code>/<\/code> means division;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>≠<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_2\\ne x_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nprevents a zero denominator. The quantity is the <strong>gradient of the\nsecant<\/strong> between the two points. The unit and meaning depend on\nthe axes: metres and seconds give metres per second.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The instantaneous rate of change: from average to limit<\/h2>\n<p>For the derivative we keep an input\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfixed and consider a second point at a distance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>h<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThen we let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>h<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntend to zero. Provided that the function is defined in an <strong>open\nneighbourhood<\/strong> of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the finite two-sided limit exists, this is the ordinary derivative\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">lim<\/mi><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><\/munder><mfrac><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>h<\/mi><\/mfrac><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f&#39;(x_0)=\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(x_0+h)-f(x_0)}{h}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘Eff prime of x nought equals the limit\nas h tends to zero of eff of x nought plus h minus eff of x nought, all\ndivided by h.’ The numerator describes the change in output;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>h<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the gap between the two inputs; the\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">lim<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lim<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nexpression considers arbitrarily small <strong>non-zero<\/strong> gaps.\n<strong>Never substitute zero directly for h in the fraction.<\/strong>\nThe required limit is the instantaneous gradient. <strong>Both signs of\nh<\/strong> must lead to admissible inputs for an ordinary two-sided\nderivative. Endpoints of a model require separate one-sided\nconsideration.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f&#39;(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrepresents a <strong>new function<\/strong>, where it is defined for the\ncorresponding x values. Its value is the local gradient of the original\nfunction. For a graph with a positive gradient the derivative is\npositive; for a negative gradient it is negative. A derivative of zero\nmeans a <strong>horizontal tangent<\/strong> — but not necessarily a\nmaximum or minimum point.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Fully explained mini-example: a quadratic distance\nmodel<\/h2>\n<p>As a simplified model of motion for\n<strong><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t\\ge0\\,\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/strong>,\nsuppose that the distance travelled since the start at time\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfollows a quadratic pattern. For negative times the interpretation as\n<em>distance travelled since the start<\/em> would not be appropriate. To\nensure that the <strong>units agree<\/strong>, with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin seconds and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>s<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin metres we first write:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mi>t<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Numerical-value formula: <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>t<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">: numerical value of time in <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mo>;<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">: numerical value of distance in <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\ns(t)&amp;=(1\\,\\mathrm{m}/\\mathrm{s}^2)\\cdot t^2\\\\\n\\text{Numerical-value formula: }\\quad s(t)&amp;=t^2\\\\\n&amp;\\quad(t\\text{: numerical value of time in }\\mathrm{s};\\\\\n&amp;\\qquad s(t)\\text{: numerical value of distance in }\\mathrm{m})\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘Ess of t equals one metre per second\nsquared times t squared.’ The factor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm{m}/\\mathrm{s}^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nensures that the right-hand side has the unit metre. In the following\nshort rearrangements,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>h<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nstand for <strong>numerical values of times in seconds<\/strong>; the\ncalculated change in distance is given in metres and the derivative in\nmetres per second. In the physical model, the two times being compared\nmust also satisfy\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t\\ge0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t+h\\ge0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe notation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>t<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s(t)=t^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nalone is therefore a simplified numerical-value formula, not a complete\nphysical equation with units.<\/p>\n<p>Between the times\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=2\\,\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=4\\,\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe distance increases from four to sixteen metres. The average speed is\ntherefore:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>16<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{16\\,\\mathrm{m}-4\\,\\mathrm{m}}{4\\,\\mathrm{s}-2\\,\\mathrm{s}}=6\\,\\mathrm{m}/\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘Sixteen metres minus four metres,\ndivided by four seconds minus two seconds, equals six metres per\nsecond.’ This is an average speed <strong>over two seconds<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<p>What happens instead <strong>at exactly three seconds<\/strong>? For\nthe instantaneous rate of change we substitute the general function rule\nwith the small gap\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>h<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>h<\/mi><\/mfrac><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><\/mrow><mi>h<\/mi><\/mfrac><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>6<\/mn><mi>h<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>h<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><mi>h<\/mi><\/mfrac><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">for <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo>≠<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{s(3+h)-s(3)}h&amp;=\\frac{{(3+h)}^2-9}h\\\\\n&amp;=\\frac{6h+h^2}h\\\\\n&amp;=6+h\\quad\\text{for }h\\neq0\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘The distance value at three plus h\nminus the distance value at three, divided by h, simplifies to six plus\nh — provided that h is not zero.’ Here\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>h<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas the dimension of time, measured in seconds; the numerical\ncoefficients of the simplified terms carry the appropriate units in the\nphysical model. The closer\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>h<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis to zero, the closer the quotient is to six metres per second.\nTherefore:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>s<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">(shorthand: derivative at <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">)<\/mtext><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s&#39;(3)=6\\,\\mathrm{m}/\\mathrm{s}\\qquad\\text{(shorthand: derivative at }t=3\\,\\mathrm{s}\\text{)}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘Ess prime at three equals six metres\nper second.’ The <strong>instantaneous speed<\/strong> at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=3\\,\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nequals the previously calculated average speed between two and four\nseconds in this special case. This is because the interval is\nsymmetrical about three, and it does not hold for arbitrary curves and\nintervals.<\/p>\n<p>In general, the same calculation for the quadratic model gives the\ngradient function:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msup><mi>s<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Numerical-value formula: <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi>s<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>t<\/mi><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mrow><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\"> in seconds; speed in <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\ns&#39;(t)&amp;=(2\\,\\mathrm{m}/\\mathrm{s}^2)\\cdot t\\\\\n\\text{Numerical-value formula: }\\quad s&#39;(t)&amp;=2t\n\\quad(t\\text{ in seconds; speed in }\\mathrm{m}/\\mathrm{s})\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘Ess prime of t equals two times t.’ The\nnumerical value of the speed function increases linearly with time; the\nimplicit unit scale of the original distance model must be carried\nthrough.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The two notations for the derivative<\/h2>\n<p>A derivative can be written in two common forms:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">when <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f&#39;(x)=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\qquad\\text{when }y=f(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘Eff prime of x equals dee y by dee x,\nwhen y equals eff of x.’\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f&#39;(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncomes from <strong>Lagrange notation<\/strong>;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis <strong>Leibniz notation<\/strong>. Here both denote the same first\nderivative. As an introductory intuition,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncan be read as the limiting case of ‘small change in output per small\nchange in input’; however, it is <strong>not simply an ordinary fraction\nmade up of two arbitrary small numbers<\/strong>. The more formal meaning\nof differentials comes later.<\/p>\n<p>Leibniz notation often makes it particularly clear <strong>which\nquantity is differentiated with respect to which other\nquantity<\/strong>. This becomes important later with functions of\nseveral variables, in physics and in robotics.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>When does a derivative not exist?<\/h2>\n<p>A function need not be differentiable at a point. At a <strong>sharp\ncorner<\/strong>, the gradients from the left and right may differ. The\nsimplest example is the absolute value function\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=|x|<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\nto the left of zero the gradient is minus one, and to the right it is\nplus one. The function is <strong>continuous<\/strong> at zero but has\n<strong>no two-sided derivative<\/strong> there. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo>≠<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h\\ne0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe difference quotient at zero is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><mi>h<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>h<\/mi><\/mfrac><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{|h|-0}{h}=\\frac{|h|}{h}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h&lt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nit gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nit gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>+<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThere is no common limit. A jump discontinuity is also not\ndifferentiable.<\/p>\n<p>Measurements present a different problem: we have only finitely many\ntime points and no exact continuous function. A ‘derivative’ calculated\nnumerically from measurements is therefore an <strong>estimate<\/strong>.\nVery small time gaps may amplify noise or rounding errors.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is it useful for?<\/h2>\n<p>Derivatives are used in <strong>AI<\/strong> to understand how\nsensitive an objective function is to changes in individual model\nparameters — the basis of gradient methods. In <strong>robotics<\/strong>\nthey describe speeds and, later, accelerations of movements. In\n<strong>software analysis<\/strong> they help describe local changes in a\nmetric and optimise smooth models. For actual sensor data we must\ndistinguish between the mathematical derivative of a model and a\nnumerically estimated rate of change.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A derivative is <strong>local<\/strong> information; a large local\ngradient does not guarantee sustained growth.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The denominator of the difference quotient must not be zero; only\ntaking the limit gives the derivative.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f&#39;(x)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndoes <strong>not<\/strong> prove a maximum or minimum.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Continuity is necessary for differentiability at <strong>interior\npoints of the domain<\/strong>, but not sufficient; a corner may be\ncontinuous and not differentiable. At the endpoint of a physical time\ninterval we can instead examine a one-sided rate of change.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A physical model requires consistent <strong>units<\/strong>; the\nderivative of a distance function with respect to time has the unit\ndistance per time.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S11\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Link to the next unit<\/h2>\n<p>In <strong><a href=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L4<\/a><\/strong> we\nlearn practical rules for finding derivatives of combined functions\nwithout recalculating the limit for every expression each time.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S12\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<p>No individual sources are listed separately for this lesson.<\/p>\n<\/article>","school_mathematics_en:M2-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M2-L4 – Differentiation rules and combinations of functions<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M2 – Upper secondary education / Abitur<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Upper secondary education / Abitur<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> No specific prerequisites\nstated.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Classification:<\/strong> Upper secondary education at a\nGymnasium · Calculus; some rules for combining functions are studied in\ngreater depth depending on level<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is this about?<\/h2>\n<p>In <a href=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L3<\/a> we learnt about the\nderivative as the limit of average rates of change over increasingly\nsmall intervals. Calculating such limits individually would be\ncumbersome for long formulas. Fortunately, derivatives follow clear\n<strong>calculation rules<\/strong>. They show how the local change of a\nsum, product, quotient or nested function is composed of simpler\nderivatives. This makes differential calculus a practical tool for\nmodelling, optimisation and the mathematics behind neural networks.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Basic idea and intuition<\/h2>\n<p>A composite function is rather like a program that performs several\ncalculation steps. When the input changes slightly, the result may\nchange by different amounts at each intermediate step. The <strong>chain\nrule<\/strong> follows a small change through such a sequence of\noperations. In a <strong>product<\/strong>, each factor generally\nchanges; in a <strong>quotient<\/strong>, the denominator also influences\nthe change. The rules deal with these dependencies systematically.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>New symbols before the first formula<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Symbol<\/th>\n<th>Read aloud<\/th>\n<th>Meaning here<\/th>\n<th>Other possible uses<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>g<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>u<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>v<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘eff’, ‘gee’, ‘you’, ‘vee’<\/td>\n<td>Names of functions of an input x<\/td>\n<td>The same letters may denote numbers, vectors or quantities<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f&#39;(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g&#39;(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘eff prime of x’, ‘gee prime of x’<\/td>\n<td>First derivatives of f and g respectively<\/td>\n<td>In geometry a prime also marks similar figures<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(fg)(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘product function f times g at x’<\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)\\cdot g(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>Brackets may otherwise group arguments<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(g(x))<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo>∘<\/mo><mi>g<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f\\circ g<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘eff of gee of x’, ‘f composed with g’<\/td>\n<td>Composition: apply g first, then f; <code>∘<\/code> is the\ncomposition symbol<\/td>\n<td>Often nested function calls in code<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘en’<\/td>\n<td>Here: fixed exponent<\/td>\n<td>Often a count or index in other topics<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>c<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘see’<\/td>\n<td>Here: fixed constant<\/td>\n<td>In geometry, for example, the hypotenuse<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>≠<\/code><\/td>\n<td>‘not equal to’<\/td>\n<td>Excludes a zero denominator<\/td>\n<td>General comparison symbol<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℝ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>π<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<code>°<\/code><\/td>\n<td>‘real numbers’, ‘pi’, ‘degrees’<\/td>\n<td>Real number system, circle constant, angles in degrees<\/td>\n<td>The degree sign is not a differentiation variable<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>e<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ln<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cos<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘Euler’s number’, ‘natural logarithm’, ‘sine’, ‘cosine’<\/td>\n<td>Familiar functions from <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L19&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L19<\/a>/M2-L1<\/td>\n<td>Differentiation rules for trigonometric functions use radians<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The simplest rules: constants, sums and powers<\/h2>\n<p>For a <strong>constant function<\/strong>, the derivative is zero: a\nnumber that always stays the same does not change. The derivative of the\n<strong>constant multiple<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c\\cdot u(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mi>u<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c\\cdot u&#39;(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\na sum or difference can be <strong>differentiated term by term<\/strong>.\nFor a power, the fixed exponent becomes a factor in front and is reduced\nby one.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>⟹<\/mo><msup><mi>f<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>v<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>⟹<\/mo><msup><mi>f<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>u<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>v<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>⟹<\/mo><msup><mi>f<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><msup><mi>u<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msup><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>⟹<\/mo><msup><mi>f<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\nf(x)=c&amp;\\implies f^{\\prime}(x)=0\\\\\nf(x)=u(x)+v(x)&amp;\\implies f^{\\prime}(x)=u^{\\prime}(x)+v^{\\prime}(x)\\\\\nf(x)=cu(x)&amp;\\implies f^{\\prime}(x)=cu^{\\prime}(x)\\\\\nf(x)=x^n&amp;\\implies f^{\\prime}(x)=nx^{n-1}\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud, first line:<\/strong> ‘If eff of x is the constant\nc, then eff prime of x is zero.’\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>c<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndoes not depend on\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Second line:<\/strong> ‘The derivative of the sum of u of x\nand v of x is the sum of u prime and v prime at x.’\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>u<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>v<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare two differentiable component functions.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Third line:<\/strong> ‘The derivative of c times u of x is c\ntimes u prime of x.’ The constant is not itself differentiated.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Fourth line:<\/strong> ‘The derivative of x to the power of n\nis n times x to the power of n minus one.’ The exponent\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis <strong>fixed<\/strong>, not a second variable. This power rule\napplies to <strong>positive<\/strong> integer exponents throughout\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℝ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nas the constant function\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>1<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(defined this way even at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthis does not imply a general definition of the isolated expression\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>0<\/mn><mn>0<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0^0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>).\nFor <strong>negative integer<\/strong> exponents we require\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>≠<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x\\ne0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nfor general real exponents the usual real domain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis safe. The exponent is not a second variable.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Mini-example:<\/strong> For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>7<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=3x^2+2x+7<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe derivative of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3x^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>6<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe derivative of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the constant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>2<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand the derivative of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>7<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">7<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis zero. Hence:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f&#39;(x)=6x+2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘Eff prime of x equals six times x plus\ntwo.’ The result describes the local gradient of the original parabola\nfor each admissible input.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The product rule — when both factors vary<\/h2>\n<p>In a product of two functions, <strong>both factors<\/strong> may\nchange with the input. Therefore it is not enough simply to multiply the\ntwo derivatives together:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">{(f\\cdot g)}&#39;<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis generally <strong>not<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mi>g<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f&#39;\\cdot g&#39;<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>f<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mi>g<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">{(f\\cdot g)}&#39;(x)=f&#39;(x)\\cdot g(x)+f(x)\\cdot g&#39;(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘The derivative of the product of f and\ng is f prime times g plus f times g prime, each at x.’ The first term\naccounts for the change in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwhile imagining\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>g<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nheld fixed; the second accounts for the change in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>g<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwhile imagining\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nheld fixed. For an ordinary derivative both component functions must be\ndifferentiable at the point.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Mini-example:<\/strong> We multiply\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=x^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nby\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(x)=x+1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msup><mi>h<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\nh(x)&amp;=x^2(x+1)\\\\\nh^{\\prime}(x)&amp;=(2x)(x+1)+x^2\\cdot1=3x^2+2x\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘H prime of x is two x times x plus one\nin brackets, plus x squared times one; altogether three x squared plus\ntwo x.’\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>h<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nnames the new product function. We can check the result by first\nexpanding\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^2(x+1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^3+x^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand then differentiating term by term. For example, at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwe obtain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>h<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>2<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>16<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h&#39;(2)=3\\cdot2^2+2\\cdot2=16<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nincorrectly multiplying the individual derivatives would give only\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2x\\cdot1=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The quotient rule — when the denominator also varies<\/h2>\n<p>For a quotient of functions the denominator must be non-zero. The\nrule for differentiation accounts for the fact that the denominator\nitself may depend on the input.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac><mi>f<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>f<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mi>g<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mfrac><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\left(\\frac fg\\right)&#39;(x)=\\frac{f&#39;(x)\\cdot g(x)-f(x)\\cdot g&#39;(x)}{{(g(x))}^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘The derivative of f over g is f prime\ntimes g minus f times g prime, divided by g squared, all at x.’\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the numerator function,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>g<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe denominator function;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">{(g(x))}^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmeans that the entire denominator function value is squared. Both\nfunctions must be differentiable at the point being considered and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≠<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(x)\\ne0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\n<strong>Do not confuse:<\/strong> The derivative of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mfrac><mi>f<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/mfrac><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac fg<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis generally not\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mfrac><msup><mi>f<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><msup><mi>g<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><\/mfrac><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{f&#39;}{g&#39;}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Mini-example:<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>≠<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msup><mi>q<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mfrac><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\nq(x)&amp;=\\frac{x}{x+1}\\quad(x\\neq-1)\\\\\nq^{\\prime}(x)&amp;=\\frac{(1)(x+1)-x(1)}{{(x+1)}^2}=\\frac1{{(x+1)}^2}\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘Q prime of x is one times x plus one in\nbrackets, minus x times one, divided by x plus one squared; this\nsimplifies to one over x plus one squared.’ The value\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nremains excluded; the differentiated formula must not be used there\neither.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The chain rule — change through several calculation\nsteps<\/h2>\n<p>In a <strong>composition<\/strong>, we first calculate the inner\nfunction\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand substitute its result into the outer function\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe derivative takes account of how much the inner quantity changes\n<strong>and<\/strong> how sensitive the outer function is to that\nchange.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-mathematics-en/media/img/S_MAT_023_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Chain of functions and derivative of a composite function\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: The chain rule combines inner and outer derivatives:\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=(x^2+1)^3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y^{\\prime}=3(x^2+1)^2\\cdot2x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo>∘<\/mo><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>f<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mi>g<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">{(f\\circ g)}&#39;(x)=f&#39;(g(x))\\cdot g&#39;(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘The derivative of f composed with g is\nf prime evaluated at g of x, times g prime of x.’ The new symbol\n<code>∘<\/code> is called the <strong>composition symbol<\/strong> or\n‘circle’ and means: apply the function\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>g<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\non the right first, then the function\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\non the left. It is <strong>not ordinary multiplication<\/strong>. For the\nrule,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>g<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmust be differentiable at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmust lie in the domain of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmust be differentiable at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Fully explained mini-example:<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msup><mi>H<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\nH(x)&amp;={(3x+1)}^2\\\\\nH^{\\prime}(x)&amp;=2(3x+1)\\cdot3=6(3x+1)\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘H of x is three x plus one in brackets\nsquared. H prime of x is two times three x plus one in brackets times\nthree, that is six times three x plus one in brackets.’ The\n<strong>inner function<\/strong> is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(x)=3x+1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith derivative three. The <strong>outer function<\/strong> squares its\ninput; its derivative is twice that input. Therefore the factor three\nfrom the inner derivative must be included. It must not be\nforgotten.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>More common derivatives as a reading dictionary<\/h2>\n<p>The following familiar functions have particularly simple\nderivatives. We will need them again later in specific models.<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Original function<\/th>\n<th>First derivative<\/th>\n<th>In words and with domain of validity<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=e^x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f&#39;(x)=e^x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘E to the power of x stays e to the power of x’; all real x<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=\\ln(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><\/mfrac><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f&#39;(x)=\\frac1x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘The natural logarithm becomes one over x’; positive x<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=\\sin(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f&#39;(x)=\\cos(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘The sine function becomes the cosine function’; angles in\nradians<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=\\cos(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f&#39;(x)=-\\sin(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘The cosine function becomes minus sine’; angles in radians<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p><strong>Explanation of symbols:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>e<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis Euler’s number,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ln<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe natural logarithm, and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cos<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare sine and cosine. <code>−<\/code> before\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmeans the negative of the sine value. The natural logarithm model\nrequires\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor trigonometric derivatives, <strong>radians<\/strong> are essential:\nif an angle variable is measured in degrees instead, the differentiation\nfactors change. Example for a numerical value\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin <strong>degrees<\/strong>:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>π<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><mn>180<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(x)=\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{180}\\right)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac><mi>π<\/mi><mn>180<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>⋅<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>π<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><mn>180<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g&#39;(x)=\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{180}\\right)\\cdot\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{180}\\right)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe extra factor comes from the chain rule.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>π<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndenotes the circle constant pi.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Which rule suits which formula?<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">For a <strong>sum<\/strong>, we study the individual terms.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">For a <strong>product<\/strong> of two variable factors, we use the\nproduct rule.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">For a <strong>quotient<\/strong> with a variable denominator, we use\nthe quotient rule as long as the denominator is non-zero.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">For a <strong>nested<\/strong> calculation, we use the chain rule; it\nmay be needed in addition to the product or quotient rule.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>The notation determines which calculation methods are particularly\nsimple: sometimes <strong>expanding before differentiating<\/strong> is\nshorter than using the product rule. There is not always exactly one\nsuitable method.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is it useful for?<\/h2>\n<p>In <strong>AI<\/strong>, the chain rule is the mathematical core of\n<em>backpropagation<\/em>: an error measure depends on model parameters\nthrough many layers, and local changes are combined backwards. In\n<strong>robotics<\/strong>, derivatives determine how a movement responds\nto changes in joint angles or control variables. In\n<strong>software<\/strong>, they help optimise objective functions or\nanalyse the sensitivities of a model. Automatic differentiation can\napply these rules systematically to a computational graph; however, it\ndoes not replace checking domains and modelling assumptions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S11\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The rules apply at points where the relevant functions are\n<strong>differentiable<\/strong>. For compositions, the outer function\nmust also be differentiable at the <strong>inner function\nvalue<\/strong>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">In the quotient rule the <strong>denominator must be\nnon-zero<\/strong>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The power rule must be restricted appropriately depending on the\nexponent and domain; the simple school formula with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndoes not permit arbitrary complex values.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Results for discrete or noisy data may only be useful as\napproximations. Mathematical differentiability of a model is not the\nsame as physical measurability.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Advanced-course scope (Leistungskurs):<\/strong> The\nselection and depth of individual combinations and methods here is a\nsubject-based presentation, not an officially verified list of\nrequirements for a particular Berlin Abitur year.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S12\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Link to the next unit<\/h2>\n<p>In <strong><a href=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L5<\/a> – Curve\nsketching and geometric properties<\/strong>, we use derivatives to study\nintervals of increase and decrease, horizontal tangents, maximum and\nminimum points, and points of inflection in an understandable way.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S13\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<p>No individual sources are listed separately for this lesson.<\/p>\n<\/article>","school_mathematics_en:M2-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M2-L5 – Curve sketching and geometric properties<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M2 – Upper secondary education / Abitur<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Upper secondary education / Abitur<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> No specific prerequisites\nstated.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Classification:<\/strong> Upper secondary education at a\nGymnasium · Calculus<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is this about?<\/h2>\n<p>A formula can describe the shape of a curve without our having to\ndraw it immediately. Using derivatives, we can identify where the curve\nrises or falls, where it has local maxima and minima, and where its\nconcavity changes. This investigation is called <strong>curve\nsketching<\/strong>. It answers not only the question of what a curve\nlooks like, but also which conclusions about a model are actually\njustified. We build on the concept of the derivative from <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L3<\/a> and the calculation rules from\nM2-L4.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Basic idea and intuition<\/h2>\n<p>Imagine travelling through a hilly landscape. The <strong>first\nderivative<\/strong> tells you whether the road runs uphill or downhill\nat a particular point. The <strong>second derivative<\/strong> describes\nhow this gradient changes along the horizontal coordinate. A local\nmaximum often occurs where the gradient changes from positive to\nnegative; a local minimum occurs at a change from negative to positive.\nA point of inflection marks a change in concavity, not necessarily a\nmaximum or minimum.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>New symbols before the first formula<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Symbol<\/th>\n<th>Read aloud<\/th>\n<th>Meaning here<\/th>\n<th>Other possible uses<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f&#39;(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘eff prime of x’<\/td>\n<td>First derivative; local gradient<\/td>\n<td>A prime can mark points and objects<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f<\/mi><mo>″<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f&#39;&#39;(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘eff double prime of x’, ‘second derivative’<\/td>\n<td>Rate of change of the first derivative<\/td>\n<td>Two primes do not indicate squaring<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f<\/mi><mo>‴<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f&#39;&#39;&#39;(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘eff triple prime of x’, ‘third derivative’<\/td>\n<td>Rate of change of the second derivative<\/td>\n<td>Not a triple product<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘x nought’<\/td>\n<td>Input value at which we check a property<\/td>\n<td>Initial value, index or reference point<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&gt;<\/code> and <code>&lt;<\/code><\/td>\n<td>‘greater than’, ‘less than’<\/td>\n<td>Sign of the derivative<\/td>\n<td>General comparisons<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>⇒<\/code><\/td>\n<td>‘it follows that’<\/td>\n<td>A sufficient condition leads to a conclusion<\/td>\n<td>General implication symbol in logic<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>m<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>b<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘em’, ‘bee’<\/td>\n<td>Gradient and intercept of a straight line<\/td>\n<td>Any other parameters<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘tee of x’<\/td>\n<td>Name of a tangent line<\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nalso often denotes time<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Monotonicity: where does the graph rise or fall?<\/h2>\n<p>The derivative measures the <strong>local gradient<\/strong>. If it is\npositive throughout an interval, the function value increases strictly\nthere as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nincreases. If it is negative throughout the interval, the function value\ndecreases strictly there. We consider a <strong>connected\ninterval<\/strong> on which the function is continuous and differentiable\nin its interior; the sign of the derivative must satisfy the stated\ncondition throughout the interior. A positive derivative at just one\npoint does not establish monotonicity along an entire stretch.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msup><mi>f<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>⟹<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mrow><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\"> increases on the interval under consideration.<\/mtext><\/mrow><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msup><mi>f<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>⟹<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mrow><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\"> decreases on the interval under consideration.<\/mtext><\/mrow><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\nf&#39;(x)&gt;0&amp;\\implies f\\text{ increases on the interval under consideration.}\\\\\nf&#39;(x)&lt;0&amp;\\implies f\\text{ decreases on the interval under consideration.}\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘If eff prime of x is greater than zero,\nthe function increases on the interval under consideration.’ The second\nline reads: ‘If eff prime of x is less than zero, the function decreases\non the interval under consideration.’ The derivative must exist in the\nrelevant interval and have the stated sign throughout it.<\/p>\n<p>A derivative that is non-negative everywhere allows us to say the\nfunction is <strong>monotonically non-decreasing<\/strong>. Even if the\nderivative is zero at individual points, the function as a whole may be\nstrictly increasing: the function\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nfor example, is strictly increasing, although its derivative vanishes at\nzero. Its derivative is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3x^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\nit is zero at zero and positive elsewhere. This also shows why\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f&#39;(x_0)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n<strong>does not by itself prove that there is an extremum<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Extremum points: identifying maxima and minima<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>local maximum<\/strong> is a function value that is at least\nas large as the values in its immediate neighbourhood. A <strong>local\nminimum<\/strong> is correspondingly no greater than those values. We\ncall the corresponding points extremum points.<\/p>\n<p>For an interior local extremum of a differentiable function, the\nfollowing is a <strong>necessary<\/strong> condition:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f&#39;(x_0)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘Eff prime at x nought equals zero.’ The\ntangent is horizontal there. Necessary means that this condition must\nhold at an interior extremum if the function is differentiable there. It\ndoes <strong>not<\/strong> mean that every zero of the derivative is\nautomatically an extremum.<\/p>\n<p>To decide, we consider the signs of the first derivative immediately\nto the left and right of the candidate input value:<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Sign change of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>f<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f&#39;<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/th>\n<th>Conclusion<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>positive → negative<\/td>\n<td>Local maximum point<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>negative → positive<\/td>\n<td>Local minimum point<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>no change<\/td>\n<td>Not an extremum for that reason alone<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>If a function is <strong>not differentiable<\/strong> at a possible\nextremum, there may still be an extremum:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=|x|<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas a local and global minimum at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nbut because of the sharp corner it has no derivative there (<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L3<\/a>). An often convenient\nadditional test uses the second derivative: if\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f&#39;(x_0)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f<\/mi><mo>″<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f&#39;&#39;(x_0)&lt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthere is a <strong>strict local maximum<\/strong> there; if\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f<\/mi><mo>″<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f&#39;&#39;(x_0)&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthere is a <strong>strict local minimum<\/strong>. If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f<\/mi><mo>″<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f&#39;&#39;(x_0)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthis test is inconclusive. It does not automatically establish an\nextremum or a point of inflection.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Points of inflection and concavity<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>point of inflection<\/strong> is a point at which a\nsufficiently smooth curve changes its concavity. In the usual German\nupper-secondary terminology, the change in sign of the second derivative\nis described as a change between ‘curving to the left’ and ‘curving to\nthe right’ (concave upwards and concave downwards).<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msup><mi>f<\/mi><mo>″<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>→<\/mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">The gradient increases there.<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msup><mi>f<\/mi><mo>″<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>→<\/mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">The gradient decreases there.<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\nf&#39;&#39;(x)&gt;0&amp;\\longrightarrow\\text{The gradient increases there.}\\\\\nf&#39;&#39;(x)&lt;0&amp;\\longrightarrow\\text{The gradient decreases there.}\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘If the second derivative is greater\nthan zero, the first derivative increases.’ If the sign is negative, it\ndecreases. The sign must be considered over an entire region, not just\nat one isolated point.<\/p>\n<p>For a function that is twice differentiable in a neighbourhood,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f<\/mi><mo>″<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f&#39;&#39;(x_0)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis a typical <strong>candidate<\/strong> for a point of inflection. What\nmatters is an actual <strong>change in concavity<\/strong>; a sign change\nof\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>f<\/mi><mo>″<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f&#39;&#39;<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nprovides a simple way of establishing it.\n<strong>Counterexample:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(x)=x^4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g<\/mi><mo>″<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g&#39;&#39;(x)=12x^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand therefore\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g<\/mi><mo>″<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g&#39;&#39;(0)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nbut the second derivative is positive on both sides: there is no point\nof inflection at zero. If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>f<\/mi><mo>″<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f&#39;&#39;<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nexists in a neighbourhood of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand is differentiable at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f<\/mi><mo>″<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f&#39;&#39;(x_0)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntogether with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f<\/mi><mo>‴<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≠<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f&#39;&#39;&#39;(x_0)\\ne0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis a <strong>sufficient test<\/strong>: the second derivative then\nchanges sign near that input value.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A worked mini-example from start to finish<\/h2>\n<p>We consider a simple cubic function. Cubic powers have already been\nintroduced, and the derivatives can be calculated using the power rule\nfrom <a href=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L4<\/a>:<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-mathematics-en/media/img/S_MAT_024_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Graph of the function f(x) equals x cubed minus three x, with a local maximum at minus one, two, a local minimum at one, minus two, and a point of inflection at the origin.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: The graph of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=x^3-3x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas a local maximum at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(-1,2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\na local minimum at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1,-2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand an inflection point at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(0,0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>Curve sketching:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H(-1,2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T(1,-2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>W<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W(0,0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msup><mi>f<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msup><mi>f<\/mi><mo>″<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\nf(x)&amp;=x^3-3x\\\\\nf^{\\prime}(x)&amp;=3x^2-3\\\\\nf&#39;&#39;(x)&amp;=6x\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘Eff of x equals x cubed minus three\ntimes x.’ The first derivative is ‘three x squared minus three’. The\nsecond derivative is ‘six x’.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Step 1 – horizontal tangents:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3x^2-3=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^2=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nso\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThese are only candidates for extremum points.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Step 2 – check:<\/strong> For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe second derivative is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nso there is a local maximum there. The function value is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(-1)=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe second derivative is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>6<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ngiving a local minimum with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(1)=-2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Step 3 – point of inflection:<\/strong> From\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f<\/mi><mo>″<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f&#39;&#39;(x)=6x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwe obtain the candidate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor negative\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>6<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis negative; for positive\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nit is positive. The concavity changes:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(0;0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis a point of inflection. The tangent here is not horizontal, since\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f&#39;(0)=-3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Step 4 – shape:<\/strong> The function increases for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x&lt;-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ndecreases for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-1&lt;x&lt;1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand increases again for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x&gt;1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis follows from the factorisation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3x^2-3=3(x-1)(x+1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\noutside the zeros, both factors have the same sign (positive product);\nbetween them, their product is negative. The points\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare the interval endpoints and belong to the extremum points.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Result:<\/strong> The formula can be translated into an\nunderstandable map: local maximum\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(-1;2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nlocal minimum\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1;-2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\npoint of inflection\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(0;0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the corresponding intervals of increase and decrease. Nevertheless,\nover the entire set of real numbers there is <strong>no global maximum\nor minimum<\/strong>, because the cubic function is unbounded above and\nbelow.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Reading the tangent equation<\/h2>\n<p>The tangent is the straight line that has the same gradient as a\ndifferentiable curve at the point under consideration. For its equation\nwe need the function value and the derivative at that point:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>f<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>⋅<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t(x)=f&#39;(x_0)\\cdot(x-x_0)+f(x_0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘Tee of x equals eff prime at x nought\ntimes x minus x nought, plus eff at x nought.’ The tangent gradient is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f&#39;(x_0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand the point of contact has coordinates\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>;<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(x_0;f(x_0))<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Mini-example:<\/strong> For our function at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_0=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe value is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(2)=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the gradient is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f&#39;(2)=9<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe tangent is therefore\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t(x)=9\\cdot(x-2)+2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nsimplified to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>16<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t(x)=9x-16<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nit gives the same value as the curve. The tangent is a <strong>linear\napproximation near<\/strong> this point, not a second representation of\nthe entire curve: for example, at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe straight line gives the value\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>11<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">11<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nbut the curve gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>18<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(3)=18<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Why is this useful?<\/h2>\n<p>In technical data analysis, a derivative reveals when a measured\nquantity increases or decreases; a point of inflection can indicate a\nchange in the behaviour of its growth rate. Local optima can be\nidentified in performance curves, for example for throughput or energy\nrequirements. In AI optimisation, derivatives help to determine the\ndirection of beneficial parameter changes. Curve sketching is an example\nof analysing <strong>one-dimensional, smooth models<\/strong>, not yet a\ngeneral algorithm for high-dimensional neural networks.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Statements about first and second derivatives require the\ncorresponding <strong>differentiability<\/strong>. Sharp corners, jumps\nor undefined points must be investigated separately.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f&#39;(x_0)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis necessary but not sufficient for interior extrema at differentiable\npoints. Similarly,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f<\/mi><mo>″<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f&#39;&#39;(x_0)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndoes not guarantee a point of inflection.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Local maxima and minima are not automatically the highest or lowest\nvalues of an entire domain. <strong>Endpoints<\/strong>, gaps in the\ndomain and limiting behaviour can be decisive; a curve investigation\ntherefore begins with the domain.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A polynomial is an idealised description. Real measurement curves\ncontain noise; numerical differentiation of them can be unstable.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S11\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Link to the next lesson<\/h2>\n<p><a href=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L6<\/a> uses exactly these tools\nto address a new question: <strong>How do we find the best permissible\nvalue of a quantity when there are constraints?<\/strong><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S12\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<p>No individual sources are listed separately for this lesson.<\/p>\n<\/article>","school_mathematics_en:M2-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M2-L6 – Optimisation using derivatives<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M2 – Upper secondary education / Abitur<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Upper secondary education / Abitur<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> No specific prerequisites\nstated.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Classification:<\/strong> Upper secondary education at a\nGymnasium · Calculus and modelling<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is this about?<\/h2>\n<p>Optimisation means finding the most favourable solution possible\nunder given conditions. Depending on the problem, we want to maximise\narea, profit or throughput, or minimise time, costs or energy\nconsumption. A practical challenge is first to translate a situation\ninto a mathematical function. Derivatives can then locate possible\noptimal values. However, the result must be checked against the\n<strong>permissible values and boundary conditions<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Basic idea and intuition<\/h2>\n<p>Imagine you have only a certain amount of material and want to\nenclose as large a rectangular area as possible. The length of one side\nnecessarily affects the other side. If you keep making one side longer,\nthe other will eventually become so short that the area shrinks again.\nThere is therefore a best compromise, which can be determined\nmathematically.<\/p>\n<p>The three components are called the <strong>objective\nquantity<\/strong>, <strong>decision variable<\/strong> and\n<strong>constraint<\/strong>. The objective quantity is described as a\nfunction of the decision variable; the constraint restricts the allowed\nvalues. This terminology is also used later in operations research and\nmachine learning.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>New symbols before the first formula<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Symbol<\/th>\n<th>Read aloud<\/th>\n<th>Meaning here<\/th>\n<th>Other possible uses<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘ay of x’<\/td>\n<td>Rectangular area depending on a side length x<\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmay be an event, matrix or point<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘x’<\/td>\n<td>Decision variable: first side length<\/td>\n<td>Otherwise a coordinate or function argument<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>y<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘y’<\/td>\n<td>Second side length<\/td>\n<td>Otherwise a function value<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[0;12]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘closed interval from zero to twelve’<\/td>\n<td>Permissible values including both endpoints<\/td>\n<td>Square brackets also for lists and matrices<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A&#39;(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘ay prime of x’<\/td>\n<td>First derivative of the objective function<\/td>\n<td>A prime has other uses<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A<\/mi><mo>″<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A&#39;&#39;(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘ay double prime of x’<\/td>\n<td>Second derivative; helps with local testing<\/td>\n<td>Not the square of the first derivative<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>max<\/code> and <code>min<\/code><\/td>\n<td>‘maximum’, ‘minimum’<\/td>\n<td>Largest or smallest permissible value<\/td>\n<td><code>max<\/code> also a function name in programs<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>≤<\/code>, <code>≥<\/code><\/td>\n<td>‘less than or equal to’, ‘greater than or equal to’<\/td>\n<td>Inequalities for constraints<\/td>\n<td>General comparisons<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From the problem to the objective function<\/h2>\n<p>A rectangular area is to be enclosed using a total of <strong>24\nmetres of boundary material<\/strong>. All four sides require material.\nOpposite sides are equal in length. The lengths must not be negative.\n<strong>Unit convention for the calculation:<\/strong> in the following\nexample,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>y<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndenote the <strong>numerical values<\/strong> of the side lengths in\nmetres, and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe numerical value of the area in square metres. The numbers 24, 12 and\n6 in the equations therefore always have a clear connection to the\nunits.<\/p>\n<p>The <strong>constraint<\/strong> is thus:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>24<\/mn><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">(<\/mtext><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">: numerical values of the lengths in metres)<\/mtext><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2x+2y=24\\qquad\\text{(}x,y\\text{: numerical values of the lengths in metres)}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘Two times x plus two times y equals\ntwenty-four.’\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>y<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare the numerical values of the two side lengths in metres, each\ncontributing twice to the perimeter. The perimeter is <strong>24\nm<\/strong>; hence the numerical value 24 on the right.<\/p>\n<p>We rearrange for the second length:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=12-x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe area of a rectangle is length times width. After substitution, just\nthe one independent variable remains:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A(x)=x\\cdot(12-x)=12x-x^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘The area ay of x equals x times twelve\nminus x, that is twelve x minus x squared.’ For a <strong>non-degenerate\nrectangle<\/strong>, both sides must be positive:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=12-x&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nso\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0&lt;x&lt;12<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nTo examine the <strong>boundary<\/strong> systematically, we include the\nlimiting cases\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=12<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin the calculation; both have zero area but do not represent real\nrectangles. Outside\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\le x\\le12<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\na negative side length would result.<\/p>\n<p>The actual area measures\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n<strong>square metres<\/strong>; here,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntherefore denotes its numerical value and is not itself a physical\nquantity with an attached unit. A calculated maximum without permissible\nside lengths would not be a valid solution.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Finding candidates for the optimum using a derivative<\/h2>\n<p>For the derivative, we use the familiar power rule:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A&#39;(x)=12-2x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘Ay prime of x equals twelve minus two\nx.’ The derivative describes how strongly the calculated area increases\nor decreases when\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nchanges slightly. As\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhere means only the numerical value of the area in square metres, and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nonly the numerical value of the length in metres,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A&#39;(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nitself is a derivative of numerical values. The <strong>physical rate of\nchange of area with respect to length<\/strong> has the unit\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{\\mathrm{m}^2}{\\mathrm{m}}=\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe numerical values agree for the chosen unit, the metre.<\/p>\n<p>We first look for a <strong>horizontal tangent<\/strong>, where the\nlocal rate of change is zero:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>12<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mo>⇒<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">12-2x=0\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad x=6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘Twelve minus two x equals zero; it\nfollows that x equals six.’ Then\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=12-6=6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA square is thus a candidate for the largest area.<\/p>\n<p>That is not sufficient on its own. We check using the second\nderivative and the boundary values:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msup><mi>A<\/mi><mo>″<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>36<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\nA&#39;&#39;(x)&amp;=-2\\\\\nA(0)&amp;=0,\\quad A(6)=36,\\quad A(12)=0\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘The second derivative is minus two.’ It\nis negative everywhere: the function is concave downwards on the\ninterval, and at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthere is a strict local maximum. The fact that the value of <strong>36\nsquare metres<\/strong> is also the <strong>global<\/strong> maximum for\nall real rectangles with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0&lt;x&lt;12<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncan even be demonstrated exactly rather than just graphically. We\ncomplete the square:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>36<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A(x)=12x-x^2=36-{(x-6)}^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA square is never negative, so this expression is <strong>at most\n36<\/strong>, and is exactly 36 when\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe zero areas at the <strong>endpoints included only for calculation\npurposes<\/strong> are correspondingly smaller. The boundary values are\ncomparison and limiting values, not rectangles in their own right.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Result:<\/strong> With a perimeter of 24 metres, the rectangle\nhas its greatest area at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mo>×<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6\\,\\mathrm{m}\\times6\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nnamely\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>36<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">36\\,\\mathrm{m}^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis is a <strong>model result<\/strong>, not an instruction for every\nreal plot of land.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The steps of a simple optimisation<\/h2>\n<p>The same sequence of reasoning helps with other problems too:<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Set the objective:<\/strong> What should be as large or small\nas possible – costs, time, yield, area?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Determine the variable:<\/strong> What can we actually\nchange?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Formulate constraints:<\/strong> Which resources, limits,\nrelationships or safety requirements are binding?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Set up the objective function:<\/strong> How does the\nobjective quantity depend on the variable quantity?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">5.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Identify candidates:<\/strong> Set the derivative equal to\nzero; also consider points where there is no derivative and permissible\n<strong>endpoints<\/strong>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">6.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Compare candidates:<\/strong> Only allowed values can provide\npossible solutions. For global optima, the limits of an unbounded domain\nalso matter where relevant.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>This sequence also corresponds to the modelling of software problems:\nonly after we clarify <strong>what<\/strong> should be improved and\n<strong>which rules<\/strong> apply can we choose a suitable\nalgorithm.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Local and global optima<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>local maximum<\/strong> is largest only within a small\nneighbourhood. At an endpoint, such neighbourhoods refer to\n<strong>permissible<\/strong> values; global optima may also occur there.\nExample:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\non the closed interval\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[0;2]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas the global maximum\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(2)=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat an endpoint, even though\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f&#39;(x)=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthroughout the interior and is <strong>nowhere zero<\/strong>. A\n<strong>global maximum<\/strong> is largest over the entire permissible\ndomain. A derivative becoming zero initially identifies only candidates.\nA local peak may be lower than another peak or even an allowed boundary\nvalue.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-mathematics-en/media/img/S_MAT_025_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Local and global extrema of a function on an interval\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: A curve may have several local extrema; on the\nclosed interval shown, the global maximum is at the right\nendpoint.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>On a <strong>closed and bounded interval<\/strong>, every continuous\nreal-valued function attains a largest and a smallest value. Open\nintervals or unbounded domains behave differently. For instance, the\nfunction\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\non the open set\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0&lt;x&lt;1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas no largest function value, although its values approach one\narbitrarily closely.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Read the restriction\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0&lt;x&lt;1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\naloud:<\/strong> ‘X is greater than zero and less than one.’ The\nendpoints do not belong to the permissible domain. A limiting value that\ncan only be approached need not be an actually attainable optimum.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Technical application: an ideal solution cannot always be\nimplemented<\/h2>\n<p>Consider running several software jobs in parallel. A theoretical\nobjective function might use a real variable for computing capacity. In\nreality, however, the number of processes started, for example, can\noften be chosen only as a whole number. A calculated solution of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6.4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=6.4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nprocesses would then not be permissible at all. Instead, we must check\nthe allowed whole numbers nearby – and possibly further permissible\nvalues if the model is complicated.<\/p>\n<p>In AI systems, the <strong>loss function<\/strong> to be minimised may\ndepend on millions of parameters. There, methods such as gradient\ndescent replace simple curve sketching. The basic idea remains related:\ncalculate changes, look for a favourable step and respect constraints.\nThese methods are studied later in university-level optimisation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Why is this useful?<\/h2>\n<p>An optimisation model helps compare costs and capacities, for example\nwhen allocating server resources to several tasks. When planning\ntechnical systems, we can look for the best possible geometry or setting\nwithin a material or energy limit. AI control uses similar concepts of\nobjectives and constraints, although often in many more dimensions. Not\nevery real problem can be represented by a smooth function of a single\nvariable.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The rectangle calculation considers only a fixed perimeter, plane\ngeometry and freely selectable side lengths; building regulations,\ndoors, wall thicknesses and material losses are omitted.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=12<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare merely limiting cases of the calculation model, <strong>not\npermissible real rectangles<\/strong>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A&#39;(x)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nprovides <strong>candidates<\/strong>, not a guarantee. Optima may occur\nat non-differentiable points and permissible boundary values.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A local optimum need not be globally optimal; this distinction\nbecomes especially important with several variables.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Continuous variables must not be equated with discrete decisions.\nPrograms also need tolerances for numerical calculation errors.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S11\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Link to the next lesson<\/h2>\n<p>So far, we have determined the <strong>instantaneous change<\/strong>\nfrom a function. <a href=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L7<\/a> reverses\nthe direction: how can we reconstruct an original function from a known\nrate of change?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S12\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<p>No individual sources are listed separately for this lesson.<\/p>\n<\/article>","school_mathematics_en:M2-L7":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L7\">M2-L7 – Antiderivatives and integral notation<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M2 – Upper secondary education / Abitur<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Upper secondary education / Abitur<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> No specific prerequisites\nstated.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Classification:<\/strong> Upper secondary education at a\nGymnasium · Introduction to integral calculus<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is this about?<\/h2>\n<p>Using a derivative, we determine how a quantity is changing at a\ngiven instant. Often, however, we know only this change and want to\nreconstruct the original quantity from it. Such a calculation in reverse\nleads to the concept of the <strong>antiderivative<\/strong>. The\nintegral sign allows us to write the entire family of antiderivatives\ncompactly. This is the starting point for integral calculus, which in\nthe next lessons also describes accumulated changes and signed\nareas.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Basic idea and intuition<\/h2>\n<p>Suppose we know the velocity of a moving object at every instant. The\nderivative of a <strong>position function along a straight axis<\/strong>\ngives the <strong>signed velocity<\/strong>; conversely, we can recover\nposition as a function of time from it. However, knowing the velocity\ndoes not yet tell us <strong>where<\/strong> the object was initially.\nVelocity differs from <em>speed<\/em>, which is always non-negative: when\nthe object turns back, its displacement can be negative even though\nfurther distance is travelled. The result therefore contains an\ninitially free additive constant that is later determined by an initial\ncondition.<\/p>\n<p>The transition from a <strong>rate of change<\/strong> to a\n<strong>stored quantity<\/strong> is also useful in software models: a\n<strong>net rate of change<\/strong> from inflow and outflow describes,\nfor example, the change in the length of a queue, but not its initial\nlength. In the real queue model the quantity must not become negative; a\nsolution reconstructed mathematically must therefore be adapted to the\nlimits of the model if necessary. The definite integral – the actual\naccumulated change over an interval – is covered in detail only in\nM2-L8.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>New symbols before the first formula<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Symbol<\/th>\n<th>Read aloud<\/th>\n<th>Meaning here<\/th>\n<th>Other possible uses<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘capital eff of x’<\/td>\n<td>Antiderivative of the function\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>Capital letters may denote sets, matrices or other functions<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>F<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F&#39;(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘capital eff prime of x’<\/td>\n<td>Derivative of the antiderivative<\/td>\n<td>The derivative is familiar from <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L3<\/a>/M2-L4<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘lower-case eff of x’<\/td>\n<td>Given function whose antiderivative is sought<\/td>\n<td>Any other function rule<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>∫<\/mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘integral sign’, ‘integral of’<\/td>\n<td>Here indicates indefinite integration<\/td>\n<td>With limits it later denotes a definite integral<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{d}x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘dee x’<\/td>\n<td>Identifies the integration variable x<\/td>\n<td>A separate, context-dependent role in differential notation<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>C<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘capital see’<\/td>\n<td>Freely selectable <strong>constant of integration<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>Otherwise any constant or name of an object<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>∈<\/code><\/td>\n<td>‘is an element of’<\/td>\n<td>Membership of a set<\/td>\n<td>General set theory, studied in more depth later<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℝ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘set of real numbers’<\/td>\n<td>Real number system<\/td>\n<td>Already introduced in <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L1<\/a><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘en’<\/td>\n<td>Fixed exponent in the power rule<\/td>\n<td>Often also a step count or index<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘kay’<\/td>\n<td>Any constant numerical value<\/td>\n<td>In other problems a running index<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Absolute value notation<\/td>\n<td>‘absolute value of x’<\/td>\n<td>Non-negative distance from zero; here in the logarithm for both\nsigns<\/td>\n<td>Norms and other absolute value notation too<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Antiderivatives: thinking of differentiation in reverse<\/h2>\n<p>A function\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>F<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis called an antiderivative of the function\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\non an interval if\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>F<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis differentiable there and its derivative at every interior point of\nthe interval equals\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(at included endpoints, suitable one-sided derivatives are\nconsidered).<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>F<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F&#39;(x)=f(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘The derivative of capital eff at x\nequals lower-case eff of x.’ This equation defines what is meant by an\n<strong>antiderivative<\/strong>. Unlike differentiation, the reverse\nprocess can give several different functions with the same\nderivative.<\/p>\n<p>For example, the functions\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^2+5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^2-100<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nall have the same derivative\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAny constant shift disappears during differentiation. A constant term is\ntherefore needed when calculating in reverse.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The indefinite integral<\/h2>\n<p>We use compact notation for the family of all antiderivatives:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∫<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int f(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x=F(x)+C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read in full:<\/strong> ‘The indefinite integral of lower-case\neff of x with respect to x equals capital eff of x plus the constant of\nintegration see.’<\/p>\n<p>The individual components:<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>∫<\/mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\nan elongated S, historically connected with the idea of summation.\n<strong>Here<\/strong> it indicates finding antiderivatives.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\nthe <strong>integrand<\/strong>, the expression we want to\nintegrate.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{d}x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\nindicates the variable <strong>with respect to which<\/strong> we\nintegrate. In this introduction, we should not simply treat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{d}x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nas an arbitrarily tiny number by which we multiply.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\na chosen antiderivative whose derivative gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>+<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\nthe free additive constant. On a connected interval, all antiderivatives\nof the same function differ only by a constant.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Important:<\/strong> In this representation, the indefinite\nintegral is a <strong>family of functions<\/strong>, not a single\nnumerical area. We discuss the interpretation as area and net change\nwith <strong>limits of integration<\/strong> in M2-L8.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A fully explained mini-example<\/h2>\n<p>We want to find an antiderivative of the function\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=2x+3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nTo do so, we recall two derivatives: the derivative of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand the derivative of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>3<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-mathematics-en/media/img/S_MAT_026_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Graph of the function f(x) equals two x plus three, with positive area under the graph from zero to two, of size ten.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: The area under\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=2x+3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbetween\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nequals 10 square units.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>For the antiderivative\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F(x)=x^2+3x+C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\nthe net change from 0 to 2 is 10.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mo>∫<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>C<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\nf(x)&amp;=2x+3\\\\\n\\int(2x+3)\\,\\mathrm{d}x&amp;=x^2+3x+C\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘Eff of x equals two x plus three. The\nindefinite integral of two x plus three with respect to x equals x\nsquared plus three x plus see.’<\/p>\n<p>Why is this correct? If we differentiate the function we found,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F(x)=x^2+3x+C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe obtain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2x+3+0=2x+3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe constant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>C<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndisappears during differentiation. Thus each of these functions\nsatisfies the condition for an antiderivative.<\/p>\n<p>Now an <strong>initial condition<\/strong> might be added: the\noriginal stored quantity is exactly five units at input\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWe read this as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F(0)=5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand substitute:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>0<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>C<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0^2+3\\cdot0+C=5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nHence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C=5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe antiderivative determined by this condition is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F(x)=x^2+3x+5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Understanding simple integration rules<\/h2>\n<p>We can recognise many standard functions by using the familiar\ndifferentiation rules in reverse:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mo>∫<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>+<\/mo><mi>C<\/mi><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>≠<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">suitable domain<\/mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mo>∫<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>C<\/mi><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mrow><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\"> is a constant<\/mtext><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n\\int x^n\\,\\mathrm{d}x&amp;=\\frac{x^{n+1}}{n+1}+C\\quad(n\\neq-1;\\ \\text{suitable domain})\\\\\n\\int k\\,\\mathrm{d}x&amp;=kx+C\\quad(k\\text{ is a constant})\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>First formula read aloud:<\/strong> ‘The integral of x to the\npower en with respect to x equals x to the power en plus one divided by\nen plus one, plus see.’ The exponent\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis fixed and must not be minus one for this formula. For <strong>every\nreal exponent\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>≠<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n\\ne-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/strong>,\nthe rule is valid on the interval\n<strong><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/strong>.\nFor integer exponents it is also valid on larger suitable domains, for\nexample for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\non all of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℝ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nor for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=-2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\non any interval not containing zero. Division by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n+1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrequires this value to be non-zero.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Second formula read aloud:<\/strong> ‘The integral of the\nconstant number kay with respect to x equals kay times x plus see.’ If\nthe rate of change always stays the same, the reconstructed quantity\nvaries linearly.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Mini-example:<\/strong> For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∫<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int x^2\\,\\mathrm{d}x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nincrease the exponent two to three and divide by three:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><mn>3<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>+<\/mo><mi>C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{x^3}{3}+C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nChecking by differentiation gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nagain.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"abschnitt-8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The important exception for an exponent of minus\none<\/h3>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><\/mfrac><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^{-1}=\\frac1x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe general power rule just given would require division by zero. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe following applies instead:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∫<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><\/mfrac><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>C<\/mi><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int\\frac1x\\,\\mathrm{d}x=\\ln(x)+C\\qquad(x&gt;0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘The integral of one over x with respect\nto x equals the natural logarithm of x plus see.’ We have already\nencountered\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ln<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwhen studying logarithms. For negative\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\non the separate interval we obtain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ln(-x)+C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncorrespondingly; in summary, on an interval not containing zero, we can\nwrite the formula\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ln|x|+C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nHere the absolute value bars mean that the value between them is\nconsidered without its sign. Zero itself remains excluded. On the two\nseparate intervals\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x&lt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe may choose <strong>different additive constants<\/strong> for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∫<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><\/mfrac><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int\\frac1x\\,\\mathrm{d}x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nonly within a connected interval is the difference between two\nantiderivatives constant.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The connection with accumulation<\/h2>\n<p>Why is the new calculation step called <strong>integration<\/strong>?\nIn a model involving time, a rate of change can be considered for many\nsmall time intervals. Adding the respective small changes over\nincreasingly fine intervals leads to the idea of a <strong>definite\nintegral<\/strong>. The antiderivative then provides an elegant way to\ncalculate this accumulated result.<\/p>\n<p>We should distinguish two statements clearly:<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Question<\/th>\n<th>Object sought<\/th>\n<th>Covered in detail in<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>‘Which function has this derivative?’<\/td>\n<td>Family of antiderivatives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F(x)+C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td><a href=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L7<\/a><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>‘How much has changed in total between two times?’<\/td>\n<td>A number with an appropriate unit<\/td>\n<td><a href=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L8&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L8<\/a><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>‘Why can I calculate an accumulated change using an\nantiderivative?’<\/td>\n<td>Fundamental theorem of calculus<\/td>\n<td><a href=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L9&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L9<\/a><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Why is this useful?<\/h2>\n<p>In physical simulations we can reconstruct how position varies from a\n<strong>signed velocity function<\/strong> once an initial position is\nknown. By contrast, the <strong>total distance travelled<\/strong> is the\naccumulated magnitude of the velocity: an outward and return journey can\nhave zero net displacement and still cover a distance. In modelling\nenergy and consumption, time-dependent rates lead to total quantities.\nIn software metrics, the idea can explain how throughput data produce a\nrecord of how a stored quantity varies. Derivatives are commonplace in\nneural networks; thinking in the reverse mathematical direction is more\ncommon in continuous models, probability theory and dynamical\nsystems.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∫<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int f(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n<strong>here<\/strong> denotes an indefinite integral, a family of\nantiderivatives; without limits of integration, we are not yet\ncalculating a specific signed area.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A continuous function on an interval has an antiderivative. However,\nan antiderivative cannot always be expressed as a simple combination of\nfamiliar elementary functions.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The constant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>C<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncan be chosen freely on a <strong>connected interval<\/strong>. If a\ndomain consists of separate intervals, the constants on them can be\nchosen independently.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Initial conditions must fit the function model. Real measurement\ndata also involve measurement errors, rounding errors and uncertainty\nabout the rate of change.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The symbol\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{d}x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nidentifies the integration variable in this lesson. Its more precise\nmeaning is explained in greater depth at university.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S11\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Link to the next lesson<\/h2>\n<p>In <strong><a href=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L8&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L8<\/a><\/strong>, we\nadd limits to the integral sign and for the first time directly answer\nthe question: <strong>How large is the accumulated change between two\nspecific input values?<\/strong><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S12\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<p>No individual sources are listed separately for this lesson.<\/p>\n<\/article>","school_mathematics_en:M2-L8":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L8\">M2-L8 – Definite integrals and signed area<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M2 – Upper secondary education / Abitur<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Upper secondary education / Abitur<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> No specific prerequisites\nstated.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Classification:<\/strong> Upper secondary education at a\nGymnasium · Integral calculus<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is this about?<\/h2>\n<p>A rate of change tells us how quickly something changes <strong>at a\nparticular instant<\/strong>. Often, however, we are interested in the\n<strong>total change over a period of time<\/strong>. For example, we\nmight know how many parcels a machine processes per hour and ask how\nmany were processed during an entire shift. The definite integral\ncombines very many small contributions into an overall net change.\nUnlike the indefinite integral from <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L7<\/a>, it generally gives <strong>a\nnumerical value<\/strong> for fixed limits, not a whole family of\nantiderivatives.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Basic idea and intuition<\/h2>\n<p>Imagine a curve showing the rate of inflow or outflow at each\ninstant. If we split time into small intervals, we can approximate each\ninterval by a rectangle: <strong>rate times duration<\/strong> gives a\ncontribution to the stored quantity. Many such contributions give an\napproximation to the total change. As the time intervals become finer, a\nlimit is obtained under suitable conditions: the definite integral.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-mathematics-en/media/img/S_MAT_027_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Straight line f(x) = x − 1 on the interval from 0 to 3. Red area −0.5 below the x-axis; green area +2 above it; signed total +1.5.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: The definite integral adds signed areas:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.5<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-0.5+2=1.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nover the interval\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[0,3]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>Original illustration of the signed area for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=x-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\non [0,3]:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.5<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-0.5+2=1.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>Graphically, this looks like an area between the curve and the\nhorizontal axis. The crucial point, however, is the sign: above the axis\nthe area counts as <strong>positive<\/strong>, below it as\n<strong>negative<\/strong>. The result is therefore called the\n<em>oriented area<\/em> or <em>signed area<\/em>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>New symbols before the first formula<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Symbol<\/th>\n<th>Read aloud<\/th>\n<th>Meaning here<\/th>\n<th>Other possible uses<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>∫<\/mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘integral of’<\/td>\n<td>Summing contributions distributed over infinitely fine intervals;\nalready introduced in <a href=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L7<\/a><\/td>\n<td>Without limits: family of antiderivatives<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘ay’<\/td>\n<td>Lower limit of integration / starting point<\/td>\n<td>General number or parameter<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>b<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘bee’<\/td>\n<td>Upper limit of integration / endpoint<\/td>\n<td>Function parameter or other number<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msubsup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int_a^b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘integral from a to b’<\/td>\n<td>Definite integral with a specified interval<\/td>\n<td>In LaTeX: <code>int_a^b<\/code><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘eff of x’<\/td>\n<td>Function to be summed, e.g. a rate<\/td>\n<td>General function rule<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{d}x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘dee x’<\/td>\n<td>Integration is with respect to the variable x<\/td>\n<td>Differential notation, made more precise later<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F(b)-F(a)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘capital eff of b minus capital eff of a’<\/td>\n<td>Difference between the values of an antiderivative at the\nlimits<\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>F<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmay otherwise be another function<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>∑<\/mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sum<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘capital sigma’, ‘sum’<\/td>\n<td>Sign for adding many contributions; introduced here in anticipation\nof later work<\/td>\n<td>General summation sign in statistics and computer science<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘delta x’<\/td>\n<td>Width of a small interval<\/td>\n<td>Change or distance between two x-values; familiar from <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L8&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L8<\/a><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>An integral with upper and lower limits<\/h2>\n<p>The <strong>lower limit<\/strong> is written at the bottom of the\nintegral sign and the <strong>upper limit<\/strong> at the top. The\nintegrand is written between the integral sign and the differential\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{d}x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msubsup><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n&amp;\\int_a^b f(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read in full:<\/strong> ‘The definite integral of lower-case\neff of x with respect to x, from the lower limit a to the upper limit\nb.’<\/p>\n<p>Here\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrepresents the height or rate of change at the relevant point;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{d}x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nidentifies the variable and reminds us that contributions are summed\nalong its axis.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>b<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndelimit exactly the interval being considered. For a <strong>continuous\nfunction on the closed interval<\/strong> between\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>b<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthis integral (the Riemann integral) exists. Many functions with\nisolated jump discontinuities are also integrable; continuity is\n<strong>sufficient but not always necessary<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Important difference from <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L7<\/a>:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∫<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int f(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwithout limits denotes a family of antiderivatives with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>+<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msubsup><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int_a^b f(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith limits is a net numerical result – with the corresponding units in\na correctly formulated mathematical model. No arbitrary constant of\nintegration appears in the result of the definite integral.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>How calculation using an antiderivative works<\/h2>\n<p>If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>F<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis an antiderivative of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\non the interval under consideration, then for a continuous\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msubsup><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int_a^b f(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x=F(b)-F(a)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read in full:<\/strong> ‘The definite integral of f of x with\nrespect to x between a and b equals the value of the antiderivative\ncapital F at the upper limit b minus its value at the lower limit\na.’<\/p>\n<p>The <strong>roles of the symbols<\/strong>:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>∫<\/mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsums the contributions;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>b<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndefine the interval;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the integrand;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{d}x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nspecifies the variable of integration;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>F<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis any antiderivative with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>F<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F&#39;(x)=f(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\n<code>−<\/code> forms the difference between the end and the beginning.\nThe constant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>C<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndisappears automatically because it would occur at both the end and the\nbeginning and cancel out.<\/p>\n<p>This calculation rule is part of the <strong>fundamental theorem of\ncalculus<\/strong>, which <a href=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L9&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L9<\/a>\nexplains in more detail. Here we use it in practice first.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Fully explained mini-example: positive and negative\ncontributions<\/h2>\n<p>Suppose a simplified rate of change is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>t<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(t)=2t-2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe variable\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nstands for the <strong>numerical value<\/strong> of time in hours, and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor the <strong>numerical value<\/strong> of the net rate of change in\nitems per hour. The number\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>2<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbefore\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbelongs to the gradient, measured in items per hour squared; the\nconstant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbelongs to the rate, measured in items per hour. The formula is an\nequation between numerical values for these specified units. We consider\nthe interval from 0 to 3 hours.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>t<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>F<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>t<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>t<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mn>3<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>t<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\nf(t)&amp;=2t-2\\\\\nF(t)&amp;=t^2-2t\\\\\n\\int_0^3(2t-2)\\,\\mathrm{d}t&amp;=F(3)-F(0)\\\\\n&amp;=(9-6)-(0-0)\\\\\n&amp;=3\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘The integral of two times tee minus two\nwith respect to tee, from zero to three, is three.’ We have found an\nantiderivative, substituted the upper limit and then subtracted the\nvalue at the lower limit. The result represents a <strong>net increase\nof three items<\/strong> over three hours.<\/p>\n<p>Why is this not simply the total geometrical area? Initially,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(0)=-2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\nthe rate is negative. At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nit is zero, and afterwards it is positive. The negative contribution\nbetween 0 and 1 is <strong>−1 item<\/strong>; the positive contribution\nbetween 1 and 3 is <strong>+4 items<\/strong>. Together these give the\nnet change\n<strong><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">items<\/mtext><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-1+4=3\\,\\text{items}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/strong>.\nBy contrast, the accumulated <strong>magnitude of the change<\/strong>\nover the whole interval is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1+4=5\\,\\mathrm{items}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn the appropriately scaled time–rate diagram, this corresponds to the\nsum of the <strong>unsigned component areas<\/strong>. The numerical\nresult is the same, but its physical unit comes from ‘rate times time’,\nwhich here gives items.<\/p>\n<p><strong>An important distinction:<\/strong> A negative rate of change\nmay mean a <strong>decrease<\/strong> in the stored quantity without the\nquantity itself being negative.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Geometrical area and signed area are not the same<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Question<\/th>\n<th>What is counted?<\/th>\n<th>Result in the example<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>‘What is the <strong>net change<\/strong>?’<\/td>\n<td>Positive contributions minus the magnitudes of negative\ncontributions<\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>3<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nitems<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>‘What is the <strong>sum of the non-negative component\nareas<\/strong>?’<\/td>\n<td>All contributions counted positively, splitting at zeros if\nnecessary<\/td>\n<td>Numerical value\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>5<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nin the rate model\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>5<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nitems<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>‘How far has something <strong>travelled in total<\/strong>,\nregardless of direction?’<\/td>\n<td>In motion models, often the integral of the magnitude of\nvelocity<\/td>\n<td>Not the same as displacement<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>For the geometrical area, we can integrate the <strong>absolute\nvalue<\/strong> of a suitable integrable function on a <strong>finite\ninterval<\/strong>. The notation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|f(x)|<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmeans ‘absolute value of f of x’; the vertical bars turn negative\nfunction values into positive ones. This is the right answer only when\n<strong>all parts of the area are to be counted positively<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Reversing the limits and splitting the interval<\/h2>\n<p>Two practical rules are:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msubsup><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msubsup><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msubsup><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msubsup><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msubsup><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_a^b f(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x&amp;=-\\int_b^a f(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\\n\\int_a^c f(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\int_c^b f(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x&amp;=\\int_a^b f(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>First rule read aloud:<\/strong> ‘The integral from a to b is\nthe negative of the integral from b to a.’ Reversing the direction\nreverses the sign.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Second rule read aloud:<\/strong> ‘The net change from a to c\nplus the net change from c to b gives the net change from a to b.’ The\nnew intermediate point\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>c<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(pronounced ‘see’) splits the interval. In linear plain-text notation\n<code>∫_a^c<\/code>, the underscore identifies the lower limit and the\ncaret the upper limit.<\/p>\n<p>In the example, we can use this to examine the change of sign at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nseparately. The intermediate point need not lie between a and b for\nalgebraic additivity to hold; for an intuitive division of a time\ninterval, we usually choose it within that interval.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From rectangle approximations to the limit<\/h2>\n<p>A rough calculation might use a representative rate for each time\ninterval, multiply it by the duration of the interval and add all the\ncontributions. For sufficiently regular functions, the approximation\nbecomes more accurate as the rectangles become narrower.\n<strong>Concrete check:<\/strong> In our linear example, three rectangles\nof one hour each, using the <strong>left-hand<\/strong> endpoint value in\neach case, give the approximation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-2+0+2=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nitems; six rectangles of half an hour each give\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>1.5<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nitems. The exact net change is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>3<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nitems. A finer division improves the left-hand approximation here; for\nother functions, it need not improve monotonically at every intermediate\nstep. Mathematical notation often uses a capital sigma, the summation\nsign\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>∑<\/mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sum<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand a limiting process. We will study the complete construction of such\n<em>Riemann sums<\/em> later at university.<\/p>\n<p>This is the bridge between a textbook formula and a practical\nnumerical algorithm: computer programs can approximate the integral\nusing discrete values even when no simple antiderivative is known.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is it useful for?<\/h2>\n<p>In computer science, the total data transmitted can be calculated\nfrom a data rate that varies over time. In a simulation, integrating\nsigned velocity gives displacement; the magnitude may be needed to\nobtain the total distance travelled. In AI systems and sensors,\nintegrals help summarise signals that vary over time. For cost or energy\nprofiles, a rate per unit time gives a total quantity.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S11\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The result is a <strong>net value<\/strong>, not automatically a\npositive area.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The limits, units and sign must match the real model. ‘Items per\nhour’ multiplied by ‘hours’ gives items.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F(b)-F(a)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrequires an appropriate antiderivative on the interval; if no simple\nclosed-form expression exists, numerical methods are possible.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">When measurements are available only at individual points,\nassumptions about behaviour <strong>between<\/strong> the measurement\npoints are needed. Integral calculus cannot determine a unique area from\nsuch points alone.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">If the function is not properly integrable on the entire interval,\nthe case must be examined separately; in particular, unbounded functions\nor infinite intervals later lead to <strong>improper\nintegrals<\/strong>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S12\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Connection to the next lesson<\/h2>\n<p><strong><a href=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L9&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L9<\/a><\/strong>\nexplains why accumulation and differentiation are so closely related:\nthe fundamental theorem of calculus connects the two ideas precisely and\nshows how rates of change give accumulated quantities.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S13\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<p>No individual sources are listed separately for this lesson.<\/p>\n<\/article>","school_mathematics_en:M2-L9":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L9\">M2-L9 – Fundamental theorem of calculus, growth and accumulated quantities<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M2 – Upper secondary education / Abitur<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Upper secondary education / Abitur<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> No specific prerequisites\nstated.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Classification:<\/strong> Upper secondary education at a\nGymnasium · Integral calculus and modelling<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is this about?<\/h2>\n<p>Differential calculus answers the question of <strong>how quickly a\nquantity is changing at that instant<\/strong>. Integral calculus answers\nthe question of <strong>how these changes accumulate over a period of\ntime<\/strong>. The two methods are so closely connected that, under\nsuitable conditions, they are in a sense reverse processes. This is\nexpressed by the fundamental theorem of calculus. It enables many\nintegrals to be calculated using antiderivatives and forms the basis of\nmodels of motion, growth, decay and accumulated quantities.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Basic idea and intuition<\/h2>\n<p>If we have a curve representing an accumulated quantity, we can\nexamine its <strong>gradient<\/strong>: this is its instantaneous rate of\nchange. Conversely, if we know only the rate of change, we can\n<strong>sum all the small changes<\/strong> to determine the difference\nbetween the beginning and the end. The initial quantity is additional\ninformation: the same rate of change could correspond to very different\ncurves of absolute quantities.<\/p>\n<p>Example: A machine processes files sometimes faster and sometimes\nmore slowly. The <strong>processing rate<\/strong> is not the\n<strong>number of files already processed<\/strong>. From the rate over\ntime we can determine the increase in the number. How many files had\nbeen completed beforehand must be known separately.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>New symbols before the first formula<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Symbol<\/th>\n<th>Read aloud<\/th>\n<th>Meaning here<\/th>\n<th>Other possible uses<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘capital gee of x’<\/td>\n<td>Accumulated change from a fixed lower limit to x<\/td>\n<td>Any other function<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>G<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G&#39;(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘capital gee prime of x’<\/td>\n<td>Instantaneous change in this accumulated quantity<\/td>\n<td>Derivative notation from <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L3<\/a><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msubsup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int_a^x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘integral from a to x’<\/td>\n<td>Fixed lower limit, variable upper limit<\/td>\n<td>Limits may also be numbers or other variables<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘tee’<\/td>\n<td>Time variable in an integral<\/td>\n<td>General running variable, also a parameter<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v(t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘vee of tee’<\/td>\n<td>Velocity at time t<\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>v<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncan generally denote a rate or a vector<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s(t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘ess of tee’<\/td>\n<td>Position or coordinate along the path at time t<\/td>\n<td>Later also arc length<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘ess nought’<\/td>\n<td>Initial position at time zero<\/td>\n<td>A subscript zero otherwise marks a starting value<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>e<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘Euler’s number’, ‘ee’<\/td>\n<td>Base of the natural exponential function from <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L19&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L19<\/a><\/td>\n<td>Other constants in other subjects<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘kay’<\/td>\n<td>Constant growth or decay parameter<\/td>\n<td>General constant<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>≈<\/mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\approx<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘is approximately equal to’<\/td>\n<td>Rounded numerical value<\/td>\n<td>Approximation relation in general<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The first connection: derivative of the accumulated\nchange<\/h2>\n<p>Suppose a continuous function\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndescribes a rate over time. For each endpoint\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe consider the accumulated change since a fixed starting point\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>G<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msubsup><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msup><mi>G<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\nG(x)&amp;=\\int_a^x f(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\nG^{\\prime}(x)&amp;=f(x)\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>First line read aloud:<\/strong> ‘Capital gee of x is the\nintegral of lower-case eff of tee with respect to tee, from a to x.’<\/p>\n<p><strong>Second line read aloud:<\/strong> ‘The derivative of capital\ngee at x equals lower-case eff of x.’<\/p>\n<p>Here\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis simply a <strong>variable of integration or running\nvariable<\/strong>. It identifies the points over which the contributions\nare summed.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the <strong>variable upper limit<\/strong>; the function\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>G<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntherefore specifies how much has changed in total from the fixed limit\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto the current point\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>Intuitively, if we extend the time interval under consideration by a\nsmall amount, the additional change is approximately <strong>rate at the\nend times additional time<\/strong>. That is why the instantaneous rate\nof change of the accumulated quantity is the original function again. We\nassume here that\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis continuous on the interval under consideration. The equation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>G<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G&#39;(x)=f(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nholds at <strong>interior points<\/strong>; at the endpoints of the\ninterval we refer to one-sided derivatives where appropriate. Continuity\nat the point in question is the sufficient condition used here.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The second connection: an antiderivative at two limits<\/h2>\n<p>If you know an antiderivative\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>F<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nof\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nyou can calculate the total change from two function values:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msubsup><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int_a^b f(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x=F(b)-F(a)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read in full:<\/strong> ‘The integral of lower-case eff of x\nwith respect to x from a to b equals capital eff at b minus capital eff\nat a.’<\/p>\n<p>Why is this a reverse process? Because the derivative of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>F<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nequals\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nProvided that\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis continuous on the interval between the limits and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>F<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis an antiderivative there, the sum of all the small changes equals the\n<strong>difference between the corresponding accumulated\nquantities<\/strong>. As in <a href=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L8&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L8<\/a>,\nreversing the limits changes the sign. This equation was already used as\na calculation rule in <a href=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L8&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L8<\/a>; <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L9&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L9<\/a> identifies it as part of the\nfundamental theorem.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Fully explained mini-example: distance from velocity<\/h2>\n<p>At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\na mobile device has an initial position of 40 kilometres on an imaginary\naxis along a path. Its velocity increases with time in a simplified\nmodel:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v(t)=6t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nTime\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis given in <strong>hours<\/strong> and velocity\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v(t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin <strong>kilometres per hour<\/strong>; the numerical factor 6\ntherefore has the unit kilometres per hour squared. How far has the\ndevice moved forward after two hours?<\/p>\n<p>The antiderivative of the <strong>numerical-value function<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>6<\/mn><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><msup><mi>t<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3t^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe factor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>6<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin this model represents a constant acceleration of\n<strong><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6\\,\\mathrm{km/h}^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/strong>,\nbecause time is substituted as a numerical value in hours. The product\ngives a velocity in km/h; after integration over hours, the result is a\nlength in kilometres again. The initial position of 40 km is a\n<strong>coordinate<\/strong> on an imaginary axis, not a distance already\ntravelled. We consider the change from 0 to 2 hours:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>v<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mi>t<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mn>6<\/mn><mi>t<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><msup><mi>t<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>2<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>0<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>40<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>52<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\nv(t)&amp;=6t\\\\\n\\int_0^2 6t\\,\\mathrm{d}t&amp;=\\left[3t^2\\right]_0^2\\\\\n&amp;=3\\cdot2^2-3\\cdot0^2\\\\\n&amp;=12\\\\\ns(2)&amp;=s_0+12=40+12=52\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘The integral of six times tee with\nrespect to tee from zero to two is twelve; the position after two hours\nis the initial position forty plus twelve, or fifty-two kilometres.’<\/p>\n<p>The square brackets <code>[3t²] from 0 to 2<\/code> are an\n<strong>evaluation instruction<\/strong> here: first substitute the upper\nlimit, then subtract the value at the lower limit. Elsewhere, square\nbrackets can denote intervals, vectors or lists.<\/p>\n<p>Important: The twelve kilometres represent\n<strong>displacement<\/strong>, not automatically the total distance\ntravelled if direction changes. In our model the velocity is\nnon-negative during the period considered, so the two are equal\nhere.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A model of an accumulated quantity as a general\nprinciple<\/h2>\n<p>A quantity\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>B<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(pronounced ‘capital bee’) describes an <strong>accumulated\nquantity<\/strong>, such as an amount of data, a volume of water or the\nnumber of entries already processed. The initial quantity is called\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>B<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(‘bee nought’). We call a time-dependent rate of change\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r(t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(‘ar of tee’).<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>B<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mi>t<\/mi><\/msubsup><mi>r<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B(t)=B_0+\\int_0^t r(u)\\,\\mathrm{d}u<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read in full:<\/strong> ‘The accumulated quantity bee at time\ntee is the initial quantity bee nought plus the integral of the rate ar\nof u with respect to u from zero to tee.’<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>u<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(pronounced ‘you’) is simply a <strong>running variable<\/strong> within\nthe integral; we do not use\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin the integrand because\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nalready represents the upper limit here.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r(u)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmay increase, decrease or take negative values. A positive rate\nincreases the quantity, and a negative rate reduces it.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Technical application:<\/strong> The difference between the\nnumber of messages arriving and being processed per second can be\nmodelled as the net rate of change of the queue length. This continuous\nmodel is an approximation: real messages arrive discretely, and the\nnumber of waiting messages cannot have arbitrary fractional values. A\ncalculated negative quantity would be impossible in a real queue; in\nthat case, the processing rate, empty state or model equation, for\ninstance, must be adjusted.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Growth and decay: what distinguishes two types of\nmodel?<\/h2>\n<p>With <strong>linear growth<\/strong>, the quantity increases by a\nconstant absolute amount per unit of time. With <strong>exponential\ngrowth<\/strong>, the instantaneous change is proportional to the\nquantity currently present. We know the constant of proportionality\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfrom the previous lessons; in time models it often has the unit ‘per\nhour’ or ‘per day’.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msup><mi>B<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>B<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\nB^{\\prime}(t)&amp;=kB(t)\\\\\nB(t)&amp;=B_0e^{kt}\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>First formula read aloud:<\/strong> ‘The derivative of the\naccumulated quantity bee with respect to tee equals kay times the\ncurrent quantity bee of tee.’<\/p>\n<p><strong>Second formula read aloud:<\/strong> ‘The accumulated quantity\nbee after tee equals the initial quantity bee nought times e to the\npower kay times tee.’<\/p>\n<p>For positive\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthere is growth when\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B_0&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nfor negative\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthere is decay. In a time model,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas the unit <strong>1/time<\/strong>; this makes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k\\cdot t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin the exponent dimensionless. The notation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e^{kt}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmeans that <strong>the entire product\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k\\cdot t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis in the exponent<\/strong>. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe quantity remains constant;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B_0=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nalso gives the zero function for every\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\n<strong>Checking by differentiation:<\/strong> The derivative of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B_0\\cdot e^{kt}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith respect to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><msub><mi>B<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k\\cdot B_0\\cdot e^{kt}=k\\cdot B(t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis function therefore satisfies the model equation and the initial\nvalue\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>B<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B(0)=B_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Brief interpretation:<\/strong> A <strong>decay\nparameter<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=-0.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nper day does not mean a linear decrease of 20 percentage points each\nday: the <strong>instantaneous relative rate of decrease<\/strong> is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-0.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nper day. After <strong>one whole day<\/strong>, the remaining factor in\nthe continuous model is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.8187<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e^{-0.2}\\approx0.8187<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nor about <strong>81.87%<\/strong> of the initial quantity. This is a\ndecrease of approximately <strong>18.13%<\/strong> during that day, not\nexactly 20%. The absolute decrease over each equally long time interval\nbecomes smaller as the quantity falls.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Three perspectives on the same quantity<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Information<\/th>\n<th>Mathematical question<\/th>\n<th>Typical tool<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Accumulated quantity\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B(t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘How much is present at time t?’<\/td>\n<td>Function value<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rate of change\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B&#39;(t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘How quickly is the quantity growing or decreasing at this\ninstant?’<\/td>\n<td>Derivative<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Change between two instants<\/td>\n<td>‘How much has been added or lost in total?’<\/td>\n<td>Definite integral<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The <strong>units<\/strong> must match: kilometres per hour multiplied\nby hours gives kilometres; megabytes per second multiplied by seconds\ngives megabytes. Checking the units is often the quickest way to\nidentify a misinterpreted formula.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is it useful for?<\/h2>\n<p>The connection between the derivative and the integral is central to\nrobotics and motion control: position, velocity and acceleration are\nrelated in this way. In data analysis, rates can be summed to obtain\ntotal quantities. In AI and simulation models, differential equations\ndescribe how a state changes continuously; the solution reconstructs the\nevolution of the state from initial conditions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S11\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">For the simple form of the fundamental theorem used here, we\nconsider in particular <strong>continuous rates of change<\/strong> on\nthe relevant interval. University-level study broadens these\nconditions.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The fundamental theorem is no substitute for suitable modelling\nassumptions. If real events are discrete or discontinuous, a continuous\nmodel may be only an approximation.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A rate alone does <strong>not<\/strong> determine the initial\nquantity; an additional initial condition is needed for that.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Integrating <strong>signed<\/strong> velocity gives displacement; for\nthe total distance travelled when direction changes, the magnitude of\nvelocity must be integrated.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Exponential models assume a constant relative rate of change within\nthe model. In real systems, capacity limits and interventions often lead\nto different behaviour. A positive initial quantity remains positive in\nthis model; real quantities may also be discrete and bounded.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S12\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Connection to the next lesson<\/h2>\n<p><strong><a href=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L10&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L10<\/a><\/strong>\ndevelops calculus further: families of functions with parameters,\nintegration by linear substitution, more complex area models and\nselected topics from Berlin’s advanced course (Leistungskurs) are\nintroduced in terms of their meaning and notation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S13\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<p>No individual sources are listed separately for this lesson.<\/p>\n<\/article>","school_mathematics_en:M2-L10":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L10\">M2-L10 – Advanced calculus and selected advanced-course (Leistungskurs) topics<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M2 – Upper secondary education / Abitur<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Upper secondary education / Abitur<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> No specific prerequisites\nstated.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Classification:<\/strong> Upper secondary education at a\nGymnasium · Advanced level / Leistungskurs<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is this about?<\/h2>\n<p>So far, we have considered individual functions and their derivatives\nor integrals. In advanced calculus, we now examine <strong>whole\nfamilies of functions<\/strong> that differ by an adjustable parameter.\nIntegrals also become more challenging when functions are combined,\nareas are bounded by two curves or a function graph generates a\nthree-dimensional solid. The basic approach remains the same: clarify\nthe question, establish the model, read the new symbols and only then\ncalculate. Some selected techniques are explicitly included in the\nBerlin advanced-course (Leistungskurs) examination priorities for 2026;\nthis does not mean that every variant shown here is required in every\nexamination year.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Basic idea and intuition<\/h2>\n<p>Think of a graph as a system with an adjustable control: when you\nchange a parameter, the curve shifts, stretches or changes shape.\nInstead of treating a completely new function for each parameter value,\nwe examine the pattern of the entire <strong>family of\nfunctions<\/strong>. Similarly, integrals can be understood in terms of\nreusable procedures: a well-chosen new variable can simplify a nested\nexpression. Areas and solids of revolution also connect calculus with\ngeometry and 3D models.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>New symbols before the first formula<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Symbol<\/th>\n<th>Read aloud<\/th>\n<th>Meaning here<\/th>\n<th>Other possible uses<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_a(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘eff sub a of x’<\/td>\n<td>Function from a family; a is the fixed chosen parameter<\/td>\n<td>Subscripts also identify series of measurements<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a &gt; 0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘a is greater than zero’<\/td>\n<td>Permitted positive parameter of the family<\/td>\n<td>‘a’ may otherwise be a limit of integration<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^{2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘x squared’<\/td>\n<td>Square of the variable x<\/td>\n<td>A superscript digit can also denote another type of index<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>u<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘you’<\/td>\n<td>New integration variable in a substitution<\/td>\n<td>Any other variable<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{d}u<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘dee u’<\/td>\n<td>Differential notation for the new integration variable<\/td>\n<td>Explained more precisely in higher mathematics<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{d}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘dee’<\/td>\n<td>Differential notation,\ne.g. <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{d}u<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{d}x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>Not itself an ordinary number<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>∞<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\infty<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘infinity’<\/td>\n<td>Unbounded limit of integration<\/td>\n<td>In limit problems generally<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">lim<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lim<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘limit’<\/td>\n<td>Limiting process when a bound grows indefinitely; from <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L2<\/a><\/td>\n<td>Limit of a sequence or function<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>π<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘pi’<\/td>\n<td>Circle constant, needed for circle areas and volumes<\/td>\n<td>Already familiar from <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L15&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L15<\/a><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>V<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘vee’<\/td>\n<td>Volume of a solid of revolution<\/td>\n<td>Vector space, electrical voltage, etc.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘gee of x’<\/td>\n<td>Second function graph when dealing with areas between curves<\/td>\n<td>Any other function<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Families of functions: a curve with a free parameter<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>family of functions<\/strong> contains several functions\ndiffering only by one or more parameters. The parameter is <strong>held\nfixed<\/strong> for a particular curve, while the input\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nvaries along the graph. Example:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_a(x)=a\\cdot x-x^2\\qquad(a&gt;0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read in full:<\/strong> ‘Eff with subscript a of x equals a\ntimes x minus x squared, where a is greater than zero.’<\/p>\n<p>Here\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the positive parameter,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the variable input;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a\\cdot x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis a product and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^{2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the square of the input. Each chosen parameter value produces a\nseparate downward-opening parabola. It has zeros at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFrom the derivative\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>f<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_a&#39;(x)=a-2x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe see that the curve reaches its highest point at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mi>a<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=\\frac a2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nCompleting the square gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><msup><mi>a<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mn>4<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mfrac><mi>a<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_a(x)=\\frac{a^2}{4}-\\left(x-\\frac a2\\right)^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nsince a square is never negative, the <strong>global<\/strong> maximum\nfor all real\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mfrac><msup><mi>a<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mn>4<\/mn><\/mfrac><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{a^2}{4}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthis maximum point lies between the two zeros.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Mini-example:<\/strong> For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe function is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>6<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6x-x^{2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIts zeros are at 0 and 6; the maximum is at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwith function value 9. A single symbolic differentiation therefore\nexplains a whole family of graphs.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Limitation of the model:<\/strong> A parameter in a family is\nnot treated simultaneously as a time variable. If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nitself is made dependent on\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe differentiation rules and the question change.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Connecting parameter and area<\/h2>\n<p>If we want to calculate the positive area under the graph of the\nfamily between its two zeros, we obtain:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mi>a<\/mi><\/msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><msup><mi>a<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><mn>6<\/mn><\/mfrac><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\nA(a)&amp;=\\int_0^a(ax-x^2)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\\n&amp;=\\frac{a^3}{6}\\quad(a&gt;0)\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read in full:<\/strong> ‘The area capital A of a is the\nintegral of a times x minus x squared with respect to x from zero to a;\nthe result is a cubed divided by six.’<\/p>\n<p>The capital letter\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndenotes <strong>area<\/strong> here, whereas lower-case\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the parameter of the family. The same letter in upper and lower case\ntherefore has different roles here. When integrating with respect to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nremains constant. An antiderivative is therefore\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>−<\/mo><mfrac><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><mn>3<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{ax^2}{2}-\\frac{x^3}{3}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSubstituting first\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand then\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>a<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>−<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>3<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><msup><mi>a<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><mn>6<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a^3\\left(\\frac12-\\frac13\\right)=\\frac{a^3}{6}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthis gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><msup><mn>6<\/mn><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><mn>6<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>36<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{6^3}{6}=36<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n<strong>square units in the chosen coordinate representation<\/strong>.\nHere we use\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_a(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nas pure coordinate numbers. If physical units are introduced instead,\nthe unit of each term of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a\\cdot x-x^{2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand of the integral must be checked separately; the notation alone does\nnot establish a real unit of measurement. Conversely, given a desired\npositive area\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe parameter\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a={(6A)}^{1/3}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncan be determined, provided the coordinate and sign assumptions chosen\nhere hold. Without the condition\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe statement ‘positive area between two zeros from 0 to a’ cannot be\nadopted in this simple form.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Integration by linear substitution<\/h2>\n<p>With a nested expression, it sometimes helps to temporarily denote\nthe inner part by a new variable\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>u<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis is particularly simple if the inner function is linear, so its\nderivative is constant.<\/p>\n<p>We consider:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mn>1<\/mn><\/msubsup><mn>2<\/mn><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n&amp;\\int_0^1 2{(2x+1)}^3\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘The integral of two times open bracket\ntwo x plus one close bracket cubed with respect to x from zero to\none.’<\/p>\n<p>The substitution is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u=2x+1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nread ‘u equals two x plus one’. In differential notation,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{d}u=2\\,\\mathrm{d}x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nread ‘dee u equals two times dee x’. At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe get\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe get\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWe must convert <strong>both limits<\/strong> if we write the definite\nintegral wholly in the new variable.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>u<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>⟹<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>⟹<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mn>1<\/mn><\/msubsup><mn>2<\/mn><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mn>3<\/mn><\/msubsup><msup><mi>u<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mfrac><msup><mi>u<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/msup><mn>4<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mn>3<\/mn><\/msubsup><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>81<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><mn>4<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\nu&amp;=2x+1,\\quad \\mathrm{d}u=2\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\\nx=0&amp;\\implies u=1,\\quad x=1\\implies u=3\\\\\n\\int_0^1 2{(2x+1)}^3\\,\\mathrm{d}x&amp;=\\int_1^3 u^3\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\\\\n&amp;=\\left[\\frac{u^4}{4}\\right]_1^3\\\\\n&amp;=\\frac{81-1}{4}\\\\\n&amp;=20\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read in full:<\/strong> ‘The integral from one to three of u\ncubed with respect to u is u to the fourth power divided by four,\nevaluated between one and three, and equals twenty.’<\/p>\n<p>The transformed problem is simpler because the brackets have\ndisappeared. We can check by differentiating the antiderivative\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>4<\/mn><\/msup><mn>4<\/mn><\/mfrac><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{{(2x+1)}^4}{4}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nby the <strong>chain rule<\/strong>, its derivative is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><mn>4<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{4{(2x+1)}^3\\cdot2}{4}=2{(2x+1)}^3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe factor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>2<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbefore the brackets is crucial here; if this factor were missing, the\nsubstitution would have to be adjusted accordingly.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Limitation:<\/strong> You must not perform a substitution and\nkeep the old integration limits unchanged without checking them.\nMoreover, simple <strong>linear<\/strong> substitution is not a\nreplacement for every more advanced method of integration. Integration\nby parts or special trigonometric substitutions are not automatically\npart of the Berlin 2026 examination requirements described here.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Area between two graphs<\/h2>\n<p>When two curves enclose an area, we count the vertical difference\nbetween the upper and lower graphs. If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndenote the two heights on an interval, and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≥<\/mo><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x) \\geq g(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nholds there, then the area is:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≥<\/mo><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\"> on the interval<\/mtext><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=\\int_a^b (f(x)-g(x))\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\qquad(f(x)\\geq g(x)\\text{ on the interval})<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘The area A is the integral of eff of x\nminus gee of x with respect to x between a and b.’<\/p>\n<p>The upper function really must be above the other throughout the\ninterval being considered. If the graphs intersect, it may be necessary\nto split the interval at the intersections or integrate the absolute\nvalue of the difference. More precisely, for continuous functions on a\nfinite interval the <strong>total<\/strong> non-negative area is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int_a^b|f(x)-g(x)|\\,\\mathrm{d}x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nwithout the absolute value, component areas can cancel each other out.\nAbsolute value notation was already explained in M2-L8.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Mini-example:<\/strong> Between\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(x)=x^{2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin the interval from zero to one,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the upper graph. Therefore,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mn>1<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>−<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>3<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>6<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int_0^1(x-x^2)\\,\\mathrm{d}x=\\frac12-\\frac13=\\frac16<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsquare units. In a 3D graphics program, a similar calculation could give\nan intersection area or a cross-sectional area of two mathematically\ndefined profiles.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Improper integrals: a limit lies at infinity<\/h2>\n<p>Some areas extend indefinitely. This does <strong>not\nautomatically<\/strong> mean that their area is infinite. We first\nexamine the integral up to a finite upper limit and then take a\nlimit.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>∞<\/mi><\/msubsup><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mfrac><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">lim<\/mi><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mi>∞<\/mi><\/mrow><\/munder><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>b<\/mi><\/msubsup><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mfrac><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int_1^\\infty\\frac1{x^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x=\\lim_{b\\to\\infty}\\int_1^b\\frac1{x^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read in full:<\/strong> ‘The integral of one over x squared\nwith respect to x from one to infinity is the limit, as b tends to\ninfinity, of the integral from one to b; this limit is one.’<\/p>\n<p>Here\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>∞<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\infty<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwritten at the upper right, is the infinite upper limit; in a simple\ntext representation we can write <code>∫₁^∞<\/code> for clarity. For\nfinite\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b&gt;1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe antiderivative\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><\/mfrac><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-\\frac1x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives the value\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mi>b<\/mi><\/mfrac><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1-\\frac1b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAs\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>b<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbecomes arbitrarily large,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mi>b<\/mi><\/mfrac><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac1b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\napproaches zero. The area therefore converges to one.<\/p>\n<p>Not every improper integral converges. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><\/mfrac><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac1x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nstarting at one, for example, the accumulated area grows without a\nfinite limit, because the expression\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ln(b)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngrows without bound as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mi>∞<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b\\to \\infty<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA symbol\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>∞<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\infty<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nalone therefore <strong>tells us nothing yet<\/strong> about whether the\nresult is finite. An <strong>unbounded function at a finite\nlimit<\/strong> also leads to an improper integral. We denote an\narbitrarily small <strong>positive approaching limit<\/strong> by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ε<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(‘epsilon’) and let it tend to zero from the right:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mn>1<\/mn><\/msubsup><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><msqrt><mi>x<\/mi><\/msqrt><\/mfrac><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int_0^1\\frac1{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmeans the limit of integrals from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ε<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>1<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe antiderivative\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><msqrt><mi>x<\/mi><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\sqrt{x}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msqrt><mi>ε<\/mi><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2-2\\sqrt{\\varepsilon}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>2<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin the limit. In contrast,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mn>1<\/mn><\/msubsup><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><\/mfrac><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int_0^1\\frac1x\\,\\mathrm{d}x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndiverges because its limit contains\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>ε<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-\\ln(\\varepsilon)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand grows without bound. Here the notation <code>∫_ε^1<\/code> is used\nfor the temporary positive lower limit.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solids of revolution: a curve generates a volume<\/h2>\n<p>If <strong>the region between the graph of a continuous non-negative\nfunction\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the x-axis<\/strong> on the interval from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>b<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a\\leq b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>)\nis rotated about the x-axis, a <strong>filled solid of\nrevolution<\/strong> is formed. Rotating only the graph line gives merely\nits curved surface, not its volume. The cross-section of the filled\nsolid perpendicular to the x-axis is a circular disc whose radius\ncorresponds to the function value. The area of the circle is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>π<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi\\,\\mathrm{radius}^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n– familiar from M1-L15. Summing the thin circular discs gives the\nvolume:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>π<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msubsup><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V=\\pi\\cdot\\int_a^b{[f(x)]}^2\\,\\mathrm{d}x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read in full:<\/strong> ‘The volume vee is pi times the\nintegral of the square of eff of x with respect to x, from a to b.’<\/p>\n<p>Here\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>V<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the volume,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>π<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the circle constant, and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">{[f(x)]}^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmeans <strong>square the entire function value<\/strong>, not just the\nvariable. The limits determine the length of the section being\nrotated.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Mini-example:<\/strong> Rotating the <strong>region\nbetween<\/strong> the straight line\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the x-axis for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\leq x\\leq 2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nabout the x-axis produces a cone with height two and radius two. The\nintegral gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>π<\/mi><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>π<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mfrac><mn>8<\/mn><mn>3<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>8<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><\/mrow><mn>3<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi\\int_0^2x^2\\,\\mathrm{d}x=\\pi\\cdot\\frac83=\\frac{8\\pi}{3}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncubic units. The cone formula familiar from geometry gives the same\nvalue.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Limitation:<\/strong> For hollow solids between two surfaces\nof revolution or rotations about other axes, adjusted formulae apply.\nThe disc formula must not be applied to every 3D problem without\nchecking it.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is actually included in Berlin’s advanced course?<\/h2>\n<p>The official <strong>Mathematics Examination Priorities 2026,\nadvanced course (Leistungskurs), pages 3–4<\/strong>, mention, among\nother things, families of functions, limits, the connection between\ndifferentiation and integration, integration by linear substitution,\ncalculations of areas, improper integrals, volumes of revolution and\ncalculating parameters or integration limits for specified quantities.\nThe particular classes of functions and restrictions described there\nmust be taken into account. Our lesson presents a <strong>selection\naimed at understanding<\/strong>, not an exhaustive list of examination\nrequirements.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Official source (checked on 3 November 2026):<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"https://www.berlin.de/sen/bildung/schule/pruefungen-und-abschluesse/abitur/ps_mathematik_2026_lk.pdf\">https://www.berlin.de/sen/bildung/schule/pruefungen-und-abschluesse/abitur/ps_mathematik_2026_lk.pdf<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\n– particularly pp. 3–4, the calculus section. The file is mentioned here\nonly as an external documentary source; all mathematical explanations in\nthis lesson remain readable <strong>without internet\naccess<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S11\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is it useful for?<\/h2>\n<p>Families of functions are a basic tool in parametric modelling: a\nparameter might represent component size, a cost factor or an input\nthreshold. Linear substitution is a useful way of thinking in symbolic\ncomputer algebra. Areas between curves help compare models; we encounter\nintegrals to infinity later in probability and signal processing. Solids\nof revolution connect analytical formulae with 3D geometry, design and\nrobotics.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S12\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A <strong>parameter<\/strong> and a <strong>running variable<\/strong>\nmust be clearly distinguished both in words and in calculations.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Areas require the correct upper and lower graphs, or splitting at\nintersections; a signed integral is not always positive.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Improper integrals are defined using limits. Some converge, others\ndiverge.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The formula for rotation using circular discs assumes the\n<strong>filled region<\/strong> between a continuous curve and the\nx-axis, the appropriate axis of rotation and the correct radius. A\nrotated graph alone describes a surface; for hollow solids, the\ndifference between two circular areas may be needed.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The scope of this lesson follows the <strong>Berlin 2026 examination\npriorities for the advanced course (Leistungskurs)<\/strong>. Other\nyears, regions or degree courses must not be treated as equivalent\nwithout further checking.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">More complex techniques of integration, the theory of parameter\ndependence and general integration in several variables are covered only\nin advanced university studies.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S13\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Connection to the next lesson<\/h2>\n<p>With <strong><a href=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L11&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L11<\/a> –\nVectors, dot product and angles<\/strong>, we move from functions and\ncontinuous change to <strong>analytical geometry<\/strong>. This begins\nthe next upper secondary block, which is particularly relevant to 3D\ngraphics and robotics.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S14\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<p>External subject sources and further links mentioned in the teaching\ntext:<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.berlin.de/sen/bildung/schule/pruefungen-und-abschluesse/abitur/ps_mathematik_2026_lk.pdf\">https://www.berlin.de/sen/bildung/schule/pruefungen-und-abschluesse/abitur/ps_mathematik_2026_lk.pdf<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_mathematics_en:M2-L11":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L11\">M2-L11 – Vectors, scalar product and angles<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M2 – Upper secondary education / Abitur<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Upper secondary education / Abitur<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> No fixed prerequisites\nspecified.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Context:<\/strong> Upper secondary education (Gymnasium) ·\nanalytical geometry<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is this about?<\/h2>\n<p>A point describes a location, whereas a vector describes a\ndisplacement or direction with a particular length. Vectors allow us to\ndeal precisely with motion, forces and distances in space, independently\nof a drawing. Unlike a single number, a vector has several components,\nfor example for the three spatial directions. The <strong>scalar\nproduct<\/strong> combines two vectors to produce a single number,\nallowing us to make statements about their angle and orientation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Basic idea and visual intuition<\/h2>\n<p>Imagine a robot in three-dimensional space: a position is a location,\nwhile the instruction ‘two metres to the right, one metre forwards,\nthree metres upwards’ is a displacement. Several displacements can be\nadded; we can calculate their lengths and their directions relative to\none another. The crucial distinction is between <strong>location\n(point)<\/strong>, <strong>displacement (vector)<\/strong> and\n<strong>number (scalar)<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>New symbols before the first formula<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Symbol<\/th>\n<th>How to read it<\/th>\n<th>Meaning here<\/th>\n<th>Other possible uses<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(1;2;3)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘P with coordinates one, two, three’<\/td>\n<td>Point with x-, y- and z-coordinates<\/td>\n<td>P can denote a probability in probability theory<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>vec v<\/code> or\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{v}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘vector v’<\/td>\n<td>Directed displacement<\/td>\n<td>Also written in bold as <strong>v<\/strong> in texts<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(2;-1;3)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘vector with components two, minus one, three’<\/td>\n<td>A vector in three dimensions; semicolons separate the\ncomponents<\/td>\n<td>Read as a point only in the appropriate context<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>overrightarrow{AB}<\/code><\/td>\n<td>‘vector from A to B’<\/td>\n<td>Displacement from point A to point B<\/td>\n<td>An arrow above letters can also indicate direction in general<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>λ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘lambda’<\/td>\n<td>Arbitrary real multiplier<\/td>\n<td>Later often a parameter or eigenvalue<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>lVert v⃗rVert<\/code> or\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">‖<\/mo><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">‖<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lVert\\vec{v}\\rVert<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘norm of v’, ‘length of vector v’<\/td>\n<td>Non-negative vector length<\/td>\n<td>Other norms are possible at university level<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>⋅<\/mo><mover><mi>w<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{v}\\cdot\\vec{w}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘v dot w’<\/td>\n<td>A number obtained from two vectors<\/td>\n<td>The same centred dot often denotes ordinary multiplication\nelsewhere<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>θ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘theta’<\/td>\n<td>Angle between two vectors<\/td>\n<td>Arbitrary angle variable or model parameter<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cos(\\theta)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘cosine of theta’<\/td>\n<td>Cosine of the included angle<\/td>\n<td>Also a general periodic function<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>perp<\/code> or\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>⟂<\/mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\perp<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘is perpendicular to’<\/td>\n<td>Vectors at right angles<\/td>\n<td>Orthogonality of objects in general<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The notation with enclosing brackets and semicolons is a compact\n<strong>text representation<\/strong>. In school textbooks, the three\ncomponents are often written one below another in a column. Both forms\nmean the same thing here. The arrows above the letters are not part of\nthe numbers; they indicate the type of mathematical object.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From a point to a vector<\/h2>\n<p>Two points define a displacement. For example,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A(1;2;0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B(4;6;0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare points in space. To get from A to B, we take the coordinate\ndifference <strong>destination minus start<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/mrow><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>6<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>0<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\overrightarrow{AB}=B-A=(4-1;\\,6-2;\\,0-0)=(3;4;0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read in full:<\/strong> ‘The vector from A to B is point B\nminus point A, giving the vector three, four, zero.’ Strictly speaking,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B-A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis shorthand for subtracting the respective position coordinates. The\ncomponents give the displacement: three units in the x-direction, four\nin the y-direction and none in the z-direction.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Important distinction:<\/strong> A point\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A(1;2;0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis not automatically the same object as the vector\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1;2;0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn a fixed coordinate system, we can form the <strong>position vector\nfrom the origin to A<\/strong> with these components. This relationship\nis useful, but does not replace the conceptual distinction.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Adding and scaling vectors<\/h2>\n<p>When adding vectors, we add the components in each corresponding\ndirection. When <strong>scaling<\/strong> a vector, we multiply every\ncomponent by the same number.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>+<\/mo><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mn>2<\/mn><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n\\vec{u}&amp;=(2;-1;3),\\quad\\vec{v}=(1;4;-2)\\\\\n\\vec{u}+\\vec{v}&amp;=(3;3;1)\\\\\n2\\vec{u}&amp;=(4;-2;6)\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘Vector u plus vector v is the vector\nthree, three, one; twice vector u is the vector four, minus two, six.’\nThe first line gives two input vectors. The result\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(3;3;1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncomes from the three sums\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2+1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-1+4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3-2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nMultiplying by two doubles the length of vector u and preserves its\ndirection, provided u is not the zero vector.<\/p>\n<p>If a non-zero vector is instead multiplied by a\n<strong>negative<\/strong> number, its direction is reversed. The factor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntherefore doubles the length and reverses the direction. In general, for\nevery real number\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>λ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">‖<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">‖<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">|<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">‖<\/mo><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">‖<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lVert\\lambda\\vec{u}\\rVert=\\lvert\\lambda\\rvert\\,\\lVert\\vec{u}\\rVert<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘The length of lambda times u is the\nabsolute value of lambda times the length of u.’ The <strong>absolute\nvalue<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\lambda|<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis never negative. A factor of zero produces the <strong>zero\nvector<\/strong>, all of whose components are zero. It has length zero,\nbut no unique direction.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Length and distance<\/h2>\n<p>The length of a vector extends Pythagoras’ theorem to several\ndimensions. In a Cartesian coordinate system with perpendicular axes,\nthe following applies to three components:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">‖<\/mo><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">‖<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><msubsup><mi>v<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>+<\/mo><msubsup><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>+<\/mo><msubsup><mi>v<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lVert\\vec{v}\\rVert=\\sqrt{v_1^2+v_2^2+v_3^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘The length of vector v is the square\nroot of the square of its first component plus the square of its second\ncomponent plus the square of its third component.’ The subscript numbers\n<code>₁<\/code>, <code>₂<\/code>, <code>₃<\/code> are <strong>component\nindices<\/strong>, not powers; <code>²<\/code>, on the other hand, denotes\nthe square.<\/p>\n<p>For our connecting vector\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(3;4;0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe length is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>9<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>16<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{9+16+0}=5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe distance between points A and B is therefore five units of length.\nThe length is always non-negative and is zero only for the zero\nvector.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Limitation:<\/strong> This simple formula assumes coordinate\naxes with equal scales that are perpendicular to one another. Other\ncoordinate systems or other notions of distance require adapted\nformulas.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The scalar product: how similar are two directions?<\/h2>\n<p>The word <strong>scalar<\/strong> here means a single number. The\nscalar product of two vectors therefore yields <strong>not a third\nvector<\/strong>, but a number. In Cartesian coordinates, we multiply the\ncorresponding components and add the products:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>⋅<\/mo><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{u}\\cdot\\vec{v}=u_1v_1+u_2v_2+u_3v_3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘Vector u dot vector v is u one times v\none plus u two times v two plus u three times v three.’ Each index\nidentifies the corresponding spatial direction. For two <strong>non-zero\nvectors<\/strong>, a positive result means an acute angle, a negative\nresult means an obtuse angle and a result of zero means a right angle.\nIf at least one of the vectors is zero, its scalar product with any\nvector is zero, but an angle is then <strong>undefined<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<p>Example:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>⋅<\/mo><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n\\vec{u}&amp;=(3;4;0),\\quad\\vec{v}=(-4;3;0)\\\\\n\\vec{u}\\cdot\\vec{v}&amp;=3(-4)+4\\cdot3+0\\cdot0=0\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘Three times minus four plus four times\nthree plus zero times zero equals zero.’ Because both vectors are\nnon-zero, the angle between them is exactly 90 degrees: they are\n<strong>orthogonal<\/strong>, meaning perpendicular to each other.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Calculating the angle using the scalar product<\/h2>\n<p>The scalar product can also be described geometrically using the two\nlengths and the included angle. For <strong>two non-zero\nvectors<\/strong>:<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-mathematics-en/media/img/S_MAT_028_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Vector u points right to (1,0), vector v diagonally to (1,1), with a right angle at the foot of the projection and an included angle of 45 degrees.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: Projecting\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=(1,1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nonto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u=(1,0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nillustrates the dot product and the 45° angle.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>Scalar product: the angle between\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u=(1,0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=(1,1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>45<\/mn><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">°<\/mtext><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">45^{\\text{°}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>⋅<\/mo><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">‖<\/mo><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">‖<\/mo><\/mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">‖<\/mo><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">‖<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mrow><\/mfrac><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cos(\\theta)=\\frac{\\vec{u}\\cdot\\vec{v}}{\\lVert\\vec{u}\\rVert\\,\\lVert\\vec{v}\\rVert}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘The cosine of theta is the scalar\nproduct of u and v divided by the product of the length of u and the\nlength of v.’ Here\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>θ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the angle between the vectors, between zero and 180 degrees. The\ndenominator is non-zero only if neither vector is the zero vector.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Fully explained mini-example:<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>⋅<\/mo><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">‖<\/mo><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">‖<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">‖<\/mo><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">‖<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>45<\/mn><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">°<\/mtext><\/msup><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n\\vec{u}&amp;=(1;0;0),\\quad\\vec{v}=(1;1;0)\\\\\n\\vec{u}\\cdot\\vec{v}&amp;=1\\\\\n\\lVert\\vec{u}\\rVert&amp;=1,\\quad\\lVert\\vec{v}\\rVert=\\sqrt2\\\\\n\\cos(\\theta)&amp;=\\frac1{\\sqrt2},\\quad\\theta=45^{\\text{°}}\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Read aloud: ‘The cosine of the angle is one divided by the square\nroot of two; the angle is 45 degrees.’ The inverse cosine (familiar from\n<a href=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L16&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L16<\/a>; often written\n<code>arccos<\/code>) gives the angle from the cosine value. To obtain\nthis result, a calculator must be set to <strong>degrees<\/strong> rather\nthan radians, if it is not already working in degrees.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Checking the limiting cases:<\/strong> For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{u}=(1;0;0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>w<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{w}=(-2;0;0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe have\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>⋅<\/mo><mover><mi>w<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{u}\\cdot\\vec{w}=-2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">‖<\/mo><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">‖<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>⋅<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">‖<\/mo><mover><mi>w<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">‖<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lVert\\vec{u}\\rVert\\cdot\\lVert\\vec{w}\\rVert=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nso\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cos(\\theta)=-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>180<\/mn><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">°<\/mtext><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta=180^{\\text{°}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor non-zero vectors pointing in the same direction, the cosine is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>+<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nfor perpendicular vectors, it is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe quotient is therefore mathematically between <strong>−1 and\n+1<\/strong>. In software, rounding can produce a result slightly outside\nthis range. Only after checking the condition on the denominator can\nsuch a rounded result be restricted to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[-1;1]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbefore applying <code>arccos<\/code>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is it useful for? – Software and robotics<\/h2>\n<p>In a 3D engine, vectors determine viewing directions, camera\nmovements and lighting calculations. A scalar product shows, for\nexample, whether a surface tends to face the light source and how\nclosely its orientation matches the direction of the light. In AI\nembeddings, the version divided by <strong>both vector lengths<\/strong>,\ncalled <strong>cosine similarity<\/strong>, measures directional\nsimilarity for non-zero data vectors; vectors pointing in the same\ndirection receive a value of one, and vectors pointing in opposite\ndirections receive minus one. However, this does not automatically imply\nsemantic equivalence or factual truth.<\/p>\n<p>For robots, a vector might describe a desired movement target\nrelative to the current position. For physical forces and velocities, we\nmust also pay attention to compatible units and the frame of reference\nused.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The formulas used here apply to ordinary Euclidean vectors in an\northonormal Cartesian coordinate system.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>A point and a vector are not the same<\/strong>; we must not\ntreat a position coordinate as a displacement without further\nconsideration.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The length of a zero vector is zero. The formula above cannot\ndetermine an angle between the zero vector and another vector.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The scalar product can give negative values. It is neither a\ndistance nor a probability.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">In numerical software practice, rounding errors can occur: a\ntheoretically perpendicular pair may yield a very small value instead of\nexactly zero. For comparisons, a tolerance appropriate to the scale of\nthe quantities is useful.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Vectors with different physical units (e.g. metres and seconds) must\nnot be added as though they represented the same kind of\ndisplacement.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S11\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Connection to the next lesson<\/h2>\n<p><strong><a href=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L12&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L12<\/a> – Parametric\nequations of lines and planes<\/strong> uses a starting point and one or\nmore direction vectors to describe infinitely many points in space\ncompactly.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S12\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<p>No individual sources are listed separately for this lesson.<\/p>\n<\/article>","school_mathematics_en:M2-L12":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L12\">M2-L12 – Parametric equations of lines and planes<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M2 – Upper secondary education / Abitur<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Upper secondary education / Abitur<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> No fixed prerequisites\nspecified.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Context:<\/strong> Upper secondary education (Gymnasium) ·\nanalytical geometry<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is this about?<\/h2>\n<p>A straight line is an infinitely long line with no thickness. A plane\nis an infinitely extended flat surface with no thickness. In\nthree-dimensional space, we can describe both using vectors. Instead of\nlisting each point individually, we use a known point and one or two\ndirections. Varying the associated <strong>parameters<\/strong> gives us\nall the points on the line or plane.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Basic idea and visual intuition<\/h2>\n<p>A straight line can be understood as a <strong>starting point plus\nany amount of movement in exactly one direction<\/strong>. A plane needs\n<strong>two linearly independent directions<\/strong>: neither may be a\nmultiple of the other; in particular, neither direction vector may be\nzero. Think of a straight rail in space or an idealised, infinitely\nextended tabletop. A parametric equation is a compact mathematical\ninstruction that can also serve as a generator of points on such\nobjects.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>New symbols before the first formula<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Symbol<\/th>\n<th>How to read it<\/th>\n<th>Meaning here<\/th>\n<th>Other possible uses<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>g<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>E<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘line g’, ‘plane E’<\/td>\n<td>Names for a line and a plane<\/td>\n<td>The letters can be chosen freely<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>X<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘point X’<\/td>\n<td>Variable point on a line or plane<\/td>\n<td>x can otherwise be a number or coordinate<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>p<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{p}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘vector p’<\/td>\n<td>Position vector of a known reference point<\/td>\n<td>p can stand for probability<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{u}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{v}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘vector u’, ‘vector v’<\/td>\n<td>Direction vectors<\/td>\n<td>General displacements, velocities<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>s<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘t’, ‘s’<\/td>\n<td>Freely chosen parameters<\/td>\n<td>Time variables in models of motion<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>∈<\/code><\/td>\n<td>‘is an element of’<\/td>\n<td>Membership of a set of numbers<\/td>\n<td>Also used for points and other sets<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℝ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb{R}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘real numbers’<\/td>\n<td>Parameters may take any real value<\/td>\n<td>Already familiar from <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L1<\/a><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>∈<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℝ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t\\in\\mathbb{R}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘t is an element of the real numbers’<\/td>\n<td>Unrestricted parameter range<\/td>\n<td>For a line segment the range would be restricted<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>n<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{n}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘normal vector n’<\/td>\n<td>Vector perpendicular to a plane<\/td>\n<td>n can otherwise be a count or an index<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>n<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>⋅<\/mo><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{n}\\cdot\\vec{u}=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘n dot u is zero’<\/td>\n<td>Perpendicular directions as in <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L11&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L11<\/a><\/td>\n<td>A scalar product of zero alone is not sufficient to define an angle\ninvolving zero vectors<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>A <strong>position vector<\/strong> is the vector from the coordinate\norigin to a point. A <strong>reference point<\/strong> is a known point\nthrough which our line or plane passes. The parameter names have no\nfixed meaning: we can use other letters instead of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>s<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nas long as their roles are clear.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The line as a point generator<\/h2>\n<p>The parametric equation of a line in space in text notation is:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo>:<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mi>X<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mover><mi>p<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>+<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo>∈<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℝ<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>≠<\/mo><mover><mn>0<\/mn><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g:\\ X=\\vec{p}+t\\vec{u}\\qquad(t\\in\\mathbb{R},\\ \\vec{u}\\neq\\vec{0})<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read in full:<\/strong> ‘Line g consists of points X with\nposition vector p plus t times u. Parameter t may be any real number; u\nis not the zero vector.’\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>p<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{p}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\npoints to the starting point;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{u}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndetermines the direction. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwe obtain the reference point; for positive or negative values we move\nalong the line in either direction.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Mini-example – a rail in three-dimensional\nspace:<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mover><mi>p<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n\\vec p&amp;=(1;2;0),\\quad\\vec u=(2;-1;3)\\\\\nX(t)&amp;=(1;2;0)+t\\cdot(2;-1;3)\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘Point X of t is one, two, zero plus t\ntimes the vector two, minus one, three.’ Substituting\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe obtain, component by component:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>;<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>0<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X(2)=(1+2\\cdot2;\\,2+2\\cdot(-1);\\,0+2\\cdot3)=(5;0;6)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The point\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(5;0;6)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntherefore lies on the line. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwe would instead obtain the point\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(-1;3;-3)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nBecause all real parameter values are permitted, this equation describes\na <strong>line<\/strong>, not a segment bounded by two endpoints.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>When does a point lie on a line?<\/h2>\n<p>To check whether point\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q(3;1;3)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nlies on our example line, we look for a <strong>single common parameter\nvalue<\/strong> that satisfies all three coordinate conditions.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>t<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mn>2<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mn>3<\/mn><mi>t<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n1+2t&amp;=3\\\\\n2-t&amp;=1\\\\\n3t&amp;=3\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘One plus twice t must be three, two\nminus t must be one, and three times t must be three.’ All equations\ngive\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe point Q therefore lies on the line.<\/p>\n<p>Another point may satisfy two of the three equations but fail to\nsatisfy the third; in that case it does <strong>not<\/strong> lie on the\nline. This shows why we must not choose parameter values separately for\neach component.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A plane needs two independent directions<\/h2>\n<p>For a plane, we use two direction vectors in addition to the\nreference point. The two parameters can vary independently of each\nother:<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-mathematics-en/media/img/S_MAT_029_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Schematic parametric representation of a plane through a reference point P with two linearly independent directions u and v. The destination point is P plus 2u plus 3v.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: A plane is spanned by the reference point\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>P<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand two independent directions;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>u<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mi>v<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P+2u+3v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis shown as an example.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>Two independent parameters generate points on a plane; the diagram is\na projection.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo>:<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mi>X<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mover><mi>p<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>+<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>+<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo>∈<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℝ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E:\\ X=\\vec{p}+s\\vec{u}+t\\vec{v}\\qquad(s,t\\in\\mathbb{R})<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘Plane E consists of all points X with\nposition vector p plus s times u plus t times v; s and t may be any real\nnumbers.’ Unlike the line, there are now <strong>two degrees of\nfreedom<\/strong>. The direction vectors must be <strong>linearly\nindependent<\/strong>. Here this means that neither is a scalar multiple\nof the other; in particular, neither may be zero. Otherwise, at most a\nline or a single point is produced instead of a plane.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Mini-example – a sloping plane:<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mover><mi>p<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>E<\/mi><mo>:<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n\\vec p&amp;=(1;0;2),\\quad\\vec u=(1;0;0),\\quad\\vec v=(0;1;1)\\\\\nE:\\quad X(s,t)&amp;=(1;0;2)+s\\cdot(1;0;0)+t\\cdot(0;1;1)\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\naddition component by component gives the point:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>0<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X(2,3)=(1+2;\\,0+3;\\,2+3)=(3;3;5)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The two directions are linearly independent: with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{u}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwe change only the first coordinate, while with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{v}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwe change the second and third coordinates together. They therefore\nreally describe a two-dimensional plane.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Counterexample to the requirement:<\/strong> If we replaced\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{v}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(2;0;0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{v}=2\\vec{u}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWe could then write\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>+<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s\\vec{u}+t\\vec{v}=(s+2t)\\vec{u}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nDespite having two parameter names, we would obtain only the line\nthrough the reference point in direction\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{u}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<strong>not a plane<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>An alternative representation: normal form<\/h2>\n<p>For a plane, we can give a <strong>non-zero<\/strong> vector that is\n<strong>perpendicular to every direction within that plane<\/strong>.\nThis is called a <strong>normal vector<\/strong>. For a plane with\nlinearly independent direction vectors u and v, we must therefore have\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>n<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>⋅<\/mo><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{n}\\cdot\\vec{u}=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>n<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>⋅<\/mo><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{n}\\cdot\\vec{v}=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nNormal form is useful for many questions involving distances and\nintersections.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>n<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>⋅<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mover><mi>p<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{n}\\cdot(X-\\vec{p})=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘The scalar product of normal vector n\nand the vector from reference point p to variable point X is zero.’ Here\nthe difference\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mover><mi>p<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X-\\vec{p}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis understood as the difference between position vectors. Because the\nscalar product is zero, the direction of the difference is perpendicular\nto the non-zero\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>n<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{n}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand thus lies in the plane specified by the reference point. At the\nreference point itself, the zero difference vector also satisfies the\nequation; no angle with the zero vector needs to be defined for\nthis.<\/p>\n<p>For our example plane, we can check a normal vector directly:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>n<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{n}=(0;-1;1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsatisfies both\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>n<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>⋅<\/mo><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{n}\\cdot\\vec{u}=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>n<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>⋅<\/mo><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{n}\\cdot\\vec{v}=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>This converts the parametric description into what is called\n<strong>Cartesian form<\/strong>:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z-y=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘Zed minus y is two.’ This equation\nholds for every point in the plane. For example, the previously\ncalculated point\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(3;3;5)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives the calculation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5-3=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nBy contrast, point\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q(3;3;4)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndoes <strong>not<\/strong> lie in the plane:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4-3=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ninstead of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>2<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe letter\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>z<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nstands for the third spatial coordinate, and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>y<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor the second.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Important:<\/strong> The normal vector is not unique. Twice\nthe same vector or its negative is also perpendicular to the plane.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What about line segments, rays and bounded surfaces?<\/h2>\n<p>The parametric form normally represents infinite geometrical objects.\nFor a <strong>line segment<\/strong> from the reference point with\nposition vector\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>p<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{p}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto the point with position vector\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>p<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>+<\/mo><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{p}+\\vec{u}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe parameter in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mover><mi>p<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>+<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X(t)=\\vec{p}+t\\vec{u}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis restricted to the interval\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\leq t\\leq 1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor a <strong>ray<\/strong> starting at the reference point in direction\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{u}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe have\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t\\geq 0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAnother parametrisation may use different parameter limits for the same\nline segment. These restrictions are important for actual paths of\nmotion, components or screen segments.<\/p>\n<p>Likewise, a physical tabletop is only a bounded portion of a\nmathematically infinite plane. Its boundaries must be specified\nadditionally, for example using parameter intervals or a polygonal\nboundary.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is it useful for?<\/h2>\n<p>A 3D application can treat a camera’s viewing direction as a line and\ninvestigate where it meets the surface of an object. In robotics\nsimulations, possible directions of motion and obstacle surfaces are\ndescribed using vector equations. Lines and planes are fundamental\nobjects in CAD and engineering design. In computer games and ray\ntracing, a <strong>ray<\/strong> rather than the complete line is often\nconsidered in addition; this means that intersections behind the camera\ndo not count as visible objects.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A line’s direction vector must be non-zero; a plane’s two direction\nvectors must be linearly independent.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A <strong>parametric equation<\/strong> describes all points\ngenerated using the same common set of parameters. We must not choose a\nnew\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nindependently for each coordinate.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">With two independent direction vectors, each point of the plane has\nuniquely determined parameter values. With dependent directions, the\nrepresentation is <strong>not a true parametrisation of a\nplane<\/strong>; different pairs of parameters can describe the same\npoint on a line or even the reference point.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Our intuitive models assume straight-line Euclidean geometry. Curved\nsurfaces need other descriptions.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Whether an actual motion follows the same straight line throughout\nis a modelling assumption; time is not automatically the same as the\nparameter.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S11\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Connection to the next lesson<\/h2>\n<p><strong><a href=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L13&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L13<\/a> – Distances,\nrelative positions and simultaneous equations<\/strong> examines when\nspatial objects intersect, are parallel or are skew, and how their\ndistance is calculated.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S12\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<p>No individual sources are listed separately for this lesson.<\/p>\n<\/article>","school_mathematics_en:M2-L13":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L13\">M2-L13 – Distances, relative positions and simultaneous equations<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M2 – Upper secondary education / Abitur<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Upper secondary education / Abitur<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> No fixed prerequisites\nspecified.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Context:<\/strong> Upper secondary education (Gymnasium) ·\nanalytical geometry<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is this about?<\/h2>\n<p>Having described lines and planes as sets of points, we face a\npractical question: do these objects meet, are they parallel, or do they\nnot intersect at all? In space, two lines can even be distinct and\nnon-parallel without intersecting. We also want to know how far a point\nis from a line or plane. To answer these questions, we combine\ngeometrical intuition with systems of linear equations. Later, a matrix\nis simply a particularly clear way of writing and working with such\nsystems.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Basic idea and visual intuition<\/h2>\n<p>A point of intersection must lie on all the objects involved\n<strong>at the same time<\/strong>. We therefore set their point\ndescriptions equal to each other and look for common parameter values.\nDistance is about the <strong>shortest connection<\/strong>, which runs\nperpendicular to the object in question. Algebra answers the spatial\nquestion, and geometry helps us interpret the algebraic solution\ncorrectly.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>New symbols before the first formula<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Symbol<\/th>\n<th>How to read it<\/th>\n<th>Meaning here<\/th>\n<th>Other possible uses<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>g<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>h<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>E<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘line g’, ‘line h’, ‘plane E’<\/td>\n<td>Names of the geometrical objects being compared<\/td>\n<td>Arbitrary labels<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>r<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>s<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘r’, ‘s’, ‘t’<\/td>\n<td>Independent parameters depending on the line or plane<\/td>\n<td>Often displacement or time in physical models<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d(P,E)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘distance from P to plane E’<\/td>\n<td>Shortest distance in space<\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>d<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nalso occurs in differential notation elsewhere<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">‖<\/mo><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">‖<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lVert\\vec v\\rVert<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘norm’, ‘length of vector v’<\/td>\n<td>Vector length from <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L11&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L11<\/a><\/td>\n<td>General norms in higher mathematics<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>n<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{n}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘normal vector n’<\/td>\n<td>Direction perpendicular to the plane, from <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L12&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L12<\/a><\/td>\n<td>Name of any other vector<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lvert a\\rvert<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘absolute value of a’<\/td>\n<td>Non-negative magnitude of a real number<\/td>\n<td>Vertical bars in other contexts: cardinality of a set,\ndeterminant<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>⟂<\/mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\perp<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘is perpendicular to’<\/td>\n<td>Direction of the shortest connection to a line or plane<\/td>\n<td>Orthogonality in general<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>LGS<\/code><\/td>\n<td>‘system of linear equations’ (German abbreviation)<\/td>\n<td>Several linear equations simultaneously<\/td>\n<td>Abbreviation, not a mathematical operator<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mover><mi>b<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A\\cdot\\vec x=\\vec b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘matrix A times vector x equals vector b’<\/td>\n<td>Compact notation for a linear system<\/td>\n<td>A can denote an area elsewhere<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>[a b; c d]<\/code><\/td>\n<td>‘matrix with two rows and two columns’<\/td>\n<td>Text representation of a rectangular table of numbers<\/td>\n<td>Not an ordinary fraction; the semicolon indicates a new row\nhere<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>An <strong>intersection<\/strong> is the set of common points. Two\nlines can intersect in at most one point or be completely identical; for\na line and a plane, the common set is empty, a point or the entire line.\n<strong>Two planes<\/strong> can also share an entire plane (if they are\nidentical) or intersect along a line. We use the expressions ‘no\nintersection’, ‘one point of intersection’ and ‘identical’ without\nassuming knowledge of another set symbol.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Two lines: intersecting, parallel, identical or skew?<\/h2>\n<p>Two lines in space can intersect at a point, be parallel and\ndistinct, be identical or be <strong>skew<\/strong>. Skew means that they\nare neither parallel nor have a point in common. This is impossible in a\nplane but entirely normal in three-dimensional space.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-mathematics-en/media/img/S_MAT_030_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Three possible relative positions of two lines, including skew lines in space.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: Two lines may intersect, be parallel or be skew in\nthree-dimensional space.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><strong>Mini-example of skew lines:<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>g<\/mi><mo>:<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>h<\/mi><mo>:<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo>;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\ng:\\quad X(t)&amp;=(t;0;0)\\\\\nh:\\quad X(s)&amp;=(0;s;1)\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘Line g has points with coordinates t,\nzero, zero; line h has points with coordinates zero, s, one.’ The first\nline is the x-axis at height zero. The second runs parallel to the\ny-axis at height one. Their directions are different, so they are not\nparallel. Yet a common point is impossible, since the third coordinate\non g is always zero and on h always one. The two lines are\n<strong>skew<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<p>For a genuine intersection, <strong>all three coordinate equations\nmust be satisfied with the same parameter values<\/strong>. Two\napparently matching coordinates are not enough.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Comparison with intersecting and parallel lines:<\/strong> The\nlines\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo>:<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g:\\ X(t)=(t;0;0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>:<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k:\\ X(s)=(0;s;0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nintersect at the origin when\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=s=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\nall three coordinates agree. By contrast,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>g<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>:<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo>;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m:\\ X(s)=(s;1;0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhave the same direction vector up to a scalar factor, but have no point\nin common because their second coordinates differ: they are\n<strong>parallel and distinct<\/strong>, not skew. Two lines would be\nidentical only if their direction vectors were parallel\n<strong>and<\/strong> a reference point of one lay on the other.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A line and a plane: substituting rather than guessing<\/h2>\n<p>If a plane is already given in Cartesian form, we can substitute the\ncoordinates of the line directly. We use:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>g<\/mi><mo>:<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>E<\/mi><mo>:<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>z<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\ng:\\quad X(t)&amp;=(0;0;1)+t\\cdot(1;1;2)\\\\\nE:\\quad z&amp;=5\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘Line g starts at zero, zero, one and\nruns in direction one, one, two; plane E consists of all points with\nz-coordinate five.’ The third coordinate of a point on the line is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=1+2t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAn intersection requires\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1+2t=5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nso\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSubstituting this into the equation of the line gives:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X(2)=(2;2;5)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘When t equals two, the point on the\nline is two, two, five.’ This point satisfies the plane equation and, by\nconstruction, lies on the line. We have therefore established the point\nof intersection.<\/p>\n<p>The possible cases are:<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>One point of intersection:<\/strong> The line crosses the\nplane at exactly one point.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>No point of intersection:<\/strong> The line is strictly\nparallel to the plane.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Infinitely many points of intersection:<\/strong> The whole\nline lies in the plane.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>In an equation, we can often recognise these cases by whether there\nis exactly one solution for the line parameter, a contradiction or an\nidentity.<\/p>\n<p><strong>General decision test:<\/strong> For the line\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mover><mi>p<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>+<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X(t)=\\vec p+t\\cdot\\vec u<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>≠<\/mo><mover><mn>0<\/mn><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec u\\neq\\vec 0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand a plane with reference point\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>a<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{a}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand normal vector\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>n<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>≠<\/mo><mover><mn>0<\/mn><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec n\\neq\\vec 0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nsubstitution gives:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>n<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>⋅<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mover><mi>p<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>−<\/mo><mover><mi>a<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mover><mi>n<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>⋅<\/mo><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{n}\\cdot(\\vec{p}-\\vec{a})+t\\,(\\vec{n}\\cdot\\vec{u})=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘n dot p minus a plus t times n dot u is\nzero.’ If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>n<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>⋅<\/mo><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>≠<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec n\\cdot\\vec u\\neq0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthere is <strong>exactly one<\/strong> parameter value and hence one\npoint of intersection. If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>n<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>⋅<\/mo><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec n\\cdot\\vec u=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe other term decides: if also\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>n<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>⋅<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mover><mi>p<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>−<\/mo><mover><mi>a<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec n\\cdot(\\vec p-\\vec a)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe whole line lies in the plane; otherwise, the line and plane are\n<strong>strictly parallel<\/strong>. These are the three possible\ncases.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Distances: the shortest route<\/h2>\n<p>Between two points, the straight-line connection is the shortest\nroute. Between a point and a plane, the shortest connection is\n<strong>perpendicular to the plane<\/strong>. This explains why we can\nuse the normal vector.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"abschnitt-7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Point to point<\/h3>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A(1;2;3)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B(4;6;3)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe connecting vector is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/mrow><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\overrightarrow{AB}=(3;4;0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAs in <a href=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L11&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L11<\/a>, this gives the\ndistance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">‖<\/mo><mover><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/mrow><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">‖<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lVert\\overrightarrow{AB}\\rVert=5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nunits of length.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"abschnitt-8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Point to plane<\/h3>\n<p>Let a plane be given by a reference point with position vector\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>p<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{p}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand a non-zero normal vector\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>n<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{n}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nLet the point under consideration have position vector\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>q<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{q}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mover><mi>n<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>⋅<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mover><mi>q<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>−<\/mo><mover><mi>p<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">‖<\/mo><mover><mi>n<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">‖<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mfrac><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d(Q,E)=\\frac{\\lvert\\vec{n}\\cdot(\\vec{q}-\\vec{p})\\rvert}{\\lVert\\vec{n}\\rVert}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read in full:<\/strong> ‘The distance from point Q to plane E\nis the absolute value of the scalar product of normal vector n and the\nconnecting vector from reference point p to point q, divided by the\nlength of the normal vector.’ The absolute value symbol\n<code>|...|<\/code> ensures that the distance is not negative.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Mini-example:<\/strong> Plane\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo>:<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mi>z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E:\\ z=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the horizontal plane through the origin. Its normal vector is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(0;0;1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor point\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q(1;2;3)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe scalar product with the normal vector gives three. The length of the\nnormal vector is one, so the distance is <strong>three units of\nlength<\/strong>. This agrees with our intuition: the height of the point\nabove the plane is three. For the sloping plane\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z-y=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfrom <a href=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L12&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L12<\/a> and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q(1;0;0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nhowever, with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>p<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{p}=(1;0;2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>n<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{n}=(0;-1;1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe obtain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{\\lvert-2\\rvert}{\\sqrt2}=\\sqrt2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nHere the mere difference between the z-coordinates would\n<strong>not<\/strong> give the perpendicular distance.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Important note:<\/strong> The formula does not depend on the\narbitrary length of the chosen normal vector. If we multiply\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>n<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{n}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nby a non-zero factor, the numerator and denominator change accordingly\nand the distance remains the same.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"abschnitt-9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Point to line – an accessible preview<\/h3>\n<p>For a line, the shortest connecting vector is perpendicular to its\ndirection. We therefore split the connecting vector from a point on the\nline to Q into a component <strong>along<\/strong> the line and a\ncomponent <strong>across<\/strong> it. The perpendicular component is the\nshortest connection we are looking for.<\/p>\n<p>With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>a<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{a}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nas the position vector of the reference point on the line,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{u}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nas the non-zero direction vector and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>q<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{q}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nas the position vector of Q, the projection onto the line is:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mover><mi>w<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mover><mi>q<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>−<\/mo><mover><mi>a<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">proj<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mover><mi>w<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mover><mi>w<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>⋅<\/mo><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><mrow><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>⋅<\/mo><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>⋅<\/mo><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n\\vec w&amp;=\\vec q-\\vec a\\\\\n\\operatorname{proj}_{\\vec u}(\\vec w)&amp;=\\frac{\\vec w\\cdot\\vec u}{\\vec u\\cdot\\vec u}\\cdot\\vec u\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘Vector w is q minus a. The projection\nof w onto u is the scalar product of w and u divided by the scalar\nproduct of u with itself, multiplied by vector u.’ <code>proj<\/code> is\nshort for <strong>projection<\/strong>. The remaining vector\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>w<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">proj<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mover><mi>w<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec w-\\operatorname{proj}_{\\vec u}(\\vec w)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis perpendicular to the line; its length is the distance from the point\nto the line.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Numerical example:<\/strong> For the x-axis with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>a<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{a}=(0;0;0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{u}=(1;0;0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand point\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q(0;2;0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe have\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>w<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{w}=(0;2;0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe scalar product with u is zero; the projection is therefore zero and\nthe distance is <strong>two<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Non-trivial projection:<\/strong> For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>a<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{a}=(0;0;0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{u}=(1;1;0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q(2;0;0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe have\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>w<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{w}=(2;0;0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSince\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>w<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>⋅<\/mo><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{w}\\cdot \\vec{u}=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>⋅<\/mo><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{u}\\cdot \\vec{u}=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe obtain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">proj<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mover><mi>w<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{proj}_{\\vec u}(\\vec w)=(1;1;0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe remaining vector\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1;-1;0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsatisfies\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>⋅<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1;-1;0)\\cdot(1;1;0)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand has length\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d(Q,g)=\\sqrt2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nunits of length; the foot of the perpendicular is at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1;1;0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Systems of linear equations behind the geometry<\/h2>\n<p>When we find the intersection of two lines or of a line and a plane,\nwe obtain several linear equations for the unknown parameters. We\nalready solved such systems algebraically in M1-L9. In space, three\ncoordinates can produce several simultaneous conditions; sometimes they\ncontradict one another, and sometimes a condition is redundant.<\/p>\n<p><strong>An accessible worked example:<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mn>2<\/mn><mi>s<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>s<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n2s+t&amp;=5\\\\\ns-t&amp;=1\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘Twice s plus t is five; s minus t is\none.’ Adding the two equations eliminates\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3s=6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nso\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe second equation then gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nBoth values satisfy the first equation: four plus one is five. Such\nvalues could be parameters of a spatial intersection problem.<\/p>\n<p>Not every system of equations has exactly one solution. The\ngeometrical interpretation is often particularly helpful: no\nintersection corresponds to a system with no solution, while a shared\nline can be associated with infinitely many <strong>parameter\nsolutions<\/strong>. The number of possible solutions depends on the\nparticular system.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Matrices: readable shorthand<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>matrix<\/strong> is, first of all, a rectangular table of\nnumbers. Each row can, for example, contain the coefficients of an\nequation, and each column a coefficient of an unknown variable. For the\npreceding example, we write the system as:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>A<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>2<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo>;<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mover><mi>b<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>A<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mover><mi>b<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\nA&amp;=\\begin{bmatrix}2&amp;1\\\\1&amp;-1\\end{bmatrix},\n\\quad\\vec x=(s;t),\\quad\\vec b=(5;1)\\\\\nA\\cdot\\vec x&amp;=\\vec b\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘A is the two-row matrix with first row\ntwo, one and second row one, minus one. Vector x contains s and t.\nVector b contains five and one. Matrix A times vector x equals vector\nb.’<\/p>\n<p>The shorthand <code>[2 1; 1 −1]<\/code> means <strong>two\nrows<\/strong>; the semicolon separates the rows here. The matrix-vector\nproduct forms the scalar product of the coefficients in each row with\nthe vector of unknowns. This reproduces precisely our two equations. In\nthis example, the system gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{x}=(2;1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe coefficient matrix has determinant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\cdot(-1)-1\\cdot1=-3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSince this is non-zero, the solution here is unique. If the determinant\nwere zero, however, a square system could have <strong>no solutions or\ninfinitely many<\/strong>; the determinant alone does not distinguish\nthese cases.<\/p>\n<p><strong>A preview rather than a claim about compulsory\ncontent:<\/strong> At university, matrices are studied extensively as\nlinear maps, transformations and mathematical objects used in\ncalculations. Here they merely provide a bridge from school-level\nsystems of equations to later linear algebra.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is it useful for?<\/h2>\n<p>Camera systems use line-plane intersections to determine where a\nviewing ray strikes a virtual surface. Robotics uses intersection and\ndistance calculations for collision warnings and obstacle planning. In\n3D CAD, distances between points, lines and surfaces help with design\nand measurement. Large linear systems and matrices also occur in\noptimisation, simulation and machine learning, often involving numerical\napproximation methods.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The formulas describe straight lines, flat surfaces and\n<strong>Euclidean<\/strong> distance in Cartesian space.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A plane’s normal vector and a line’s direction vector must both be\n<strong>non-zero<\/strong>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">In three dimensions, lines can be skew; ‘not parallel’ alone is\ninsufficient to prove an intersection.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Distances from a point to a line refer to the <strong>infinite\nline<\/strong>. For a bounded line segment, an endpoint may be the\nclosest point.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Numerical calculations should take nearly parallel directions and\nrounding errors into account. A problem that can be solved exactly by\nalgebra may become unstable with finite computational precision.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Matrix notation is merely a representation of the system; it\nguarantees neither a unique solution nor good numerical\nconditioning.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S11\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Connection to the next lesson<\/h2>\n<p><strong><a href=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L14&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L14<\/a> – Additional\ngeometry topics for engineering<\/strong> explores the cross product,\ndeterminants and rotations in greater depth. After that, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L15&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L15<\/a> begins upper secondary\nprobability and statistics.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S12\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<p>No individual sources are listed separately for this lesson.<\/p>\n<\/article>","school_mathematics_en:M2-L14":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L14\">M2-L14 – Cross product, determinants and rotations in space<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M2 – Upper secondary education / Abitur<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Upper secondary education / Abitur<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> No fixed prerequisites\nspecified.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Context:<\/strong> Upper secondary education / technical\nsupplementary module as a bridge to university. Not designated as\ngenerally compulsory material for the Berlin Abitur.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is this about?<\/h2>\n<p>The scalar product from <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L11&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L11<\/a> answers the question of how\nstrongly two vectors point in the same direction. We now need a second,\ndifferent vector operation: it gives a direction\n<strong>perpendicular<\/strong> to two given directions. Determinants\nhelp us recognise changes in area and volume and the orientation of\ncoordinate systems. Rotation matrices, in turn, describe precisely how a\npoint or vector is rotated. These tools are particularly useful in 3D\ngraphics, camera technology and robotics.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Basic idea and visual intuition<\/h2>\n<p>Imagine two arrows starting at the same point. If they are not\nparallel, they span a plane. The <strong>cross product<\/strong> points\nout of this plane at right angles; its length equals the area of the\nparallelogram spanned by the arrows. For a square matrix, a\n<strong>determinant<\/strong> gives the signed factor by which areas or\nvolumes are changed by the corresponding linear transformation. A\n<strong>rotation<\/strong> turns a direction without changing its length\nin an ideal rotation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>New symbols before the first formula<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Symbol<\/th>\n<th>How to read it<\/th>\n<th>Meaning here<\/th>\n<th>Other possible uses<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{u}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{v}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘vector u’, ‘vector v’<\/td>\n<td>Arrows in space from <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L11&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L11<\/a><\/td>\n<td>Any directed quantities<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>×<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\times<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘cross’, cross product<\/td>\n<td>Forms a new vector from two vectors in three dimensions<\/td>\n<td>Also a multiplication sign in everyday texts; here\n<strong>not<\/strong> ordinary multiplication<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>×<\/mo><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec u\\times\\vec v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘u cross v’<\/td>\n<td>Vector perpendicular to u and v<\/td>\n<td>Order is crucial<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">det<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\det(A)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘determinant of A’<\/td>\n<td>Signed area/volume factor of a square matrix A<\/td>\n<td>Other matrix properties, such as the trace, are different<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lvert A\\rvert<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘determinant of A’ in this context<\/td>\n<td>Alternative determinant notation<\/td>\n<td>Absolute value of a number or cardinality of a set; so prefer\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">det<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\det(A)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>[a b; c d]<\/code><\/td>\n<td>Matrix with two rows<\/td>\n<td>First row a, b; second row c, d<\/td>\n<td>The semicolon indicates a new row here<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>φ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R(\\varphi)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘rotation matrix R of phi’<\/td>\n<td>Rotation through angle phi<\/td>\n<td>R may otherwise denote any matrix<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>θ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘theta’<\/td>\n<td>Angle between two non-zero vectors<\/td>\n<td>Other angles in geometry<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">‖<\/mo><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">‖<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lVert\\vec{u}\\rVert<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘length of vector u’<\/td>\n<td>Euclidean vector length from <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L11&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L11<\/a><\/td>\n<td>Other norms are possible<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>R<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R^{\\mathrm T}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>I<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘R transposed’, ‘identity matrix I’<\/td>\n<td>Rows and columns interchanged; matrix that leaves vectors\nunchanged<\/td>\n<td>General matrix notation<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>φ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varphi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘phi’<\/td>\n<td>Angle of rotation here<\/td>\n<td>Any parameter elsewhere<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>φ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cos(\\varphi)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>φ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin(\\varphi)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘cosine phi’, ‘sine phi’<\/td>\n<td>Trigonometric functions familiar from <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L16&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L16<\/a>/M1-L20<\/td>\n<td>Always pay attention to the units used for angles<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A\\cdot\\vec v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘matrix A times vector v’<\/td>\n<td>Linear transformation, here a rotation<\/td>\n<td>Not component-wise multiplication<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>−<\/code><\/td>\n<td>‘minus’<\/td>\n<td>Negative component or subtraction<\/td>\n<td>Reversal of orientation<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>Unless stated otherwise, the formulae use Cartesian,\n<strong>right-handed<\/strong> coordinate axes and three-dimensional\nvectors. An angle of 90° is the same as a quarter of a full turn; in\nradians, as introduced in <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L20&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L20<\/a>, it is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mfrac><mi>π<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac\\pi2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Cross product: the perpendicular direction<\/h2>\n<p>For two vectors in space with components\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>;<\/mo><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>;<\/mo><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec u=(u_1;u_2;u_3)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>;<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>;<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec v=(v_1;v_2;v_3)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe define:<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-mathematics-en/media/img/S_MAT_031_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Spatial axes with u equal to (1,0,0), v equal to (0,1,0), and cross product u cross v equal to (0,0,1) pointing upwards.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: The perpendicular unit vectors along x and y have a\ncross product pointing in the positive z-direction.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>The cross product of the x and y unit vectors points in the positive\nz direction.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>×<\/mo><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msub><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n\\vec u\\times\\vec v&amp;=\\begin{pmatrix}u_2v_3-u_3v_2\\\\u_3v_1-u_1v_3\\\\u_1v_2-u_2v_1\\end{pmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud in full:<\/strong> ‘u cross v is the vector whose\nfirst component is u two times v three minus u three times v two; whose\nsecond component is u three times v one minus u one times v three; and\nwhose third component is u one times v two minus u two times v one.’ The\nsubscripts one, two and three denote the components in the x, y and z\ndirections here.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Mini-example:<\/strong> Let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{u}=(1;0;0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{v}=(0;1;0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe first arrow points along the x-axis, the second along the y-axis.\nSubstitution gives:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>×<\/mo><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{u}\\times\\vec{v}=(0;0;1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘u cross v gives the vector zero, zero,\none.’ The result points along the positive z-axis and is perpendicular\nto both original vectors. Its direction follows the <strong>right-hand\nrule<\/strong>: when curling the fingers from the first towards the\nsecond vector, the thumb points in the direction of the cross product,\nprovided the coordinate system is right-handed.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"abschnitt-5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Order and area<\/h3>\n<p>The cross product is <strong>not commutative<\/strong>: reversing the\norder reverses the direction of the result.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>×<\/mo><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>×<\/mo><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{v}\\times\\vec{u}=-(\\vec{u}\\times\\vec{v})<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘v cross u is the negative of the vector\nu cross v.’ Interchanging the vectors in the example gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(0;0;-1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>The length of the cross product also reveals the area of the\nparallelogram:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Area<\/mtext><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">‖<\/mo><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>×<\/mo><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">‖<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\text{Area}=\\lVert\\vec{u}\\times\\vec{v}\\rVert<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘The area equals the length of the cross\nproduct vector.’ In the example the area is one. More generally, for\nnon-zero vectors with included angle\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>0<\/mn><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">°<\/mtext><\/msup><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><msup><mn>180<\/mn><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">°<\/mtext><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0^{\\text{°}}\\leq\\theta\\leq180^{\\text{°}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe also have\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">‖<\/mo><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>×<\/mo><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">‖<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">‖<\/mo><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">‖<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>⋅<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">‖<\/mo><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">‖<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>⋅<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lVert\\vec u\\times\\vec v\\rVert=\\lVert\\vec u\\rVert\\cdot\\lVert\\vec v\\rVert\\cdot\\sin(\\theta)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nread aloud: ‘length u times length v times the sine of the included\nangle.’ At 90° the area is greatest for fixed lengths; at 0° or 180° it\nis zero.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Second worked example:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{u}=(2;0;0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{v}=(1;3;0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngive\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>×<\/mo><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec u\\times\\vec v=(0;0;6)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe parallelogram therefore has area\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>6<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthe triangle with the same two edges has area\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>3<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nBoth values are non-negative, even though the cross product changes sign\nwhen the edges are interchanged.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Limits:<\/strong> The cross product in this usual form is\ndefined specifically for three dimensions. If the vectors are parallel\nor either is a zero vector, the cross product is the zero vector; no\nunique perpendicular orientation can then be specified. For the area of\na triangle, the parallelogram area must be halved.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Determinant: orientation and scaling<\/h2>\n<p>A matrix with two rows and two columns can describe a linear\ntransformation of the plane. Its <strong>determinant<\/strong> is a\nnumber. For the matrix\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>a<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>b<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>c<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>d<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=\\begin{bmatrix}a&amp;b\\\\c&amp;d\\end{bmatrix}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwe have:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">det<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\det(A)=a\\cdot d-b\\cdot c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘The determinant of A is a times d minus\nb times c.’ The letters\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>b<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>c<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>d<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare the four matrix entries. Unlike the cross product, the result is\n<strong>a number<\/strong>, not a vector.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Mini-example of stretching:<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>A<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>2<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>3<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">det<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\nA&amp;=\\begin{pmatrix}2&amp;0\\\\0&amp;3\\end{pmatrix}\\\\\n\\det(A)&amp;=2\\cdot3-0\\cdot0=6\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The transformation stretches an area by a factor of two in one\ncoordinate direction and by a factor of three in the other. Overall the\narea grows by a factor of six.<\/p>\n<p>What does the sign mean?<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Positive:<\/strong> Orientation is preserved by a\nnon-degenerate transformation.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Negative:<\/strong> Orientation is reversed, for example by\nreflection.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Zero:<\/strong> The transformation compresses area or volume\ninto a lower dimension; it is not invertible.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>The <strong>ordinary area scaling factor<\/strong> is the non-negative\nabsolute value\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">det<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lvert\\det(A)\\rvert<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthe determinant itself is <em>signed<\/em> and may be negative.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Check cases:<\/strong> The reflection\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S=\\begin{bmatrix}-1&amp;0\\\\0&amp;1\\end{bmatrix}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">det<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>S<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\det(S)=-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\nareas stay the same, but orientation is reversed. The matrix\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>2<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>2<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>4<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D=\\begin{bmatrix}1&amp;2\\\\2&amp;4\\end{bmatrix}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">det<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\det(D)=1\\cdot4-2\\cdot2=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\nit maps the plane onto a line in a non-invertible way. Positive\ndeterminants alone do not establish that a transformation is a rotation\neither.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"abschnitt-7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Extension to space: volume<\/h3>\n<p>In three dimensions, three vectors\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{u}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{v}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>w<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{w}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncan be regarded as edges of a parallelepiped. We then have:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">signed volume<\/mtext><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>⋅<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>×<\/mo><mover><mi>w<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">volume<\/mtext><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>⋅<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>×<\/mo><mover><mi>w<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{signed volume}&amp;=\\vec u\\cdot(\\vec v\\times\\vec w)\\\\\n\\text{volume}&amp;=\\left|\\vec u\\cdot(\\vec v\\times\\vec w)\\right|\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘The signed volume is the scalar product\nof u with the cross product of v and w. The ordinary, non-negative\nvolume is its absolute value.’ The first value corresponds to the\ndeterminant of the matrix whose columns are these three vectors. If the\nvectors become linearly dependent, the volume vanishes. The sign depends\non the order: with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{u}=(2;0;0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{v}=(1;3;0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>w<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{w}=(0;0;-2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe scalar triple product\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>⋅<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>×<\/mo><mover><mi>w<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>12<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec u\\cdot(\\vec v\\times\\vec w)=-12<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthe actual volume is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>12<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">12<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nInterchanging two edges changes only the sign of the signed volume.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Rotation as matrix multiplication<\/h2>\n<p>If we rotate points about the origin of a two-dimensional plane\nanticlockwise through angle\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>φ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varphi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\na rotation matrix is sufficient:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>φ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>φ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>−<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>φ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>φ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>φ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R(\\varphi)=\\begin{pmatrix}\\cos(\\varphi)&amp;-\\sin(\\varphi)\\\\\\sin(\\varphi)&amp;\\cos(\\varphi)\\end{pmatrix}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud in full:<\/strong> ‘R of phi is the matrix with\nfirst row cosine of phi and minus sine of phi, and second row sine of\nphi and cosine of phi.’ A matrix maps the two coordinates of an input to\ntwo new coordinates.<\/p>\n<p>A point or position vector\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{v}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis rotated by multiplication\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>φ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>⋅<\/mo><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R(\\varphi)\\cdot\\vec v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\n<strong>Mini-example:<\/strong> The vector\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1;0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbecomes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(0;1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nafter rotating anticlockwise through 90 degrees, because\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mn>90<\/mn><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">°<\/mtext><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cos(90^{\\text{°}})=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mn>90<\/mn><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">°<\/mtext><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin(90^{\\text{°}})=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>R<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mn>90<\/mn><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">°<\/mtext><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>R<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mn>90<\/mn><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">°<\/mtext><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\nR(90^{\\text{°}})&amp;=\\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;-1\\\\1&amp;0\\end{pmatrix}\\\\\nR(90^{\\text{°}})\\begin{pmatrix}1\\\\0\\end{pmatrix}&amp;=\\begin{pmatrix}0\\\\1\\end{pmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘The rotation matrix for ninety degrees\nturns the unit vector in the x direction into the unit vector in the y\ndirection.’ The calculation is not a translation: the origin remains in\nplace.<\/p>\n<p>A proper rotation has determinant one and preserves lengths, angles\nand areas. <strong>However, determinant one alone is not sufficient to\nidentify a matrix as a rotation:<\/strong> a shear can also have\ndeterminant one even though it changes angles and lengths. A rotation\nmatrix must also be orthogonal, meaning its columns must have unit\nlength and be mutually perpendicular. In matrix notation this is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><\/msup><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>I<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R^{\\mathrm T}\\cdot R=I<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(read aloud: ‘R transposed times R is the identity matrix’). Together\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">det<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\det(R)=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthis characterises a proper rotation. <strong>Counterexample:<\/strong>\nThe shear matrix\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H=\\begin{bmatrix}1&amp;1\\\\0&amp;1\\end{bmatrix}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">det<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\det(H)=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nbut maps\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(0;1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1;1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\nthe length increases from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>1<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA determinant of one is therefore not sufficient. The rotation matrix\napplies to an <strong>active rotation of vectors<\/strong> in a fixed\ncoordinate system. If the coordinate system changes instead, the reverse\nrotation is often needed. In screen coordinates the y-axis often points\ndownwards; a positive rotation may therefore look different from the\nusual mathematical coordinate system.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Bridge to 3D graphics and robotics<\/h2>\n<p>A camera needs to know which directions mean ‘right’, ‘up’ and\n‘forward’. From two suitable non-parallel directions, the cross product\ncan provide a third direction perpendicular to both. However, the\noriginal directions are not automatically perpendicular to each other;\nfor an <strong>orthonormal<\/strong> camera coordinate system, they must\nalso be suitably orthogonalised and all axes normalised. Rotation\nmatrices describe the orientation of a robot joint or camera.\nDeterminants help to check whether a transformation preserves\norientation, reflects or degenerates.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Important distinction:<\/strong> A rotation and a translation\n(shift) are not the same thing. For pure rotations about the origin the\nmatrices shown here are sufficient. To combine rotations and\ntranslations, 3D systems later often use <strong>homogeneous\ncoordinates<\/strong> and larger transformation matrices. This technique\nbelongs to later university-level study of robotics and is not needed to\nunderstand this lesson.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is it useful for?<\/h2>\n<p>In a ray tracer or a 3D engine, a surface normal can be found from\ntwo edges of a triangle. In a CAD tool the determinant of a\ntransformation reveals a reflection or loss of area. In robotics,\nrotations are fundamental to comparing sensor data, gripper positions\nand camera directions between coordinate systems. In numerical software,\nparticular care is needed with nearly parallel vectors and rounding\nerrors.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limits<\/h2>\n<p>All examples refer to Euclidean coordinates with consistent units.\nThe cross product is not a universal multiplication of arbitrary\nvectors; its result depends on the orientation of the space used. The\ndeterminant requires a <strong>square matrix<\/strong>, and a small\nabsolute value of the determinant alone does <strong>not<\/strong>\nestablish poor conditioning: even uniform scaling can make it small.\nHowever, with matrix columns of comparable scale it can warn of near\nlinear dependence. In practice, rotation matrices are often estimated\nfrom noisy measurements; such matrices may deviate slightly from an\nideal rotation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Connection to the next lesson<\/h2>\n<p><a href=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L15&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L15<\/a> moves from geometrical\nmodels to <strong>probability models<\/strong>. It is no longer about the\ndirection of a vector, but about how likely various outcomes are under\nparticular assumptions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S11\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<p>No individual sources are listed separately for this lesson.<\/p>\n<\/article>","school_mathematics_en:M2-L15":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L15\">M2-L15 – Chance, conditional probability and the binomial distribution<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M2 – Upper secondary education / Abitur<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Upper secondary education / Abitur<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> No fixed prerequisites\nspecified.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Context:<\/strong> Upper secondary education (Gymnasium) ·\nprobability and statistics<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is this about?<\/h2>\n<p>With a die, a quality test or the response of an AI classifier, it is\noften uncertain which outcome will occur next. However, we can describe\nwhich outcomes are possible and how likely they are under a particular\nmodel. It is particularly important to know whether events influence one\nanother. Finally, the binomial distribution helps describe the number of\nsuccesses in a fixed sequence of similar trials. To do this, we combine\nthe foundations from <a href=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L12&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L12<\/a> and\n<a href=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L22&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L22<\/a> into a common\nmathematical language.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Basic idea and visual intuition<\/h2>\n<p>A probability is not a prediction that must come true in every\nindividual trial. It describes the behaviour of a <strong>probability\nmodel<\/strong>. A tree diagram makes several successive events visible;\nat each branch we take account of the path so far. For the binomial\ndistribution we consider many repetitions of a simple yes-or-no\ndecision: ‘success’ or ‘failure’. The distribution tells us how likely\nzero, one, two or more successes are.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>New symbols before the first formula<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Symbol<\/th>\n<th>How to read it<\/th>\n<th>Meaning here<\/th>\n<th>Other possible uses<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘omega’, sample space<\/td>\n<td>All possible elementary outcomes of the experiment<\/td>\n<td>Other technical symbols, e.g. ohm<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>B<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘event A’, ‘event B’<\/td>\n<td>Sets of outcomes<\/td>\n<td>Geometrical points, matrices<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(A)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘probability of A’<\/td>\n<td>Number from zero to one<\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>P<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncan otherwise denote a point<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>∩<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A\\cap B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘A intersection B’<\/td>\n<td>Both events occur<\/td>\n<td>Intersection of any sets<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>∪<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A\\cup B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘A union B’<\/td>\n<td>At least one of the events occurs<\/td>\n<td>Union of any sets<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>A<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A^{\\mathrm c}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘complement of A’<\/td>\n<td>A does not occur<\/td>\n<td>Superscript c is <strong>not an exponent<\/strong> here<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(B\\mid A)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘probability of B given A’<\/td>\n<td>Probability after learning that A occurred<\/td>\n<td>Vertical bar in other contexts: absolute value, divisibility<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘n’<\/td>\n<td>Number of repetitions<\/td>\n<td>General number, index<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘k’<\/td>\n<td>Number of observed successes<\/td>\n<td>Proportionality factor elsewhere<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘p’<\/td>\n<td>Probability of success in one trial<\/td>\n<td>Also a model parameter<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1-p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘one minus p’<\/td>\n<td>Probability of failure<\/td>\n<td>General complementary event<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mi>!<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n!<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘n factorial’<\/td>\n<td>Product of all positive integers up to n;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mi>!<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0!=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td><code>!<\/code> often means logical NOT in programs<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C(n,k)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n/ <code>n choose k<\/code><\/td>\n<td>‘n choose k’, binomial coefficient<\/td>\n<td>Number of ways of placing k successes in n positions<\/td>\n<td>Notation with two numbers written one above the other<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>X<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘random variable X’<\/td>\n<td>Number of successes in n trials<\/td>\n<td>Position coordinate or arbitrary variable elsewhere<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(X=k)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘probability that X equals k’<\/td>\n<td>Exactly k successes<\/td>\n<td>General probability of a random variable<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The vertical bar <code>∣<\/code> in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(B\\mid A)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n<strong>means ‘given A’ here<\/strong>, not division. The notation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>A<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A^{\\mathrm c}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nassumes that the sample space of the experiment has been clearly\ndefined.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Events and basic rules<\/h2>\n<p>An <strong>event<\/strong> is a collection of possible outcomes. When\nrolling a fair die once,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmight be the event ‘even number’. It then includes 2, 4 and 6, and the\nprobability is one half.<\/p>\n<p>Two types of relationship are important:<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>∩<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A\\cap B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\n<strong>Both A and B<\/strong> occur.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>∪<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A\\cup B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\n<strong>A or B or both<\/strong> occur; the ‘or’ is inclusive.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>The complementary probability is:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>A<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(A^{\\mathrm{c}})=1-P(A)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘The probability that A does not occur\nis one minus the probability of A.’ If a test gives a positive result\nwith a probability of 0.9, the complementary event has a probability of\n0.1 in the same model. This does not automatically mean that the test is\n90 per cent <strong>correct<\/strong>: what counts as ‘positive’ and what\ncounts as ‘correct’ must be defined separately.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Conditional probability: what do we already know?<\/h2>\n<p>Sometimes we have additional information before asking the next\nquestion. Of all the possible cases, we then consider only those in\nwhich the known condition is satisfied.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>∩<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mfrac><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(B\\mid A)=\\frac{P(A\\cap B)}{P(A)}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud in full:<\/strong> ‘The probability of B given A is\nthe probability of A and B occurring together, divided by the\nprobability of A.’ <strong>Requirement:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(A)&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nso the condition must have a positive probability in the model.<\/p>\n<p>Rearranging gives the <strong>multiplication rule<\/strong> for a\nprobability tree:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>∩<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(A\\cap B)=P(A)\\cdot P(B\\mid A)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘The probability of A and B together is\nthe probability of A times the conditional probability of B after A has\noccurred.’ The two numbers along a path through the tree are multiplied;\nalternative, disjoint paths to the same goal are added.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Mini-example without replacement:<\/strong> A box contains\nthree red and two blue counters. We draw two counters in succession\nwithout putting the first one back. The probability of ‘red twice’\nis:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">red first<\/mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>3<\/mn><mn>5<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">red next<\/mtext><mo>∣<\/mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">red<\/mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>2<\/mn><mn>4<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">red twice<\/mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>3<\/mn><mn>5<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>⋅<\/mo><mfrac><mn>2<\/mn><mn>4<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>3<\/mn><mn>10<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\nP(\\text{red first})&amp;=\\frac35\\\\\nP(\\text{red next}\\mid\\text{red})&amp;=\\frac24\\\\\nP(\\text{red twice})&amp;=\\frac35\\cdot\\frac24=\\frac3{10}\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘The probability of drawing red twice is\nthree fifths times two quarters, or three tenths.’ The second fraction\nchanges because after the first red counter, only two red counters\nremain out of four.<\/p>\n<p>With replacement, the second fraction would be three fifths again,\ngiving\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>9<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>25<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">9/25<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis shows why the <strong>assumptions<\/strong> about the experiment are\ncrucial.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Independence is a separate assumption<\/h2>\n<p>Two events are independent if information about one does not change\nthe probability of the other. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(A)&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthis can be expressed as follows, for example:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(B\\mid A)=P(B)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘The probability of B given A is the\nsame as the probability of B without any additional condition.’ In this\ncase the joint probability simplifies to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>∩<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(A\\cap B)=P(A)\\cdot P(B)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis <strong>product equation<\/strong> is the general criterion for\nindependence, even when\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(A)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the conditional probability\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(B\\mid A)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntherefore cannot be defined.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Check example to distinguish independence from mutual\nexclusivity:<\/strong> For a fair die, let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=\\{1\\}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B=\\{2\\}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nBoth have probability\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nbut they can never occur together:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>∩<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(A\\cap B)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhereas\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>36<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(A)\\cdot P(B)=\\frac1{36}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe events are therefore mutually exclusive <strong>and\ndependent<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Do not confuse:<\/strong> Independent events are not the same\nas <strong>mutually exclusive<\/strong> events. Two events that cannot\noccur together are not independent if their individual probabilities are\npositive.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Combinatorics: in which order do successes occur?<\/h2>\n<p>If we want to count in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntrials exactly\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsuccesses, we must consider <strong>which positions<\/strong> the\nsuccesses occupy. This number is the binomial coefficient:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mi>!<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mi>!<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>!<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C(n,k)=\\frac{n!}{k!\\cdot(n-k)!}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud in full:<\/strong> ‘n choose k is n factorial\ndivided by the product of k factorial and n minus k factorial.’ The\nrequirements are integers\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n\\geq 0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\leq k\\leq n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Mini-example:<\/strong> In three trials, exactly one success\ncan occur in the first, second or third position. There are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C(3,1)=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\narrangements. The order is therefore important for counting, even though\neach of these three arrangements has the same probability for identical\nindependent trials.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Binomial distribution: exactly k successes<\/h2>\n<p>A sequence of trials follows a <strong>binomial model<\/strong> if\nfour conditions hold: there is a fixed number\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nof trials; each trial has exactly two possible categories; the trials\nare independent; and the probability of success\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nremains constant.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-mathematics-en/media/img/S_MAT_032_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Discrete binomial distribution for six trials\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: Binomial distribution for six independent trials\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\nthree successes have the highest probability,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>20<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>64<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">20/64<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>For the number\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>X<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nof successes we then have:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>C<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mi>p<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msup><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(X=k)=C(n,k)\\cdot p^k\\cdot{(1-p)}^{n-k}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud in full:<\/strong> ‘The probability of exactly k\nsuccesses is n choose k times p to the power k times one minus p to the\npower n minus k.’ The binomial coefficient counts the different\narrangements.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>p<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p^k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\naccounts for the k successes and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">{(1-p)}^{n-k}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor the remaining failures. The permitted values are integers\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n\\geq 0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nintegers\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfrom zero to n, and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\leq p\\leq 1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nOutside this range of success counts,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(X=k)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthe boundary cases\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare degenerate distributions with certainly zero or certainly n\nsuccesses, respectively.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Worked mini-example:<\/strong> An independently repeated test\nproduces a success in each trial with probability\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=0.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntrials we ask for <strong>exactly one success<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0\"><mn>3<\/mn><mn>1<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.64<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.384<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>38.4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n\\binom31&amp;=3\\\\\nP(X=1)&amp;=3\\cdot0.2\\cdot{(1-0.2)}^2\\\\\n&amp;=3\\cdot0.2\\cdot0.64\\\\\n&amp;=0.384=38.4\\,\\%\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘The probability of exactly one success\nin three independent trials is thirty-eight point four per cent.’ This\nis not the probability of <strong>at least one<\/strong> success: we\nwould also need to include two and three successes.<\/p>\n<p>A compact way to calculate ‘at least one success’ uses the\ncomplementary event ‘no successes’:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>n<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(X\\geq1)=1-{(1-p)}^n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘At least one success is one minus the\nprobability that all n trials fail.’ Here the\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>≥<\/mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\geq<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsymbol means ‘greater than or equal to’ and includes one, two and three\nsuccesses. <strong>For the numbers above<\/strong>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=0.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthis gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0.512<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.488<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(X\\geq1)=1-{(0.8)}^3=1-0.512=0.488<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nor <strong>48.8%<\/strong>. As a check: the cases with exactly one, two\nand three successes have probabilities\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0.384<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.384<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0.096<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.096<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0.008<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.008<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\ntheir sum is also\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0.488<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.488<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nConfusing <strong>exactly<\/strong> one success with <strong>at\nleast<\/strong> one success would make a difference of 10.4 percentage\npoints here.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Where the models can fail in everyday life<\/h2>\n<p>A computer can simulate any number of die rolls, but only\n<strong>under the assumptions we program into it<\/strong>. A classifier,\nfor example, can make correlated errors on similar images: its test\nresults are then not independent. The success rate may also change with\ndifferent types of images, lighting or data distributions. Such\ndeviations violate the simple binomial model.<\/p>\n<p>Nor should a high conditional success probability be confused with\nthe <strong>probability of the actual cause given a positive\nresult<\/strong>. For that, we need information about base rates and\nfalse positive rates (see <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L22&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L22<\/a>; later, Bayes’ theorem).<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is it useful for?<\/h2>\n<p>Tree diagrams can help structure chains of failures in software and\ntechnical systems. The binomial distribution allows us to make rough\nplans for how many successful results to expect from repeated\nindependent tests. In quality assurance, probabilities of rare faults\ncan be calculated. In AI systems, it is particularly important to check\nwhether data points really are independent and probabilities of success\nremain stable.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S11\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limits<\/h2>\n<p>All the probabilities calculated refer to the probability models\nexplicitly stated in the text. Unknown distributions, changing success\nrates or dependent observations can alter the results. The binomial\ncoefficient counts possible arrangements but does not by itself say\nanything about their probabilities. With real data, the population, data\ncollection method and selection bias must be examined in particular.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S12\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Connection to the next lesson<\/h2>\n<p><a href=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L16&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L16<\/a> considers not only the\nprobabilities of individual success counts but also summarises entire\ndistributions with <strong>expected value, variance and standard\ndeviation<\/strong>. We then learn about normal distributions and the\nlogic of statistical hypothesis tests.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S13\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<p>No individual sources are listed separately for this lesson.<\/p>\n<\/article>","school_mathematics_en:M2-L16":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L16\">M2-L16 – Random variables, expected value, spread and tests<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M2 – Upper secondary education / Abitur<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Upper secondary education / Abitur<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> No fixed prerequisites\nspecified.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Context:<\/strong> Upper secondary education (Gymnasium) ·\nprobability and statistics; supplementary advanced material is\nmarked.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is this about?<\/h2>\n<p>After <a href=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L15&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L15<\/a>, we can\ncalculate how likely a particular number of successes is. Now we want to\nsummarise entire probability distributions: what value can we expect on\naverage? How much do the results vary? And how can we check whether\nobserved data are consistent with a particular assumption? These\nquestions lead to expected value, variance, standard deviation, the\nnormal distribution and statistical tests. It remains important that\nstatistics gives conclusions <strong>under modelling\nassumptions<\/strong>, not certainty about an individual future case.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Basic idea and visual intuition<\/h2>\n<p>Imagine repeating the same type of experiment very often. The\nobserved numbers vary, but show characteristic patterns in their\nfrequency distribution. The <strong>expected value<\/strong> describes\nthe mathematical centre of a probability distribution; the\n<strong>variance<\/strong> and <strong>standard deviation<\/strong>\ndescribe the spread around this centre. A <strong>normal\ndistribution<\/strong> is a continuous, bell-shaped model for some\nmeasurement and summation effects. Finally, with a <strong>hypothesis\ntest<\/strong>, we ask how unusual data would be <strong>if a particular\nassumption about how they arose were true<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>New symbols before the first formula<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Symbol<\/th>\n<th>How to read it<\/th>\n<th>Meaning here<\/th>\n<th>Other possible uses<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>X<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘random variable X’<\/td>\n<td>Assigns a number to each outcome of a trial<\/td>\n<td>Position coordinate or arbitrary variable<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘x with subscript i’<\/td>\n<td>The i-th possible numerical value<\/td>\n<td>The subscript can also be a measurement number<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(X=x_i)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘probability that X equals x i’<\/td>\n<td>Probability of a particular discrete value<\/td>\n<td>For continuous variables a single point usually has probability\nzero<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>∑<\/mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sum<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘capital sigma’, summation sign<\/td>\n<td>Add the specified individual terms<\/td>\n<td>Sum over other indices or sets<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E[X]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘expected value of X’<\/td>\n<td>Probability-weighted mean<\/td>\n<td>E can also name an event or plane<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>μ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘mu’<\/td>\n<td>Expected value / mean of the distribution<\/td>\n<td>Another statistical model parameter<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Var<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{Var}(X)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘variance of X’<\/td>\n<td>Mean squared deviation from the expected value<\/td>\n<td>Variance of other quantities<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>σ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n/\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>σ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘sigma’ / ‘sigma squared’<\/td>\n<td>Standard deviation / variance<\/td>\n<td>Sigma can also be a spread parameter<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>√<\/code><\/td>\n<td>‘square root’<\/td>\n<td>Non-negative square root; from <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L7<\/a><\/td>\n<td>General root function<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>∼<\/mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sim<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘is distributed as’<\/td>\n<td>Distribution notation in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X<\/mi><mo>∼<\/mo><mi>N<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>μ<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><msup><mi>σ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X\\sim N(\\mu,\\sigma^2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>In <a href=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L11&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L11<\/a> it means\ngeometrical similarity<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>μ<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><msup><mi>σ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N(\\mu,\\sigma^2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘normal distribution with mu and sigma squared’<\/td>\n<td>Continuous distribution with mean\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>μ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand variance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>σ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>Other literature may name the parameters differently<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>z<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘zed’<\/td>\n<td>Standardised measurement<\/td>\n<td>Spatial z-coordinate<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘H zero’, ‘H one’<\/td>\n<td>Null and alternative hypotheses<\/td>\n<td>Subscripts identify different hypotheses<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>α<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘alpha’<\/td>\n<td>Significance level set in advance<\/td>\n<td>Angle or another parameter<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>p-value<\/code><\/td>\n<td>‘p-value’<\/td>\n<td>Probability of data at least as extreme under a precisely specified\nnull model<\/td>\n<td>Not to be confused with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nas the probability of success from <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L15&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L15<\/a><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>≥<\/mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\geq<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘greater than or equal to’<\/td>\n<td>Includes the specified boundary<\/td>\n<td>General comparison<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The summation sign in this chapter means: for every permitted\npossibility we calculate the relevant value and then add all the\ncontributions. A statistical quantity may describe a <strong>sample\nmean<\/strong> from observed data or a <strong>theoretical expected\nvalue<\/strong> of a model. These are different quantities, even if the\nnumbers look similar.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Random variables: translating outcomes into numbers<\/h2>\n<p>A random variable does not itself have to ‘be random’ like an\nuncontrolled variable in a program. It is a <strong>specified\nmapping<\/strong>: a number is assigned to every possible outcome of the\nrandom experiment according to a clear rule.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Mini-example:<\/strong> We toss two independent, fair coins\nand count how often heads appears. The random variable\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>X<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncan take the values zero, one and two. Its probability table is:<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>X<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n= number of heads<\/th>\n<th><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(X=x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/th>\n<th>Explanation<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>0<\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>4<\/mn><\/mfrac><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac14<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>tails–tails<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1<\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac12<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>heads–tails or tails–heads<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2<\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>4<\/mn><\/mfrac><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac14<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>heads–heads<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The individual probabilities add up to one. This table is called a\n<strong>discrete probability distribution<\/strong>: we can list all\npossible values.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Expected value: where is the mathematical centre?<\/h2>\n<p>For a discrete random variable, we calculate the expected value by\nmultiplying every possible numerical value by its probability and adding\nthe products.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/munder><mo minsize=\"120%\" maxsize=\"120%\" stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo minsize=\"120%\" maxsize=\"120%\" stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E[X]=\\sum_i\\bigl(x_i\\cdot P(X=x_i)\\bigr)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud in full:<\/strong> ‘The expected value of X is the\nsum over all possible values x i, where each value x i is multiplied by\nthe probability that X takes exactly that value.’ Capital sigma is\ntherefore an instruction to add many weighted contributions.<\/p>\n<p>For the two coins we obtain:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>4<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>4<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E[X]=0\\cdot\\frac14+1\\cdot\\frac12+2\\cdot\\frac14=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘Zero times one quarter plus one times\none half plus two times one quarter equals one.’ The expected value is\n<strong>one head per two tosses<\/strong>. This does not mean that\nexactly one head occurs in every pair of tosses. The expected value is\nan average over the theoretical model, or over many independent\nrepetitions in the long run under suitable conditions.<\/p>\n<p>For a binomially distributed random variable with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntrials and probability of success\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe result is particularly simple:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E[X]=n\\cdot p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\n<strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘The expected number of successes is the\nnumber of trials times the probability of success.’ The symbols\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwere explained in M2-L15. In that example\n(<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=0.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>),\nthe theoretical expected value is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3\\cdot0.2=0.6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsuccesses; individual results are still whole numbers. <strong>Do not\nconfuse:<\/strong> A sample mean is calculated from actual measured\nvalues, whereas\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E[X]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncomes from a specified probability model.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Variance and standard deviation: how much does X vary?<\/h2>\n<p>Two distributions can have the same expected value but very different\nspreads. A mean alone does not show this. We therefore calculate the\nmean <strong>squared deviation<\/strong> of each value from the expected\nvalue\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>μ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Var<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/munder><mo minsize=\"120%\" maxsize=\"120%\" stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mi>μ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo minsize=\"120%\" maxsize=\"120%\" stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{Var}(X)=\\sum_i\\bigl[{(x_i-\\mu)}^2\\cdot P(X=x_i)\\bigr]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud in full:<\/strong> ‘The variance of X is the sum of\nthe squared distances of the possible values x i from the mean mu, each\nmultiplied by the probability of that value.’ Squaring prevents positive\nand negative deviations from simply cancelling when added. At first we\nalso obtain a <strong>squared unit<\/strong>, for example seconds\nsquared.<\/p>\n<p>The <strong>standard deviation<\/strong> brings the spread back to the\nunit of the original quantity:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Var<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma=\\sqrt{\\operatorname{Var}(X)}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘Sigma is the square root of the\nvariance of X.’ Sigma is non-negative. In this model, a small standard\ndeviation indicates more concentrated values, and a large one indicates\nmore widely spread values.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"abschnitt-7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Fully explained mini-example<\/h3>\n<p>Our two-coin distribution has mean\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu =1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nTherefore the deviations for the values zero, one and two are minus one,\nzero and plus one, respectively. Squaring gives one, zero and one.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Var<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>4<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>4<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>σ<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/msqrt><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.7071<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{Var}(X)&amp;=1\\cdot\\frac14+0\\cdot\\frac12+1\\cdot\\frac14=\\frac12\\\\\n\\sigma&amp;=\\sqrt{\\frac12}\\approx0.7071\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘The variance is one half; the standard\ndeviation is the square root of one half, or approximately zero point\nseven zero seven one.’ The mathematical spread is meaningful even though\nonly whole numbers of successes can occur in an individual trial.<\/p>\n<p>For a binomial distribution, the special formula is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Var<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{Var}(X)=n\\cdot p\\cdot(1-p)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\n<strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘The variance is n times p times one minus\np.’ It applies to the independent Bernoulli trials with the same\nprobability of success in the binomial model, not to just any sequence\nof tests. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=0.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe have\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Var<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.48<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{Var}(X)=3\\cdot0.2\\cdot0.8=0.48<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the standard deviation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mn>0.48<\/mn><\/msqrt><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.693<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{0.48}\\approx0.693<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis is the <strong>theoretical variance of the distribution<\/strong>,\nnot automatically the variance estimated from a small sample; different\nformulae are used for sample estimation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Normal distribution: the bell curve<\/h2>\n<p>The normal distribution is a <strong>continuous<\/strong> model. Most\nvalues lie near its mean, and extremely distant values become\nincreasingly rare towards the edges. Its shape is described by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>μ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>σ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe notation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X<\/mi><mo>∼<\/mo><mi>N<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>μ<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><msup><mi>σ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X\\sim N(\\mu,\\sigma^2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis read as ‘X is normally distributed with mean mu and variance sigma\nsquared’. The symbol\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>∼<\/mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sim<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmeans ‘is distributed as’ <strong>in this particular notation<\/strong>,\nnot the geometrical similarity from M1-L11.<\/p>\n<p>Values are often standardised:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>μ<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>σ<\/mi><\/mfrac><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=\\frac{x-\\mu}{\\sigma}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘The standardised value z is the\nobserved value x minus the mean mu, divided by the standard deviation\nsigma.’ A\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>z<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nof two means that the value in question lies two standard deviations\nabove the mean. The requirement is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma &gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Example:<\/strong> A measurement of 110 with a modelled mean\nof 100 and standard deviation of five corresponds to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>110<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><\/mrow><mn>5<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=\\frac{110-100}{5}=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>With the normal distribution, probabilities are determined for\n<strong>intervals<\/strong>, not for a single exact point. The area under\nthe density curve over an interval gives the probability of that\ninterval. The total area is one. For an ideal continuous normally\ndistributed random variable, any individual exact outcome has\nprobability <strong>zero<\/strong>, even though intervals can have\npositive probabilities. When\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma =0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthere is no longer a normal distribution with a bell-shaped density. Not\nevery real series of measurements is normally distributed; strong\nasymmetry, dependence, a few discrete values and outliers may call for\nother models.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Further study / outlook:<\/strong> For sufficiently large\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand values of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthat are not too extreme, a binomial distribution can be approximated by\na normal distribution. For small or very skewed samples this should not\nbe done uncritically; exact binomial calculation remains a natural\nchoice.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Hypothesis tests: what do data tell us about an\nassumption?<\/h2>\n<p>A statistical test begins with <strong>two competing\nstatements<\/strong>:<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(<em>null hypothesis<\/em>): A particular assumption about the model\nholds.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(<em>alternative hypothesis<\/em>): The departure from that assumption\nunder investigation holds.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>We decide in advance which data should count as particularly\ninconsistent with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWe also set the <strong>significance level<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>α<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\noften five per cent, for example. We then calculate a\n<strong>p-value<\/strong> from the data, which indicates how probable a\nresult at least as extreme would be <strong>under the null\nhypothesis<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"abschnitt-10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Mini-example with a test that can be checked<\/h3>\n<p>Under the null assumption, an automated procedure makes the correct\ndecision with probability\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0.5<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\non each independent trial. It is tested <strong>20 times<\/strong> and\nproduces <strong>15 correct results<\/strong>. We carry out a\n<em>one-sided<\/em> test of whether the probability of success is greater\nthan 0.5:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>:<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">probability of success <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>:<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">probability of success <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>n<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">observed <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mi>X<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>15<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\nH_0&amp;:\\ \\text{probability of success }p=0.5\\\\\nH_1&amp;:\\ \\text{probability of success }p&gt;0.5\\\\\nn&amp;=20,\\quad\\text{observed }X=15\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘Under H zero, the probability of\nsuccess is one half; under H one, it is greater than one half. In twenty\ntrials, fifteen successes are observed.’ Under\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe can use the binomial distribution from <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L15&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L15<\/a> and add the probabilities of\n<strong>15 or more<\/strong> successes:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">p-value<\/mtext><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>15<\/mn><mo>∣<\/mo><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0\"><mn>20<\/mn><mn>15<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>+<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0\"><mn>20<\/mn><mn>16<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>+<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0\"><mn>20<\/mn><mn>17<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>+<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0\"><mn>20<\/mn><mn>18<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>+<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0\"><mn>20<\/mn><mn>19<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>+<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0\"><mn>20<\/mn><mn>20<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mrow><msup><mn>2<\/mn><mn>20<\/mn><\/msup><\/mfrac><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>21<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>700<\/mn><\/mrow><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>048<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>576<\/mn><\/mrow><\/mfrac><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.0207<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.07<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{p-value}&amp;=P(X\\ge15\\mid H_0)\\\\\n&amp;=\\frac{\\binom{20}{15}+\\binom{20}{16}+\\binom{20}{17}+\\binom{20}{18}+\\binom{20}{19}+\\binom{20}{20}}{2^{20}}\\\\\n&amp;=\\frac{21\\,700}{1\\,048\\,576}\\\\\n&amp;\\approx 0.0207=2.07\\,\\%\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘Under the null hypothesis, the\nprobability of at least fifteen successes in twenty trials is\napproximately two point zero seven per cent.’ If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.05<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha =0.05<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwas set <strong>before<\/strong> the test, the p-value is below this\nthreshold. In this test we then reject\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin favour of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nTo check the <strong>one-sided<\/strong> decision: with only 14 successes\nthe upper binomial tail would be approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0.0577<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.0577<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand thus <strong>above<\/strong> 5%. The exact rejection region for this\ntest is therefore\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>15<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X\\geq 15<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf we instead used a two-sided test for a departure upwards\n<strong>or<\/strong> downwards, the p-value would be different; the test\nquestion must therefore be decided before the calculation.<\/p>\n<p>This does <strong>not<\/strong> mean that the null hypothesis is true\nwith a probability of just 2.07 per cent. Nor does it prove that the\nprocedure will perform better on all future data. Without random and\nrepresentative samples or when trials are dependent, the result can be\nmisleading.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Statistical uncertainty and typical misconceptions<\/h2>\n<p>A hypothesis test is a decision procedure with possible errors. A\n<strong>type I error<\/strong> occurs when we reject a null hypothesis\nthat actually holds. Under the test assumptions, the significance level\nset in advance limits the <strong>probability<\/strong> of this error\nover repeated similar tests to at most\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>α<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nBecause the number of successes is discrete, the actual error\nprobability in the above test at the null value\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis only about 2.07%, even though\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha=5\\,\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwas specified. A <strong>type II error<\/strong> occurs when we fail to\nreject a false null hypothesis; its probability depends, among other\nthings, on the actual effect and sample size.<\/p>\n<p>Likewise, <strong>not significant<\/strong> does not automatically\nmean ‘no effect’. And <strong>significant<\/strong> does not\nautomatically mean ‘large or important effect’. Additional information\ncomes, for example, from effect sizes, appropriate uncertainty intervals\nand a review of the data collection. If many tests are tried and only\nthe most striking ones are published, the risk of misleading findings\nincreases.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Further study:<\/strong> Confidence intervals, statistical\npower, multiple tests and Bayesian statistics are useful topics for\nfurther study. In the usual frequentist interpretation, a 95 per cent\nconfidence interval does not mean a 95 per cent probability that an\nalready fixed parameter lies in this particular calculated interval. It\ndescribes the long-run coverage rate of the procedure under the\nassumptions made.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is it useful for?<\/h2>\n<p>In software diagnostics, the standard deviation summarises the\nvariation in latencies or measurement errors, though often only after\nexamining the shape of the distribution. In AI benchmarks, expected\nvalues and uncertainties are useful for looking beyond a single success\nrate. Quality control uses statistical tests to distinguish systematic\nchanges from random fluctuations. In each of these cases, the\nmeasurement setup, independence and data selection must fit the\nmodel.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S11\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limits<\/h2>\n<p>Expected value and variance require the corresponding sums or\nintegrals to exist in the model used. A theoretical distribution model\nis not identical to an empirical sample. For tests, the question,\ndirection of the alternative, choice of model and significance level\nshould ideally be determined <strong>before<\/strong> inspecting the\nresults. A p-value is not a measure of the probability of a hypothesis\nand is no substitute for subject knowledge or reproducibility.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S12\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Connection to the next lesson<\/h2>\n<p><a href=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L17&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L17<\/a> provides the\n<strong>overall Abitur review<\/strong>: we relate calculus,\ngeometry/linear algebra and probability and statistics to one another,\nbringing the most important mathematical symbols together in a common\nreference section. University-level study then begins with mathematical\nlogic and reasoning through proofs.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S13\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<p>No individual sources are listed separately for this lesson.<\/p>\n<\/article>","school_mathematics_en:M2-L17":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L17\">M2-L17 – Abitur overview: three ways of thinking, one mathematical language<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M2 – Upper secondary education / Abitur<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Upper secondary education / Abitur<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> No fixed prerequisites\nspecified.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Context:<\/strong> Upper secondary education (Gymnasium),\nincluding marked advanced-course (Leistungskurs) and technical\nextensions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is this about?<\/h2>\n<p>Upper secondary mathematics is not a collection of unrelated\ncalculation recipes. Three major topic areas complement one another:\n<strong>calculus<\/strong> studies change, <strong>analytical\ngeometry<\/strong> describes spatial relationships, and\n<strong>probability and statistics<\/strong> model chance and\nuncertainty. Each area has characteristic questions and its own language\nof formulae. The aim of this final lesson is to recognise formulae while\nreading and choose the appropriate mathematical way of thinking, not to\nrecreate an Abitur examination.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Three mathematical perspectives<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Area<\/th>\n<th>Guiding question<\/th>\n<th>Typical objects<\/th>\n<th>Technical example<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Calculus (<a href=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L1<\/a>–<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L10&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L10<\/a>)<\/td>\n<td>How does something change, and what accumulates over time or\ndistance?<\/td>\n<td>Functions, derivatives, integrals<\/td>\n<td>Speed of a robot and distance travelled<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Analytical geometry (<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L11&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L11<\/a>–<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L14&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L14<\/a>)<\/td>\n<td>Where is something, which direction does it point in, and how far\napart are objects?<\/td>\n<td>Vectors, lines, planes, matrices<\/td>\n<td>Camera ray and the plane of a virtual wall<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Probability and statistics (<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L15&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L15<\/a>–<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L16&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L16<\/a>)<\/td>\n<td>What is likely under given conditions, and how uncertain is an\nestimate?<\/td>\n<td>Events, random variables, distributions<\/td>\n<td>Number of faulty test runs<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p><strong>Remember:<\/strong> A similar drawing can show a spatial path,\na function describing change over time, or the behaviour of a model\nquantity. For probability models, we must also clarify whether the curve\nrepresents measured data, a probability or a density. Which mathematics\nfits depends on the <strong>question and the quantities\nrepresented<\/strong>, not on the drawing alone.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The most important symbols before the summary formulae<\/h2>\n<p>The symbols are familiar from previous lessons; here they form a\nreference sheet that can be read directly. The same symbols may be used\ndifferently in other subjects.<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Symbol<\/th>\n<th>How to read it<\/th>\n<th>Meaning here<\/th>\n<th>Other possible uses<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘f of x’<\/td>\n<td>Function value for input x<\/td>\n<td>Any input-output mapping<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">lim<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lim<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘limit’<\/td>\n<td>Value that expressions approach<\/td>\n<td>Limits of sequences and series too<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x\\to a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘x tends to a’<\/td>\n<td>The input approaches a<\/td>\n<td>Arrows also occur in function mappings<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f&#39;(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘f prime of x’<\/td>\n<td>Derivative or local rate of change<\/td>\n<td>A prime may indicate a different variant elsewhere<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘d y by d x’<\/td>\n<td>Derivative of y with respect to x<\/td>\n<td>Notation also used later in differential equations<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>∫<\/mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘integral’<\/td>\n<td>Continuous accumulation<\/td>\n<td>Indefinite or definite integral<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{d}x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘d x’<\/td>\n<td>Integration variable or differential notation<\/td>\n<td>Differential in higher calculus<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>b<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\non the integral<\/td>\n<td>‘lower’ and ‘upper limit’<\/td>\n<td>Limits of the integration interval<\/td>\n<td>General variables<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{v}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘vector v’<\/td>\n<td>Directed quantity<\/td>\n<td>Often a velocity vector<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">‖<\/mo><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">‖<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lVert\\vec{v}\\rVert<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘magnitude/length of vector v’<\/td>\n<td>Euclidean length<\/td>\n<td>Other norms at university level<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>⋅<\/mo><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{u}\\cdot\\vec{v}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘scalar product of u and v’<\/td>\n<td>Calculates a number from two vectors<\/td>\n<td>A dot also denotes ordinary multiplication<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(A)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘probability of A’<\/td>\n<td>Probability of an event<\/td>\n<td>P may denote a point<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(A\\mid B)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘probability of A given B’<\/td>\n<td>Conditional probability<\/td>\n<td>A vertical bar can have other meanings<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E[X]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘expected value of X’<\/td>\n<td>Mean of a distribution in the model<\/td>\n<td>E may also be another variable<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>∑<\/mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sum<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘capital sigma’<\/td>\n<td>Sum of the specified terms<\/td>\n<td>Lowercase sigma is usually a measure of spread<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>σ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘sigma’<\/td>\n<td>Standard deviation<\/td>\n<td>General model parameter<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>≈<\/code><\/td>\n<td>‘approximately equal to’<\/td>\n<td>Rounded or approximate value<\/td>\n<td>Measurement and numerical approximation<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>A <strong>subscript<\/strong> is a lowered label, such as the\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>i<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(‘x with subscript i’). An <strong>exponent<\/strong> is raised, such as\nthe\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>2<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(‘x squared’). Both can refer to mathematical objects but perform\ndifferent tasks.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Calculus: change and accumulation<\/h2>\n<p>A function assigns a value to every permitted input. The derivative\ndescribes the local change at a point; the definite integral adds up the\nrate of change over an interval.<\/p>\n<p>Two central formulae to read:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">lim<\/mi><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><\/munder><mfrac><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>h<\/mi><\/mfrac><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f&#39;(x)=\\lim_{h\\to0}\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud in full:<\/strong> ‘f prime of x is the limit of\nthe quotient of f of x plus h minus f of x, divided by h, as h tends to\nzero.’ The\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>h<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nalready familiar from <a href=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L3<\/a> is a\nchange in input that tends to zero and is initially non-zero in the\ndivision. This is the <strong>definition<\/strong> of the (two-sided)\nderivative at an interior point of the domain, provided the finite limit\nexists; for the values\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo>≠<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h\\ne 0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nconsidered,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x+h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmust also lie in the domain. At boundary points, one-sided derivatives\nmust be defined separately.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msubsup><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">if <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><msup><mi>F<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int_a^b f(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x=F(b)-F(a)\\qquad\\text{if }F&#39;(x)=f(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud in full:<\/strong> ‘The definite integral of f of x\nwith respect to x from a to b gives the antiderivative F at the upper\nlimit b minus the antiderivative F at the lower limit a.’ Capital\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>F<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndenotes an antiderivative whose derivative is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nfor the simple fundamental theorem we need a continuous function on the\ninterval considered here.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Mini-example:<\/strong> A vehicle moves for two seconds at a\nconstant <strong>positive<\/strong> velocity of three metres per second.\nIt travels\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3\\,\\mathrm{m}/\\mathrm{s}\\cdot 2\\,\\mathrm{s}=6\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nas time in seconds, integrating the constant velocity function gives the\nsame value. If the direction changes, however, the signed integral\ninitially gives the <strong>displacement<\/strong>; the total distance\ntravelled requires integrating the <strong>absolute value<\/strong> of\nthe velocity.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Limit:<\/strong> Signed integration of velocity initially\ngives <strong>displacement<\/strong>; if direction changes, the actual\ndistance travelled is greater than or equal to the absolute value of the\ndisplacement.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Analytical geometry: position, direction, distance<\/h2>\n<p>A vector can describe a displacement or direction. Lines and planes\narise when reference points are combined with direction vectors and\nparameters. The scalar product compares directions and is also used to\ncalculate angles.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>⋅<\/mo><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">‖<\/mo><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">‖<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>⋅<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">‖<\/mo><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">‖<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>⋅<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>φ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{u}\\cdot\\vec{v}=\\lVert\\vec{u}\\rVert\\cdot\\lVert\\vec{v}\\rVert\\cdot\\cos(\\varphi)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud in full:<\/strong> ‘The scalar product of vectors u\nand v is equal to the length of u times the length of v times the cosine\nof the angle phi between them.’\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>φ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varphi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis read as ‘phi’; here it denotes the angle.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cos<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the cosine. The angle interpretation requires both vectors to be\nnon-zero.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Mini-example:<\/strong> The vectors\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1,0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(0,1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare perpendicular to each other in the plane coordinate system. Their\nscalar product is zero. If both vectors are non-zero, this shows that\nthe angle between them is 90 degrees.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Limit:<\/strong> Points and vectors are not the same: a point\ndenotes a location, whereas a direction vector denotes a displacement.\nThe distinctions between an infinite line and a finite line segment are\nalso essential in collision calculations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Probability and statistics: probabilities and\nuncertainty<\/h2>\n<p>In a probability model an event is assigned a number between zero and\none. Conditional probability describes how this assessment changes with\nadditional information.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>∩<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">for <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(A\\mid B)=\\frac{P(A\\cap B)}{P(B)}\\qquad\\text{for }P(B)&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud in full:<\/strong> ‘The probability of A given B is\nthe probability that A and B occur together, divided by the probability\nof B.’\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>∩<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cap<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmeans ‘intersection’, or for events ‘A and B together’.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>∣<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mid<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmeans ‘given’ here; B must have positive probability.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Mini-example:<\/strong> Of 100 equally weighted test files, 10\nare faulty. A checking program raises an alarm for 8 faulty and 9\ncorrect files. Of the 17 alarm cases, 8 are actual faults.\n<strong>If<\/strong> a file is selected with equal probability from these\n100 files, then\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>∣<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>8<\/mn><mn>17<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>47.1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(\\mathrm{fault}\\mid\\mathrm{alarm})=\\frac{8}{17}\\approx 47.1\\,\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nreversing the condition gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>∣<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>8<\/mn><mn>10<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>80<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(\\mathrm{alarm}\\mid\\mathrm{fault})=\\frac{8}{10}=80\\,\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWithout the assumption of equal probabilities, these fractions are\ninitially only <strong>relative frequencies<\/strong>, not universally\nvalid model probabilities. The distinction prevents confusion between\nthe detection rate and how informative an alarm is.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Limit:<\/strong> The modelled frequencies and probabilities\nmust fit the real population. A statistical test result is neither a\nguarantee about the future nor automatically the probability of a\nhypothesis.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>How do I choose the right area?<\/h2>\n<p>First ask the subject-specific question:<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Change or optimum?<\/strong> Derivatives, curve sketching and\noptimisation (calculus).<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Accumulated quantity from a rate of change?<\/strong>\nDefinite integral (calculus).<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Position, angle or collision?<\/strong> Vectors, lines and\nplanes (geometry).<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Uncertain success counts and significance?<\/strong>\nDistributions and statistical tests (probability and statistics).<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>In practice, several are often needed at the same time. A robot has a\nspatial position (<strong>geometry<\/strong>), moves with a velocity\n(<strong>calculus<\/strong>) and processes imprecise sensor data\n(<strong>probability and statistics<\/strong>).<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What belongs explicitly to which level?<\/h2>\n<p>Chapters <a href=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L1<\/a>–<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L17&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L17<\/a> form a <strong>teaching\noverview of upper secondary education<\/strong> and are not a substitute\nfor the current Abitur requirements. German schools distinguish between\nbasic and advanced requirement levels; the scope and weighting depend on\nthe applicable curriculum framework and examination year. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L10&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L10<\/a> explicitly lists\nadvanced-course (Leistungskurs) topics classified according to the\nBerlin examination priorities for 2026. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L14&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L14<\/a> is designated as a\n<strong>technical supplementary module, partly beyond the\nAbitur<\/strong>; it must not be presented as compulsory material\nthroughout.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Requirements and limits of the overview<\/h2>\n<p>This comparison is an <strong>orientation sheet<\/strong>, not a\ngeneral calculation procedure. The derivative\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f&#39;(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis only defined where the difference quotient has a finite limit. The\nantiderivative formula for a definite integral is applied here to a\nfunction that is continuous on the closed interval. For a discontinuous\nrate of change, the particular concept of integration and its\nrequirements must be examined separately.<\/p>\n<p>In geometry, calculating the angle from the scalar product requires\n<strong>two non-zero vectors<\/strong> in a suitably defined inner\nproduct space. Ordinary Euclidean length is not automatically the same\nas any arbitrary norm. In probability and statistics,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>∩<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mfrac><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(A\\mid B)=\\frac{P(A\\cap B)}{P(B)}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nholds only when\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(B)&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nin particular,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(A\\mid B)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis generally <strong>not<\/strong> equal to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(B\\mid A)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nObserved proportions are not universally valid probabilities without a\nsuitable sampling and probability model. Different software models can\nhave different assumptions despite using identical formula symbols.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is it useful for?<\/h2>\n<p>A mathematical toolkit emerges for software and AI: functions and\nderivatives explain error gradients and control curves, vectors and\nmatrices explain position and embedding relationships, and statistics\nexplains uncertainty in measurement and model results. The real skill\nlies in recognising the appropriate question in a technical problem and\nbeing able to read the formula together with its assumptions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S11\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Connection to university<\/h2>\n<p>School mathematics uses many familiar rules. University mathematics\nalso asks <strong>what the concepts mean precisely, under which\nconditions a statement holds and why it follows logically<\/strong>. That\nis why university mathematics <a\nhref=\"?course=university_mathematics&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L1<\/a>\nbegins with sets, statements, quantifiers and principles of proof;\nuniversity mathematics <a\nhref=\"?course=university_mathematics&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L2<\/a>\nthen shows how precisely the familiar idea of the limit can be\nexpressed.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S12\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<p>No individual sources are listed separately for this lesson.<\/p>\n<\/article>"};
